// Wonder Lab plant data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Written and fact-checked in review passes; do not hand-edit.
// 91 plants, 1060 facts.
const PLANTS = [{"id":"giant-sequoia","name":"Giant sequoia","group":"trees","blurb":"The bulkiest tree on Earth — not the tallest, but the one with the most wood packed into a single trunk.","size":"General Sherman is 275 ft (83.8 m) tall and 102.6 ft (31.3 m) around at the ground — that trunk would fill most of a tennis court.","homes":["forest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":279,"life":3000,"weight":4210000},"wonder":"Everything about it is built to outlast the fire that will certainly come: bark like a mattress, no pitch to catch, and cones that wait for the heat.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"General Sherman, in Sequoia National Park, California, is the largest single-stem tree measured anywhere: 275 ft (83.8 m) tall, 102.6 ft (31.3 m) around at the ground, 36.5 ft (11.1 m) across at the base, and 52,508 cubic feet (1,487 cubic metres) of trunk.","more":"A 1938 estimate put its wet mass at 2,105 short tons — about 4.2 million lb. Taller trees exist and wider trunks exist, but nothing measured holds more wood in one stem."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"General Sherman's largest single branch is 6.8 ft (2.1 m) thick. That one branch is fatter than the whole trunk of most oak trees you will ever stand next to."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"In January 2006 a branch over 6.6 ft (2 m) thick and more than 98 ft (30 m) long tore off General Sherman, smashing the perimeter fence and the pavement below. The tree was not sick. It just dropped a branch the size of a tree."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The bark can be 3 ft (0.9 m) thick at the base — spongy, fibrous, and carrying almost no pitch or resin. Punch it and your fist sinks in.","more":"Most conifer bark is thin and loaded with resin. Sequoia bark is neither: it is a mat of dry fibres, and a thick mat of dry fibres is a poor conductor of heat. The living layer underneath never gets hot enough to be hurt."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody designed a fire test for this. The bark is the argument: three feet of pithy, resin-free padding around the trunk, and the lowest live branches held far above a grass fire. Heat that kills a pine barely marks a sequoia.","more":"Most conifers are full of flammable resin. Sequoia bark is not. Fire scars carved into old trunks show the same tree burned repeatedly and kept growing around each wound."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seeds are 4–5 mm long — flakes of oatmeal with a papery wing. A big tree may carry 11,000 cones and let go of an estimated 300,000 to 400,000 seeds in a year. Each cone averages about 230 seeds."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Cones can hang shut on the branch for twenty years. Three things open them: a Douglas squirrel chewing the fleshy green scales, the grub of a longhorn beetle (Phymatodes nitidus) mining the cone until the scales dry and shrink, and hot air rising off a fire.","more":"The beetle grub is not after the seeds. It eats the fleshy scales and the cone shaft, and once it cuts the plumbing, the scales dry out and gape. The seeds simply fall."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The General Grant tree in Kings Canyon has the thickest measured trunk of any giant sequoia: 28.9 ft (8.8 m) across at breast height, 107.6 ft (32.8 m) around at the ground, and 267.4 ft (81.5 m) tall."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 28 April 1926 President Calvin Coolidge proclaimed the General Grant tree the Nation's Christmas Tree. On 29 March 1956 President Eisenhower declared it a National Shrine, a memorial to Americans killed in war — the only living thing ever given that status.","more":"Two presidents, thirty years apart, gave one tree two different official jobs. It was never planted, moved or shaped for either. It was already standing in Kings Canyon when the first proclamation was signed."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The naming was a mess. John Lindley called it Wellingtonia gigantea in December 1853, but that name was already taken and did not stand. Not until 1939 did John Buchholz show it was a different genus from the coast redwood and coin Sequoiadendron."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week brought seed-bearing plants and fruit trees, each yielding seed after its kind — before the sun was set in the sky on day four. The whole of this tree is folded into a winged flake 4 mm long."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"The parts fit each other. Cones that stay shut for twenty years are only useful if something reliably opens them; fire, a squirrel and a beetle each do. Bark that shrugs off fire is only useful if the seed needs fire. Take one piece out and the set stops working."}]},{"id":"coast-redwood","name":"Coast redwood","group":"trees","blurb":"The tallest tree on Earth, growing in a narrow fog belt along the California coast.","size":"Hyperion measured 381.3 ft (116.22 m) in 2026 — taller than the Statue of Liberty from her feet to the tip of her torch.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"height":381,"life":2000},"wonder":"A tree this tall has to lift water 380 feet with no pump at all, and it does it every day without noticing.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Chris Atkins and Michael Taylor found Hyperion in Redwood National Park on 25 August 2006. Stephen Sillett climbed it and dropped a tape from the top: 379.1 ft (115.55 m). It measured 380.8 ft (116.07 m) in 2019 and 381.3 ft (116.22 m) in 2026. It is still going up.","more":"A laser range finder from the ground gets you close. A tape dropped by a climber from the highest living twig gets you the number, and that is the number the record uses."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"In July 2022 the National Park Service closed the area around Hyperion. So many people had walked in to find it that the ferns at its base stopped growing. Going in now risks a fine of up to $5,000 and up to six months in jail."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Fog drip can supply about 30 percent of the water a coast redwood uses in a year. On heavy-fog nights the tree can run the flow backwards and take water in through its leaves instead of its roots.","more":"Fog is not rain and it does not fall on its own. It condenses on needles high in the crown and drips down, so the tree combs water out of the air and then waters its own roots with it. In a dry Californian summer that drip is the difference."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The bark grows up to 14 in (35 cm) thick and holds almost no flammable pitch. The wood is loaded with tannic acid and terpenoids, which is why insects and rot mostly leave it alone."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The cones are tiny for such a tree — 0.6 to 1.25 in (15–32 mm) — and hold 90 to 150 seeds each. A single seed is 3–4 mm long and half a millimetre wide. The tallest living thing on the planet starts smaller than a grain of rice."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Cut a redwood down and it sprouts back. New trunks come up from burls at the base and from the stump itself, which is why you find a perfect ring of tall redwoods standing around an empty middle. That ring is one tree, not a family.","more":"Foresters call them fairy rings. The gap in the centre is where the original trunk stood before it fell or was logged."},{"cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"George Koch's team sampled leaves at different heights and measured how much harder it gets to pull water up. Run the trend out and the ceiling lands somewhere around 400 to 427 ft (122–130 m). Hyperion is not far off it.","more":"Water is dragged up by evaporation at the leaves, pulling a thin thread of water all the way from the roots. The higher you go, the harder the pull, and the more the leaves have to shut down to avoid snapping the thread."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Climbers working in redwood crowns find soil sitting on the big branches, hundreds of feet up, with ferns and other plants rooted in it. There are gardens up there that never touch the ground.","more":"The soil up there is made of the tree's own shed needles and bark, caught in the crotches of the big limbs and slowly rotting. Once there is soil there is somewhere to root, and plants that belong on the forest floor end up living in the sky."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Tallest is not biggest. Hyperion holds about 18,600 cubic feet (530 cubic metres) of wood. General Sherman, a giant sequoia and a shorter tree, holds 52,508 cubic feet. Height and bulk are two different competitions."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The roots are shallow and spread wide rather than driving deep — no taproot under a 380-foot tree. The best read is that redwoods hold each other up: roots of neighbouring trees tangle together, so the grove braces the individual."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts plants on day three and the sun on day four. A coast redwood does its whole job on light: it takes fog, water and sunlight and turns them into 380 feet of standing timber, and the light it runs on arrived one day after it did."}]},{"id":"bristlecone-pine","name":"Bristlecone pine","group":"trees","blurb":"A short, twisted, half-dead-looking pine that grows on bare mountain rock where almost nothing else will.","size":"Only 16 to 49 ft (5 to 15 m) tall — smaller than many garden trees — but with a trunk up to 12 ft (3.6 m) thick.","homes":["mountain"],"stats":{"height":49},"wonder":"It gives up almost everything — height, speed, green wood, most of its own trunk — to keep one narrow strip of bark alive on a mountain that kills everything else.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"In 1957 Edmund Schulman bored a pencil-thin core out of a bristlecone in California's White Mountains and counted the rings under a microscope. The count came to 4,789. He named the tree Methuselah, after the man in Genesis.","more":"A core is a straw of wood pulled sideways out of the trunk with a hollow drill called an increment borer. The tree seals the hole. Rings are counted, not guessed — but a core that misses the exact centre undercounts, and a year with no growth leaves no ring at all."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"In August 1964 Donald Currey was coring bristlecones on Wheeler Peak, Nevada. His borer jammed in a gnarled tree he had labelled WPN-114, and with Forest Service permission the tree was cut down. The stump held at least 4,862 countable rings — more than anyone had counted in a tree before.","more":"The tree was afterwards named Prometheus. The cutting of it changed the rules: bristlecones on federal land are now protected, and the exact tree Schulman cored is not marked or published."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Needles stay green on the branch for as long as 45 years. Most pines swap theirs out every two to five. One flush of needles here does the work for decades before it is replaced."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"On the oldest trees most of the trunk is bare, polished dead wood, and only a narrow ribbon of living bark runs up one side to a few live branches. The tree is mostly a sculpture with a working strip attached.","more":"Damage kills the bark on the exposed side and the tree does not replace it. It abandons that side, drops the branches that side was feeding, and pushes everything through the ribbon that is left. Losing most of yourself is survivable if you keep the plumbing."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows highest and best on pale dolomite rock, in stands so thin that the canopy covers only 15 to 50 percent of the ground. Neighbours are the problem it has solved by going where there are none."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Myth-buster: bristlecones are not fireproof. The bark is resinous and catches quickly, and the trees are extremely vulnerable to fire. They survive because there is barely any grass or brush around them to carry a flame.","more":"That is worth getting straight. The tree is not tough against fire; it is standing somewhere fire struggles to reach. Where stands are denser and there is fuel between the trees, bristlecones burn like anything else."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The bristle is real. Each cone scale carries a spine 2 to 5 mm long, so a closed cone feels like a small burr. The cones are 2 to 4 in (5–10 cm) long and turn orange-buff after sixteen months."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Dead bristlecone wood lies on those slopes without rotting away. The best read is a combination: the wood is dense and resinous, the air is thin and dry, and it is cold. Rot needs damp and warmth, and gets neither."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The growth is so slow that rings can be too fine to separate with the naked eye. Counters work under magnification, and a bad run of years can produce rings you would need a good lamp and patience to find."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seeds are 5 mm long with a wing 12 to 22 mm across — a papery propeller. That is the whole travel plan for a tree that lives on a windswept ridge."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three put plants on the ground before there was an animal anywhere to eat them or a person to plant them. This one holds a bare dolomite slope at altitude, alone, on almost no soil. Somebody had to want that slope covered."}]},{"id":"baobab","name":"Baobab","group":"trees","blurb":"An African tree built like a water tank, with a trunk you could park a car in and branches that look like roots waving in the air.","size":"16 to 82 ft (5 to 25 m) tall, but the trunk is the point: 33 to 46 ft (10 to 14 m) across, wider than a school classroom.","homes":["grassland","desert"],"stats":{"height":82,"life":1000},"wonder":"It solves the dry season the way a camel does — carry the water with you — except the tank is the tree itself.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A large baobab can hold as much as 136,400 litres of water in its trunk — roughly 36,000 US gallons, or about the contents of two backyard swimming pools, stored inside the wood.","more":"The wood itself is the tank. Baobab wood is soft and spongy and full of water rather than dense and dry, which is why elephants chew into the trunks in a drought, and why a baobab is close to useless as timber."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"You can watch the tank empty. Through the dry season the trunk shrinks about 2–3 cm around, and the water content of the stem falls by roughly a tenth. When the rain comes back, it swells out again."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The Sunland Baobab in South Africa was 72 ft (22 m) tall, 47 m (154 ft) around, and 34.9 ft (10.64 m) across the trunk. In 1993 the owners cleared out a natural hollow inside and put a bar in it. Sixty people once fit in there for a party.","more":"A second hollow held a wine cellar that sat at a steady 22 °C without any cooling. In August 2016 about a third of the tree collapsed, and a second break in 2017 finished the bar."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flowers open in the late afternoon, last one single night, and fall. Each one is a white hanging bowl with petals up to 3 in (8 cm) long and between 720 and 1,600 stamens — with counts up to 2,000 reported."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"A flower that opens at dusk is not looking for bees. Fruit bats do the pollinating in east and west Africa — straw-coloured, Gambian epauletted and Egyptian fruit bats — with bushbabies and insects also visiting. In southern Africa hawkmoths appear to do most of it.","more":"Bat-pollinated flowers tend to be pale, large, strong-smelling and open at night, and this one ticks every box. A bat hovering at a hanging white bowl holding over a thousand stamens comes away thoroughly dusted."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The bark is up to 8 cm (3 in) deep and the fruit hangs on long stalks, up to 10 in (25 cm) long with a hard shell 6–10 mm thick — a wooden case you have to break to get in."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Why store water instead of digging for it? The best read is timing. Rain here arrives in a short burst and then stops for months. A tree that can take the whole burst in at once and spend it slowly does not have to find water in the dry season at all."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The Avenue of the Baobabs runs along a stretch of dirt Road No. 8 between Morondava and Belon'i Tsiribihina in western Madagascar. Twenty to twenty-five trees stand along about 850 ft (260 m) of it — and they are a different species, Adansonia grandidieri.","more":"Madagascar has six baobab species found nowhere else on Earth. The avenue trees are the tall, straight, bare-trunked kind, and they now stand in farmland. The forest that used to be around them is gone; they are what is left of it."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"record","text":"People across Africa call it the upside-down tree, and the story goes that it was pulled up and stuck back in the wrong way round. Bare in the dry season, the crown really does look like a root system waving about in the air."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Its scientific name, Adansonia digitata, means fingered. A mature leaf splits into five to seven leaflets — sometimes nine — spreading from one point like a hand."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis has plants made on day three and flying creatures on day five. This tree's flowers open at dusk, hold for one night, and are worked by bats. The flower was standing ready two days before anything with wings turned up to use it."}]},{"id":"oak","name":"English oak","group":"trees","blurb":"A broad, heavy, slow European oak that supports more other living things than any other tree in Britain.","size":"Usually up to 65 ft (20 m) and sometimes 130 ft (40 m), with a trunk 13 ft (4 m) around — and old ones over 33 ft (10 m).","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":130,"life":500},"wonder":"Almost nothing else in Britain feeds and houses 2,300 different kinds of creature at once, and the oak does it without moving.","facts":[{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"A 2019 count put 2,300 species — insects, mosses, lichens, birds, mammals and more — living on or with English oak in the UK. At least 400 of those are insects that eat its leaves, the highest count for any British plant.","more":"Not all 2,300 are on one tree at once, and most are small — mites, gall wasps, moth caterpillars, lichens, mosses. But an oak wood and a conifer plantation of the same size are not remotely the same place to be an insect."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The Major Oak stands near Edwinstowe in Sherwood Forest, Nottinghamshire. Its trunk is 33 ft (10 m) around, its canopy spreads 92 ft (28 m), and it is estimated to weigh about 23 tons."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The big acorn crops come roughly every other year rather than every year. A mast year buries the ground in acorns; the year between produces comparatively few."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"The best read on why it works: in a mast year, jays, squirrels, mice and pigs cannot possibly eat them all, so some survive. In the lean years the acorn-eaters go hungry and their numbers drop. The tree is not feeding them; it is out-producing them."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Oaks have no wings and no wind trick. Jays carry acorns off and bury them one at a time, often hundreds of yards away, and never dig up every store. Nobody watched a particular acorn become a particular tree — but that is how an oak wood walks uphill."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Acorns and bark are loaded with tannin, which is why a raw acorn tastes vile and dries your mouth out. People have used oak bark tannin to turn animal hide into leather since Roman times.","more":"Tannin grabs hold of protein. That is what makes a raw acorn taste dry and awful, and it is the same reaction that turns a raw hide into leather: the tannin locks onto the protein in the skin so the skin stops rotting."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Oak heartwood runs to about 720 kg per cubic metre — around 45 lb per cubic foot — and lasts. It was the main structural material for sailing warships, and it still goes into floors, furniture and wine barrels."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Gall wasps lay eggs in oak buds and the tree grows a hard round house around the grub. People collected those oak galls, crushed them with iron salts and made ink out of them — most old European manuscripts are written in oak-gall ink.","more":"The wasp does not build the gall. It lays an egg, and the oak does the building — growing a hard round chamber around the grub with food inside it. Crush those galls with iron salts and you get a black ink that bites into paper and stays there."},{"cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"Very old oaks get shorter. The crown dies back from the top and the tree carries on below the dieback. The reasoning is plain plumbing: a smaller crown is less to supply, and a tree that shrinks its demand can hold on far longer than one that does not."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"An oak needs up to 150 years of growing before it is big enough to cut for construction timber. The person who plants one is not the person who uses it."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three: fruit trees yielding fruit, whose seed is in itself, after its kind. An acorn is exactly that — a seed with a wooden cup and a lid, and it makes an oak, every time, and nothing else."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"English oaks routinely pass 500 years, and several claimed specimens — the Stelmuze Oak in Lithuania, the Granit Oak in Bulgaria — are reckoned to be far older than that."}]},{"id":"banyan","name":"Banyan","group":"trees","blurb":"A fig that starts life up in another tree's branches, drops roots to the ground, and ends up standing in its place.","size":"Up to 100 ft (30 m) tall — but width is the point: one banyan's canopy covers 4.67 acres, about three and a half football pitches.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"height":100},"wonder":"There is no rule that says a tree must be one trunk; the banyan simply keeps adding legs and walking outwards.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Great Banyan, at the Acharya Jagadish Chandra Bose Indian Botanic Garden in Howrah near Kolkata, covers 18,918 square metres — 203,630 sq ft, about 4.67 acres. Its crown measures 486 m (1,594 ft) around and its highest branch reaches 80 ft (24.5 m).","more":"There is a road running through it and a footpath around the outside. Visitors regularly walk into the middle believing they are in a small wood, and come out surprised."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"That one tree stands on 3,772 prop roots that have reached the ground and thickened into trunks. Walk into it and it reads as a small forest. It is one plant."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The Great Banyan's original main trunk was 50 ft across. Two cyclones around 1925 wrecked it and fungus got in, so it was cut out and removed to save the rest. The tree lost its middle and did not notice.","more":"That is only possible because every prop root is a working trunk in its own right. Remove the centre of an oak and you have a dead oak."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Thimmamma Marrimanu, in Anantapur district, Andhra Pradesh, spreads further still: 19,107 square metres (205,670 sq ft), with a perimeter of 846 m (2,776 ft). Guinness listed it as the largest tree by canopy in 1989."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A new prop root starts as a thin brown thread hanging from a branch, swinging in the air with nothing on the end of it. Once it touches soil it roots, thickens, and starts carrying weight."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"A strangler fig sprouts in a fork high in another tree and grows there as a lodger, sending roots down to the soil. Nobody watches one tree for a century — the hollow centre is the argument. Old stranglers are hollow, because the host inside them rotted away.","more":"Cut into a big strangler and there is often a chimney up the middle where the host trunk used to be. The fig grew as a hollow cage around a tree that has since rotted out of it, leaving its shape behind."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Birds eat the small figs and drop the seeds wherever they perch — including high forks in other trees, which is exactly where a strangler needs to start. Seeds that have been through a bird's gut sprout better than seeds that have not."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Every wild fig, banyan included, is pollinated by tiny wasps that crawl in through a pinhole at the tip of the fig. Different fig species carry different wasps. The fig is a sealed room, and the wasp is the only one with a key."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"In Meghalaya, in north-east India, people take the aerial roots of a related fig — Ficus elastica — and train them across rivers on frames. The roots thicken in place and knit together, and the finished bridge is alive and carries people over.","more":"A root bridge takes many years to grow and is never really finished — people keep training new roots into it. Unlike a wooden bridge it gets stronger with age, because it is still alive and still thickening."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The banyan is the national tree of India. It is treated as sacred there, and temples are often built beside one."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three gave trees that yield seed after their kind. This one yields figs the size of a marble, and out of one of them comes something that can end up covering four and a half acres. The kind holds; the scale is a surprise every time."}]},{"id":"mangrove","name":"Red mangrove","group":"trees","blurb":"A tree that stands in salt water on stilts and sends its babies out already grown.","size":"Commonly about 20 ft (6 m), reaching 80 ft (24 m) where conditions suit — standing clear of the water on arching stilt roots.","homes":["ocean","forest"],"stats":{"height":80},"wonder":"It was standing in the shallows, building a nursery out of its own legs, before there was a single fish to use it.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The prop roots arch out of the trunk and branches and stab down into the mud, holding the tree above the water on a tangle of stilts. From below at low tide it looks like the tree is standing on its hands."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Waterlogged mud has almost no oxygen in it. The best read of the aerial roots is that they solve breathing as well as standing: the parts above water take oxygen in directly, so the roots buried in airless mud do not suffocate."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Red mangroves skip the seed stage entirely. The seed germinates while still attached to the parent and grows into a finished green seedling 8 to 10 in (20–25 cm) long — a living dart — before it drops off.","more":"This is called vivipary, and it is unusual in plants. There is no dormant seed to survive being soaked in salt water, because there is no dormant seed."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"A dropped propagule can float in brackish water for over a year before it finds somewhere to root. In one Panama experiment red mangrove propagules floated for more than 100 days; related Rhizophora propagules from Micronesia floated for three to five months.","more":"A floating propagule lies flat while it is travelling and tips upright once it begins to root, heavy end down. If it grounds on mud at the right depth, it plants itself with nobody's help."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Other mangroves take the salt in and sweat it back out through their leaves. Red mangroves block most of it at the roots instead — membranes in the root let water through and hold the salt back, so the tree drinks the sea and stays fresh inside."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Mangroves flatten waves. Measurements put the drop in wave height at 13 to 66 percent across 100 m of forest, and 50 to 99 percent across 500 m. A belt of trees does the job of a sea wall.","more":"That number has a cost attached. Where mangrove belts have been cleared for shrimp ponds or building, storm damage behind them goes up. A replanted mangrove fringe is cheaper than a sea wall, and it repairs itself."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why the roots and not the trunks? The tangle is the brake. Thousands of stilt roots put drag on moving water, so the energy goes into churning through them instead of into the shore. It works best in shallow water and falls away as the water gets deeper."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"The root thickets are a nursery. Young fish shelter in there where big fish cannot follow, and birds nest above and hunt below. In parts of the range, crocodiles use them too.","more":"The value of the tangle is that it is a bad place to be big. A young fish slips between the roots; a predator that has grown past a certain size simply cannot follow it in."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The leaves are thick and waxy, 3 to 5 in (7.6–12.7 cm) long and 1 to 2 in (2.5–5.1 cm) wide — built to hold water in, which is odd for a tree standing in an ocean."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts plants on day three and the swarming creatures of the sea on day five. The mangrove nursery — stilt roots, sheltered shallows, safe water — was finished and standing two days before the first fish arrived to live in it."}]},{"id":"aspen","name":"Quaking aspen","group":"trees","blurb":"A white-barked tree whose leaves shake in the faintest breeze, and which is often not one tree at all.","size":"Usually 50 to 60 ft (15–18 m), with a record of 120 ft (36.5 m) — but the biggest one covers 106 acres.","homes":["forest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":120},"wonder":"A hillside of aspen turning gold on the same day is not a coincidence and not a forest; it is one plant showing you its edges.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The leaf stalk is flattened sideways, 1.2 to 2.8 in (3–7 cm) long, instead of round like most trees'. A flat stalk has no stiffness against twisting, so the leaf spins and flips in air you can barely feel. That is the quaking."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has watched a leaf choose to flutter. The shape is the argument: leaves that twist keep letting light past them, so the leaves lower down — and the trunk — get a share instead of sitting in permanent shade."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Scratch the white off an aspen trunk and it is green underneath. The bark is thin enough for light to reach chlorophyll in the layer below, so the trunk itself makes sugar — including in winter, with no leaves on the tree at all.","more":"Rub a finger on an aspen trunk and white powder comes off, with a distinctly green layer under it. A tree that can photosynthesise through its own trunk gets a head start every spring, before a single leaf has opened."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Pando, in the Fishlake National Forest, Utah, is about 47,000 aspen stems spread over 106 acres (42.89 hectares). Every stem shares one root system and one set of DNA. Together they weigh roughly 13.2 million lb (6,000 tonnes) — the heaviest known single organism.","more":"From a road it reads as an ordinary aspen wood. The giveaway is that every stem leafs out, turns colour and drops its leaves in step, because they are all the same plant."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Jerry Kemperman and Burton Barnes worked out in 1976 that the stand was a single organism. Michael Grant gave it the name Pando, which is Latin for I spread."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Aspen seeds are minute — about three million to the pound — packed in cotton fluff, with no food store and no hard coat. They stay alive only briefly after they land.","more":"Compare that with an acorn, which is a packed lunch with a lid on it. An aspen seed carries no lunch at all — it has to land somewhere bare and damp within days or it is finished. In most years almost none of them manage it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Which is why aspen mostly does not bother with seeds. New stems push up from buds on the roots of the plant already there. That is not a seedling; it is the same organism putting up another pole."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"Mule deer and elk eat the young aspen shoots faster than they can grow away, and Pando has been thinning because of it. About 84 acres are now inside 8-ft wildlife fencing to give the new stems a chance.","more":"A clone can lose ground for decades without looking like it. The old trunks are still standing, so nothing seems wrong. The problem shows up as an absence: no young stems coming through underneath to replace them."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Quaking aspen is the most widely spread tree in North America, running from Canada down to central Mexico."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"In autumn you can read the clones off a mountainside. One patch goes gold while the patch beside it is still green, and the line between them is sharp. The reasoning: same timing means same plant, and the boundary is where one root system stops."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three named plants that yield seed after their kind. Aspen has the seed — three million to the pound — and also a second way home, sending up new stems from the root it already has. Two methods, one kind."}]},{"id":"dragon-blood-tree","name":"Dragon's blood tree","group":"trees","blurb":"A tree shaped like an open umbrella that runs dark red when you cut it, and grows wild on one island group only.","size":"Up to 30 ft (9 m) tall with a crown 39 ft (12 m) across — wider than it is high, like a mushroom made of branches.","homes":["mountain","desert"],"stats":{"height":30},"wonder":"The crown is not decoration: everything the tree does about heat, water and its own seedlings, it does with that one umbrella shape.","facts":[{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows wild in one place on Earth: the Socotra archipelago, part of Yemen, out in the Arabian Sea. Dry mountain tops there are covered in them and nowhere else is."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The umbrella is built by repeated forking. Each branch splits into two, then each of those two splits into two, and so on — usually triggered when a growing tip stops, after flowering or damage. Count the forks and you have read the tree's history.","more":"Because a fork is triggered by a growing tip stopping, every fork marks an event — a flowering, a snapped branch. The crown is a record of things that happened to the tree, written in its own branching."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Cut the bark and dark red resin runs out. It dries to a crimson lump. The stuff is called dragon's blood, and it is where the tree gets its name."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Dragon's blood was traded around the Mediterranean in classical antiquity as a medicine and a red pigment, used on wounds and for fevers. In eighteenth-century Europe it went into violin varnish and was used in tempering steel.","more":"Traders knew the resin long before anyone knew where it came from, and stories grew up to fill the gap — that it was the blood of a dragon, or of an elephant. The real source was a slow tree on a hard-to-reach island."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Why an umbrella on a dry mountain? The best read is shade and drip. The dense crown cuts evaporation from the ground directly beneath it, and that patch of shaded soil is where seedlings actually manage to survive — right under the parent.","more":"You can see the argument on the ground. Out in the open, seedlings dry out or get eaten. Under the crown of an adult, in shade, they hang on. So the trees end up in clusters rather than spread out evenly."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It is not a tree in the oak sense at all. It is a monocot — the same broad group as lilies, grasses and asparagus — and it grows thicker in a way that leaves no clean annual rings to count."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The trunk gets to about 5 ft (1.5 m) across and then splits. Below the first fork it is a bare grey column; above it, nothing but forks."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Goats eat the seedlings, the old trees are not being replaced fast enough, and one model of the changing climate works out a loss of about 45 percent of suitable habitat by 2080. That last figure is a projection, not a measurement."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"People on Socotra have been raising them by hand. By 2022 around 600 nursery-grown saplings had got big enough to stop needing regular watering."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"From below, the leaves make a solid roof — stiff blue-green blades all crowded at the very ends of the branches, with nothing green anywhere else on the tree."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three set plants on the land before there was rain-bearing weather anybody had recorded, and before an animal or a person existed to shelter under one. This tree's whole shape is a roof — and it built it for its own seedlings."}]},{"id":"joshua-tree","name":"Joshua tree","group":"trees","blurb":"Not really a tree and not a cactus — a giant yucca of the Mojave Desert with exactly one insect willing to pollinate it.","size":"Most top out near 40 ft (12 m); the tallest known reach about 49 ft (15 m) — roughly a four-storey building of spikes.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"height":49,"life":150},"wonder":"One plant, one moth, and neither can make the next generation without the other showing up on time.","facts":[{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"The yucca moth Tegeticula synthetica carries pollen from flower to flower while laying its eggs inside them. Its grubs eat some of the seeds and leave the rest. No other animal visiting the flowers does the job — none of them move the pollen.","more":"A second moth, Tegeticula antithetica, does the same work in the eastern and north-eastern Mojave. Take the moths away and the Joshua tree sets no seed; take the trees away and the moths have nothing to feed their grubs."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The trunk is thousands of small fibres bundled together, with no annual growth rings anywhere in it. You cannot count a Joshua tree's rings, because it does not have any."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It will only flower if two things happen: rain at the right moment, and a proper winter freeze. Flowers come between February and late April, and some years a stand simply does not bloom at all.","more":"That is a strict pair of conditions for a desert plant. Flowering years come in patches and cannot be predicted, and the moths have to be there in the right year as well, or the whole effort produces nothing at all."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Seedlings grow about 3 in (7.6 cm) a year for the first ten years, then slow to roughly 1.5 in (3.8 cm) a year. A 40-ft Joshua tree has been at it for a very long time at that rate."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The roots spread far wider than the plant. In one case a Joshua tree root was traced 36 ft (11 m) from the nearest trunk — out past the edge of everything you can see."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It lives almost entirely in the Mojave Desert, between 1,300 and 5,900 ft (400–1,800 m) above sea level. Below that band it is too hot; above it, too cold."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Scott's oriole builds its nest right in among the spikes. Wood rats build houses under the heaps of fallen leaves. Desert night lizards hunt insects under the log of a Joshua tree that has come down."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Myth-buster: everyone says Mormon settlers named it for the prophet Joshua with his arms raised. The National Park Service notes there is little evidence for the story — the name is not recorded until after Mormon contact, and no one wrote it down at the time.","more":"It is a good story and it may well be true. It is just not documented. Worth keeping straight: a thing everybody says and a thing somebody wrote down at the time are two different grades of evidence."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Native people of the Mojave used most of the plant: leaf fibres bound and made sandals, root sheaths were woven into baskets, the fruit was baked or boiled, and the seeds were ground into flour."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It does not only use seed. Joshua trees sprout from their own roots and branches, so a tight cluster of trunks can turn out to be one plant that came up several times."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Genesis has plants on day three and every creeping thing on day six. The tree and the moth need each other completely — no moth, no seed; no tree, no grubs. Whatever you make of the order, a partnership that tight does not arrive one piece at a time and wait."}]},{"id":"cacao","name":"Cacao","group":"trees","blurb":"A small rainforest tree that grows its fruit straight out of its trunk, and is the only source of chocolate.","size":"20 to 39 ft (6–12 m) tall, with rugby-ball pods 6 to 12 in (15–30 cm) long hanging off the bark of the trunk itself.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"height":39},"wonder":"The whole of chocolate depends on a fly small enough to get through a flower less than an inch across.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Cacao flowers and fruits straight out of the trunk and the old thick branches, not out on the twigs. The word for it is cauliflory. A cacao tree looks like someone glued the fruit on by hand."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"The flowers are only 0.4 to 0.8 in (1–2 cm) across, and biting midges of the genus Forcipomyia do the pollinating — flies a couple of millimetres long. Only about 1 to 5 percent of flowers ever turn into a pod.","more":"A one-to-five-percent success rate sounds like a disaster. It is not, because a tree carries thousands of flowers. It just means most cacao flowers open, wait, and drop off without ever meeting a midge."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A ripe pod weighs about 1 lb (500 g) and holds 20 to 60 seeds packed in sweet white pulp. The pulp is delicious. The seeds, raw, are horribly bitter."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"It takes about 1,200 seeds — roughly 40 pods — to make 1 kg (2.2 lb) of cocoa paste. A chocolate bar is the emptied contents of several fruits off a tree trunk."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"There is no chocolate without rotting. The seeds and pulp are heaped up and left to ferment for several days, then dried for a week or two. Skip the fermenting and the flavour never appears at all.","more":"The heap heats up as it ferments and the pulp breaks down and drains away. Inside the seed, that heat and acid start the reactions that build the flavour. A washed, dried, unfermented cacao seed tastes bitter and flat, and always will."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Cocoa butter is 40 to 50 percent of the seed and melts at 93.4 °F (34.1 °C) — just under body temperature. That is exactly why chocolate is hard in the wrapper and liquid in your mouth."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Linnaeus gave it the name Theobroma cacao in 1753. Theobroma is Greek for food of the gods. Cacao comes through Spanish from Nahuatl, the language of the people who were drinking it long before Europeans arrived.","more":"Cacao was a drink long before it was a bar — ground seeds, water, chilli, no sugar at all. Sweet solid chocolate came much later, and most of what is in a bar is not cacao."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The seeds carry theobromine. People break it down without trouble; dogs process it far more slowly, which is why chocolate makes a dog seriously ill and a person merely happy."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Its home ground runs from south-eastern Mexico down into the Amazon basin, in humid forest. It wants shade, damp and no wind — a young cacao grows under other trees, not in the open."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The leaves reach 20 in (50 cm) long and 4 in (10 cm) across. New ones come out limp and reddish and hang straight down before they stiffen up and go green."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three: fruit trees yielding fruit, whose seed is in itself. Cacao is that sentence taken literally — the fruit hangs on the trunk and the seed inside it is the thing worth having."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Flowers on a trunk are hard to explain by wind or by birds. The best read is the midge: a fly that lives in wet leaf litter on the forest floor does not have to climb far to reach a flower growing at chest height on the bark."}]},{"id":"rubber-tree","name":"Rubber tree","group":"trees","blurb":"An Amazon tree whose milky sap, cut from the bark daily, became tyres, gloves and erasers.","size":"Up to 141 ft (43 m) in wild forest; plantation trees are kept far shorter so the tappers can reach the cut.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"height":141},"wonder":"One tree in one forest turned out to have a liquid in its bark that the whole modern world runs on wheels because of.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The latex is not in the wood. It sits in its own vessels inside the bark, spiralling up the trunk in a right-handed helix at about 30 degrees to the horizontal, and reaching as high as 49 ft (15 m).","more":"There is no equivalent in a familiar tree. It is a second, separate network living in the bark, carrying its own fluid, and it has nothing to do with moving water up or sugar down."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Which explains the slanted cut. A tapper's groove runs diagonally down the trunk, and the reason is arithmetic: a diagonal crosses far more of those spiralling vessels than a straight vertical or horizontal cut would."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The cut is shaved shallow — just deep enough to open the latex vessels without reaching the growing layer underneath. The latex runs down the groove into a small cup, and the tree seals the wound afterwards."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Henry Wickham delivered 70,000 rubber seeds collected in Brazil to Joseph Hooker at Kew Gardens in the early hours of 14 June 1876. About four percent sprouted. Roughly 2,000 seedlings went to Ceylon and 22 to Singapore.","more":"By 1898 there was a rubber plantation in Malaya. Brazil's rubber exports had halved by 1910 and were close to nothing by 1940. Seventy thousand seeds in a crate moved an entire industry to the other side of the world."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The fruit is a capsule holding three big seeds, and when it ripens it splits open explosively and throws them clear. In a quiet plantation you can hear them going off."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"A rubber tree takes seven to ten years before it gives its first harvest, and plantations usually fell them at 25 to 30 years, when the flow drops off. Every tyre involves a decade of waiting first."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"What is latex for, from the tree's side? The best read is defence. It floods out of a wound under pressure, gums up the mouthparts of anything chewing the bark, and sets into a sticky plug. It is not food, and it is not sap."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Raw rubber goes soft and tacky in heat and brittle in cold. Charles Goodyear worked at that through the 1830s, and in 1839 dropped a rubber and sulphur mix on a hot stove. Instead of melting, it firmed up. He patented the process in the US on 30 January 1844.","more":"Thomas Hancock had patented the same idea in Britain eight weeks earlier, on 21 November 1843. Before it, rubber was a novelty that failed in use. Heating it with sulphur locks the long molecules to one another, and the result stays springy across a useful range of temperatures. Every tyre rests on that step."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Its home is the Amazon, in forest that floods for part of the year. Thailand, Indonesia, Malaysia, India and Vietnam are now the biggest producers — none of them anywhere near where the tree grows wild."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Latex production varies wildly from tree to tree, even between trees of the same planted clone standing side by side. Growers cannot simply assume a yield; they have to tap and see."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, before there was anyone to want a tyre. The tree carries its own plumbing, its own repair fluid and its own launcher for its seeds, and it did all three long before anybody thought to put a cup under it."}]},{"id":"eucalyptus","name":"Rainbow eucalyptus","group":"trees","blurb":"A rainforest eucalyptus whose bark peels off in strips, leaving a trunk streaked green, blue, orange and purple.","size":"197 to 256 ft (60–78 m) tall — as high as a 20-storey building — with a trunk up to 94 in (240 cm) across.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"height":256},"wonder":"The colours are not paint and not a disease; they are simply the trunk's calendar, showing when each strip of bark let go.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The bark comes off in long strips, and different patches shed at different times of year. What is exposed underneath is bright green. It then ages through blue, purple, orange and reddish brown before the next strip peels.","more":"Plenty of eucalypts shed bark. This one does it in patches at different times, so the trunk is never one colour. On a single tree you can find fresh green, older blue and month-old orange side by side."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"So the streaks are a timetable. A green band is bark that was uncovered recently; a red-brown band has been out in the air longest. The trunk shows you months of its own history at once."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The best read on the colours: the fresh bark is green because it is full of chlorophyll, and as it sits in the air, tannins build up in it. Different tannins run red, yellow, brown, blue, purple and pink — and there is the rest of the range."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Its scientific name is Eucalyptus deglupta. Deglupta is Latin for peeled off, husked or shelled. Whoever named it went straight for the obvious thing."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows wild in the Philippines, Indonesia and Papua New Guinea, and it is the only eucalyptus that naturally lives in rainforest. It is also the only one whose natural range reaches into the Northern Hemisphere.","more":"Australia is the eucalyptus homeland and it is dry. This species went the other way, into wet tropical forest and north across the equator. It is the exception that shows how far the group will stretch."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Almost all of its several hundred relatives are Australian. This one is the outlier — a eucalyptus that went north across the equator and into the wet forest."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"It is planted around the world in plantations, mainly for pulpwood to make white paper. In the Philippines it is the dominant pulpwood species. A lot of ordinary paper started out as this tree.","more":"It is grown for speed, not for looks. A pulpwood plantation wants height and volume fast, and the extraordinary trunk is simply what the tree does while it is producing them."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"It grows fast, which is exactly why it is a plantation tree. Height and colour are unrelated; the growers want the height and get the paintwork thrown in."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"People assume it is fungus, dye or a hoax. The check is easy: it is on every tree in the stand, it is under the bark rather than on it, and the pattern moves as the bark sheds. Paint does not do that."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts plants on day three, before there was an eye anywhere to look at one. This trunk keeps repainting itself in green, blue, orange and purple, and it did it for a long time with nobody watching."}]},{"id":"date-palm","name":"Date palm","group":"trees","blurb":"The desert palm behind dates, feeding people in dry country as far back as written records go.","size":"Up to 98 ft (30 m) tall, with leaves 13 to 20 ft (4–6 m) long — one leaf is longer than a car.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"height":98,"life":100},"wonder":"It is one of the few crops in the world where the harvest depends on a person climbing up and doing the pollinating by hand.","facts":[{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A date palm is either male or female, never both. Only about half of palms grown from seed turn out female and bear fruit, so a grower who plants seeds is throwing away half the orchard."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"record","text":"So growers do the pollinating themselves, and have for a very long time: cut the male flower cluster, carry it up the female palm, and dust or tie it among her flowers. One male palm supplies pollen for up to 100 females.","more":"It is one of very few crops that essentially cannot be left to wind or insects if you want a real harvest. Growers climb, cut, carry and tie — the same work in the same order, in every date-growing country there is."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A mature female palm gives 150 to 300 lb (70–140 kg) of dates in a harvest. Dried dates are 63 to 64 percent sugar by mass — which is why they keep, and why a handful goes a long way in a desert."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"In 2005 Elaine Solowey managed to germinate a date seed recovered from the excavation of Masada, the clifftop fortress above the Dead Sea. Sarah Sallon named the seedling Methuselah. Six more old seeds have since been sprouted: Hannah, Adam, Judith, Boaz, Jonah and Uriel.","more":"The seeds had been sitting in storage after being dug out of the ruins. Nobody expected any of them to do anything. One of them grew into a male palm that has since fathered fruit."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Bible calls Jericho the city of palm trees. Palm branches are what the crowd carried when Jesus rode into Jerusalem, which is why the day is called Palm Sunday."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Each leaf carries about 150 leaflets, and each leaflet is around 12 in (30 cm) long and 1 in (2.5 cm) wide, ending in a spike. The lowest leaflets on a frond are stiff thorns."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Growers get around the seed problem with offshoots — young suckers cut from the base of a good female palm and planted. They come out identical to the parent and fruit two to three years sooner than a seedling."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"A palm is not built like an oak. There are no branches, no bark rings and no widening trunk in the oak sense — just one growing point at the top and a column patterned with the stumps of old leaf bases below it. Cut the top out and the palm is finished.","more":"An oak damaged at the top regrows from buds along its branches. A date palm has one growing point, at the very crown. That is why a palm is easy to kill from above and cannot be pruned back the way a broadleaf can."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A single date is 1 to 3 in (3–7 cm) long and about 1 in (2.5 cm) across, and hangs in enormous drooping clusters. A female palm may carry 15 to 25 flowering stalks in a season, each with 150 to 200 spikelets on it."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Palms are monocots, the same broad group as grasses and lilies. They lay down no annual rings, so nobody counts a palm's rings — there is nothing there to count."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three put the seed-bearing plants in place, and the account has fruit trees named specifically. A date is exactly that — a sweet parcel wrapped around one long hard seed, made to be carried off and dropped somewhere else."}]},{"id":"cedar-of-lebanon","name":"Cedar of Lebanon","group":"trees","blurb":"The great timber tree of the ancient Near East, and the tree on Lebanon's flag.","size":"Up to 130 ft (40 m) tall on a single massive column of a trunk 8 ft (2.5 m) thick — old ones measure 39 ft (12 m) around.","homes":["mountain","forest"],"stats":{"height":130,"life":1000},"wonder":"Of all the trees on all those mountains, this is the one the ancient world sent ships and armies for.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"1 Kings 5 records the deal: Hiram, king of Tyre, supplied Solomon with cedar and workmen for the temple in Jerusalem and for his own palace, and Solomon paid in wheat and oil. 2 Chronicles 2 tells it from the other side.","more":"The account is specific about the logistics. The timber was cut in Lebanon, floated down the coast in rafts and then hauled inland. Getting the wood was a national-scale project involving two kingdoms and a written agreement."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The wood is fragrant, and it resists both rot and insects. That combination made it the most valuable building timber in the ancient world — good for temples, palaces and ships, in a region where most timber does not last."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The cedar sits in the middle of Lebanon's flag, adopted on 7 December 1943, just before independence, and on the country's coat of arms. It is one of the very few national flags with a specific tree species on it."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"The Cedars of God — Horsh Arz el-Rab — grow above Bsharri in northern Lebanon. UNESCO listed the grove in 1985 together with the Qadisha Valley below it. It is one of the last remnants of the forests that once covered Mount Lebanon.","more":"Mount Lebanon was once forested with these from end to end. What is left is a handful of protected groves, and the country now carries on its flag a tree it very nearly ran out of."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Individual trees have been measured at 88 ft (27 m) tall with a girth of 39 ft (12 m). Trunk diameters of 5 to 7 ft (1.5–2.1 m) are on record, on a single straight columnar stem."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The cones stand upright on the branch like small barrels, 3 to 4.7 in (8–12 cm) long. They do not drop whole: they fall apart on the tree, shedding scales and seeds until only the bare central spike is left standing there.","more":"Pines drop their cones whole, and you can pick them up under the tree. Cedars do not. The cone comes apart where it stands, scale by scale, and all you find on the ground underneath is loose scales and seeds."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The needles do not run along the twig one by one. They come in tufts of 15 to 35 on short stubby side shoots, so a cedar branch looks studded with green rosettes rather than combed."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Cedar shows up in the boat too. The first-century fishing boat pulled out of the mud of the Sea of Galilee — the one people call the Jesus Boat — has cedar of Lebanon in it."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why does a cedar chest keep moths out of clothes? The best read is the resin. The oils that give the wood its smell are the same ones insects avoid, so the smell and the protection are one thing, not two."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows in the mountains of Lebanon, Syria and Turkey, most abundantly between 4,300 and 9,800 ft (1,300–3,000 m). It is a high, cold, snowy tree, not a tree of the warm coast."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"It grows quickly until about 45 to 50 years old, then slows sharply, and after about 70 it barely widens at all. Nearly all the growing is done early."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week put trees on the mountains before there was a single builder alive to want one. The wood that resists rot and insects was finished long before anyone had a temple to raise with it."}]},{"id":"olive","name":"Olive tree","group":"trees","blurb":"A slow, gnarled Mediterranean tree whose fruit is inedible straight off the branch.","size":"Rarely more than 25 to 50 ft (8–15 m) tall, but the trunk goes hollow, twisted and knotted — one measures 82 ft (25 m) around.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":50,"life":1000},"wonder":"Burn one to the ground and the root sends up new shoots — which is why an old olive is so hard to kill and so hard to date.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 8:11: the dove Noah sent out came back in the evening with a freshly plucked olive leaf in her beak, and that was how he knew the water had gone down off the land. The olive is the tree in the first good-news message in the Bible."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"You cannot eat an olive off the tree. A young olive is up to 14 percent oleuropein by dry weight, and oleuropein is savagely bitter. Curing takes a few days in lye or a few months in brine before the fruit is edible.","more":"Curing is removal, not seasoning. Lye or long soaking pulls the oleuropein out; brine does it slowly while fermentation changes the flavour alongside. Every olive anybody has ever eaten has been through one of those processes first."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The flesh of the fruit is 60 to 70 percent oil. A typical tree yields about 3 to 5 lb (1.5–2.2 kg) of olive oil in a year — a couple of bottles for twelve months of standing there."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Frost or fire can kill an olive's whole top and the rootstock still lives, throwing up new shoots. So an ancient olive is often a young trunk standing on a very old root — which is why counting the trunk badly undercounts the plant.","more":"It also means a grove can be older than any trunk standing in it. Cut, burned or frozen, the root keeps its place and starts again, so the grove carries on with new trunks in the old positions."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Wood sampled from three of the eight olives in the Garden of Gethsemane in Jerusalem carbon-dates to AD 1092, 1166 and 1198. DNA from all eight came out with matching profiles — they all trace back to one parent plant.","more":"Matching DNA across all eight is the stronger result. It means the grove was propagated from cuttings of a single tree rather than grown from separate seeds."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The olive known as Al Badawi, in the village of Al Walaja near Bethlehem, measures 25 m — about 82 ft — around the trunk. It would take a dozen children with their arms out to close a circle around it."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Claims about ancient olives run wild and mostly do not check out. The Bcheale tree in Lebanon was advertised at 4000 BC; the research put it at over 1,100 years instead. Portugal, Crete and Montenegro all have claimants too.","more":"Olives make their own age hard to check. The trunk goes hollow and the oldest wood rots out of the middle, so the wood available to sample is usually not the oldest wood the plant ever grew."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why make the fruit that bitter? The best read: oleuropein keeps things from eating the crop before the seed inside is finished. It works. The only reason we eat olives at all is that people figured out how to wash the bitterness out."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Olive leaves are dark green on top and silvery-grey underneath, and a whole grove flickers pale when the wind turns them over. The underside is covered in tiny scales."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The wood is dense and hard, with a wild swirling grain from the twisted trunk — good for bowls and handles, and difficult to work with anything blunt."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three named fruit trees, each with its seed in itself. An olive is a stone wrapped in oil-rich flesh. That oil has lit lamps, fried food and anointed kings — from a tree that will grow on stony ground nothing else wants."}]},{"id":"fig","name":"Common fig","group":"trees","blurb":"A Mediterranean tree whose fruit is not a fruit at all, but a bag of flowers turned inside out.","size":"23 to 33 ft (7–10 m) tall, with smooth pale bark and big hand-shaped leaves you could cover your face with.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":33,"life":100},"wonder":"Every fig is a sealed room full of flowers that will never see daylight, and the whole design only makes sense once you know about the wasp.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A fig is not a fruit. It is a syconium — a hollow fleshy pouch lined on the inside with hundreds of tiny flowers, all facing inwards. When you bite a fig you are eating a flower bed with the walls still on.","more":"Cut one in half and look. What seems to be flesh with strings through it is the inside wall of the pouch, and the strings are the flowers — hundreds of them, packed in, every one facing the middle."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"There is one way in: a pinhole at the tip called the ostiole. The fig wasp Blastophaga psenes squeezes through it to reach the flowers, and it is such a tight fit that she generally loses her wings on the way."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Myth-buster: the figs in the shop have no wasp in them. Smyrna-type figs do need wasp pollination or the young figs simply drop, but most eating varieties are persistent types that set fruit with no pollination at all. The crunch is seeds.","more":"So the answer to the question every child asks is: it depends on the fig. Smyrna types genuinely do involve a wasp. The common shop varieties involve none at all, and no wasp ever went into them."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"In a wild fig that does host a wasp, the tree deals with the body itself. Figs contain ficin, an enzyme that breaks down protein, and it dissolves the wasp where she lies."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The fig is the first plant called by name in the Bible. Genesis 3:7 has Adam and Eve sewing fig leaves together after eating the forbidden fruit — which was never identified as an apple, or as anything else."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In the Bible, a man sitting under his own vine and fig tree is the picture of peace and safety — 1 Kings 4:25 uses it of Solomon's reign, and Micah 4:4 uses it of a promised peace."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The milky sap from the green parts of a fig tree irritates skin. Get it on you, then go out in the sun, and it can burn — the sunlight makes the reaction worse. Fig pickers cover their arms for a reason."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Fig trees come in two arrangements. Caprifigs are the ones that house the wasps. In the edible kinds the female flowers are built too long for the wasp to lay her eggs down into, so she pollinates and gets nothing back."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Why put your flowers inside a bag? The best read is that the bag is what makes the partnership work: a walled nursery the wasp cannot get from anywhere else, in exchange for a courier no wind or bee could replace. Neither side works alone.","more":"This does not work halfway. A fig with a door and no wasp sets no seed. A wasp with nowhere to lay has no grubs. Whatever else you conclude about it, the two halves only make sense together."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Its home ground is the Mediterranean and western and southern Asia. It will grow out of a crack in a wall on almost no soil, which is why you find figs sprouting from ruins and bridges."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three brought seed-bearing plants and fruit trees. The fig leaf turns up again in Genesis 3 as the first thing people ever made for themselves — cut from a tree that had been standing there since the third day."}]},{"id":"apple","name":"Apple tree","group":"trees","blurb":"A tree that refuses to copy itself from seed, so every apple variety you have eaten is a cutting off one original plant.","size":"Left alone it reaches about 30 ft (9 m); orchard trees are kept to 10 or 15 ft (3–5 m) so people can pick without ladders.","homes":["backyard","forest"],"stats":{"height":30,"life":100},"wonder":"The seed always makes an apple tree and never makes the same apple — the kind holds perfectly, the variety not at all.","facts":[{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Plant a pip out of a Gala and you do not get a Gala. Apples are extreme heterozygotes, which means the seedlings come out different from both parents — usually small, usually sour, occasionally wonderful.","more":"Which is why most chance apple seedlings are small and sour. Out of a great many of them, occasionally one is excellent — and that single tree then has to be copied by hand, because its own seeds will not repeat it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"record","text":"So every Bramley, every Granny Smith, every Pink Lady is a cutting. A shoot from the original plant is grafted onto a rootstock, and the rootstock decides how big the tree gets and how tough it is. The variety is one plant, copied endlessly.","more":"A graft is a cutting joined onto another tree's root system so the two grow together. The top decides what the apples taste like; the bottom decides how big the tree gets and how tough it is. Two plants, one trunk, on purpose."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"There are more than 7,500 named apple varieties. Almost all of them started as one lucky seedling somebody noticed and then grafted."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Apples are self-incompatible: a tree cannot fertilise itself. An orchard needs at least two different varieties flowering at the same time, and something to carry pollen between them, or there is no crop."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"The wild apple Malus sieversii still grows on the forested flanks of the Tian Shan mountains, in southern Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and north-western China. Whole hillsides of wild apple trees, all of them different from each other."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The apple tree at Woolsthorpe Manor in Lincolnshire — the one in the Isaac Newton story — is a variety called Flower of Kent. It blew down in a storm around 1816, re-rooted from the base, and is still growing there.","more":"Grafted copies of that tree grow on every continent except Antarctica. Not all of them are genuine: in 2016 Canada's National Research Council found its prized Newton trees were neither original nor even Flower of Kent."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Myth-buster: Genesis never says apple. The fruit in the garden is not named at all. The apple turns up in western European art and storytelling much later, by about the twelfth century, and then stuck.","more":"Painters needed a fruit their audience would recognise, and northern Europe had apples. Later writers took the picture from the pictures. It is a tidy example of a detail everyone knows and nobody can point to in the text."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Apple pips contain amygdalin, a compound that carries cyanide. Before you panic: the US National Library of Medicine has no record of anyone being poisoned by eating apple seeds the ordinary way."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"An apple has 17 chromosomes and a genome of roughly 650 megabases — small, as fruit trees go, which is part of why it was one of the earlier fruit trees to be fully read."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"World apple production in 2023 came to 97 million tonnes, and China grew 51 percent of it. More than half the apples on Earth come from one country."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three: fruit trees yielding fruit after their kind. That is exactly what happens. An apple seed makes an apple tree, without fail — but never the same apple. The kind is fixed; the variety has to be carried by hand."}]},{"id":"willow","name":"Weeping willow","group":"trees","blurb":"The drooping tree by the water — named after a mistake, and the source of the idea behind aspirin.","size":"66 to 82 ft (20–25 m) tall, with branchlets that hang straight down to the ground like a curtain.","homes":["freshwater","backyard"],"stats":{"height":82,"life":75},"wonder":"Something in that bitter bark was worth chewing, and people worked out what and why over more than a century of careful trying.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Linnaeus named it Salix babylonica in 1753 because he thought it was the tree of Psalm 137, where the exiles hung their harps on the willows by the rivers of Babylon. He was wrong: this willow is native to northern China, Korea, Mongolia, Japan and Siberia.","more":"Scientific names stick even when they turn out wrong, because the rule is stability rather than accuracy. So a Chinese willow permanently carries the name of a Mesopotamian city it has probably never grown anywhere near."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"The trees actually growing along the Euphrates were most likely Euphrates poplar, Populus euphratica, which carries willow-shaped leaves on long drooping shoots — an easy tree to mistake. Some modern Bible translations now render the word as poplars."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 1763 the Reverend Edward Stone reported to the Royal Society that powdered white willow bark cured ague — the shivering fevers. Over five years he had given it to some fifty sufferers, with success in nearly all of them.","more":"Stone was working from a bad theory: he believed cures grow where diseases do, so he went looking for a fever remedy in a wet place and tasted a willow twig. The theory was wrong and the result was right, which happens more often than anyone likes to admit."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Johann Buchner got fairly pure salicin crystals out of willow bark in 1828, and Pierre-Joseph Leroux worked out another way to extract it the following year. That was the bitter chemical Stone had been handing out without knowing its name."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"In 1897 Felix Hoffmann at Bayer refluxed salicylic acid with acetic anhydride and made acetylsalicylic acid. Bayer began selling it in 1899 under the name Aspirin.","more":"The point of the change was the stomach. Salicylic acid works but is hard on people who take it; the acetylated version was gentler, and that is the version that ended up in a billion medicine cabinets."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Myth-buster: there is no aspirin in a willow. The tree makes salicin. Aspirin is a made compound that does not occur in the bark at all — chewing willow is not the same as taking a tablet."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Push a cut willow stick into wet ground and it roots. Willows are among the easiest trees on Earth to propagate from a piece, which is why a fallen willow branch lying in a ditch turns into a willow.","more":"Gardeners use that on purpose. Willow twigs soaked in water make a liquid that is used to help other cuttings strike. Whatever a willow does to start roots, it does a great deal of it."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Gardeners use that. Living willow is woven into fences, domes and tunnels while the rods are fresh; the rods root where they touch soil, and the structure keeps growing after it is finished."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Willows sit by water and their roots go looking for it. The best read on why they wreck drains is simple opportunism: a root that meets a damp crack in a pipe grows into it, and once inside it thickens."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"For a big tree it is short-lived — 40 to 75 years. Weeping willows grow fast, get large, and are gone in a human lifetime, which is unusual company for something 80 feet tall."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three put plants on the earth before there was anyone sick enough to need them. Whatever the tree makes salicin for, a fever remedy was sitting in the bark of a common riverside tree the whole time, waiting for somebody to taste it."}]},{"id":"birch","name":"Silver birch","group":"trees","blurb":"The white-barked tree that gets to burnt ground first, with bark that works as a waterproof sheet.","size":"49 to 82 ft (15–25 m) tall, exceptionally 102 ft (31 m), on a slim trunk usually under 16 in (40 cm) across.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":102},"wonder":"It arrives first on bare ground, makes the place liveable, and is then shaded out by the trees it sheltered.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The outer bark is up to 20 percent betulin — the white powder that comes off on your fingers. Betulin is waxy and water-repellent, which is what makes birch bark a genuinely waterproof sheet.","more":"Betulin is why birch bark peels off in papery sheets and why those sheets shed water. It is also why birch bark lights easily even when it is damp, which is the first thing anybody learns about making a fire in a birch wood."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It is a pioneer: one of the first trees to come up on bare land or on ground that has just burned. Birch does not wait for a forest; it starts one."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"And then it loses. Birch cannot tolerate shade, so the slower trees that established under its shelter grow up past it and shade it out. The reasoning is plain: fast and short-lived beats slow and steady on bare ground, and loses everywhere else.","more":"Being first is a strategy with the cost built in. Birch grows fast, seeds heavily, tolerates no shade and dies comparatively young. It is not built to hold ground; it is built to reach ground nothing else has reached yet."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One birch catkin releases roughly 1.66 million grains of pollen. The seeds are 1 to 2 mm long with papery wings, packed into hanging catkins 0.8 to 1.6 in (2–4 cm) long, and they blow a long way."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Silver birch grows with fungi wrapped around its roots, trading sugar for minerals. One of its regular partners is Amanita muscaria, the red toadstool with white spots. Fungi are not plants at all — no chlorophyll, no making food from light.","more":"The brown birch bolete and the chanterelle turn up around birch too. The fungus is the tree's mining operation, reaching soil the roots cannot, and it gets paid in sugar the tree made from sunlight."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Birch tar is a glue. The man found frozen in an Alpine glacier in 1991 carried an axe whose copper blade was set into the shaft with birch tar and bound with leather, plus two birch-bark containers stitched with lime bast — one holding charcoal embers wrapped in maple leaves."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Birch bark was paper. At least 1,025 birch-bark letters have been dug up in medieval Russian towns — 923 in Novgorod alone — scratched into the inner bark with a stylus instead of written in ink. The first was found on 26 July 1951.","more":"They are ordinary letters: debts, shopping, complaints, a child's writing practice. Parchment was expensive and birch bark was free, so people wrote everyday things on it and threw them away, which is exactly why they survived in the mud."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"First Nations across North America built canoes, bowls and tipi covers from birch bark — light, flexible and waterproof. Only the outer bark is taken, and stripped at the right time in late spring, it does not kill the tree."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Birch sap can be tapped like maple, but it is only about 1 percent sugar. Boiling it down to syrup takes an enormous quantity of sap for a very small jar."},{"cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"Birch timber goes into plywood, veneer, parquet blocks, skis and kitchen utensils, and the bark was used for tanning leather. Silver birch is Finland's national tree."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three is entirely plants — the whole day, with no animal on the earth until day five. Birch does that job in miniature, over and over: first onto bare ground, making soil and shade for everything that follows."}]},{"id":"sunflower","name":"Sunflower","group":"flowers","blurb":"What looks like one big flower is a packed disc of hundreds of tiny separate flowers sharing a single stalk.","size":"A garden sunflower stands about 10 ft (3 m) with a head the width of a dinner plate. The record holder reached 35 ft 9 in (10.9 m).","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":10,"life":1},"wonder":"Plants were made on day three and the sun on day four, which means the sun-follower was standing there first.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Alex Babich grew a sunflower in Fort Wayne, Indiana that measured 35 ft 9 in (10.9 m), verified on 3 September 2025. He nicknamed it Clover. It beat Hans-Peter Schiffer's 30 ft 1 in (9.17 m) grown in Kaarst, Germany, a record that had stood since 2014.","more":"Growing one that tall is its own contest. Schiffer broke the record four times in five years before Babich took it off him. The plants have to be staked, fed hard and sheltered from wind, because the head at the top ends up far too heavy for the stem to carry on its own."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Pull one apart. The yellow outer blades are ray florets, and the whole brown centre is packed with disc florets. Each disc floret is a complete little flower with its own petals, and each one makes exactly one seed.","more":"So a sunflower head is a crowd, not an individual. That is why the family it belongs to is called the composite family. Daisies, dandelions and thistles are built the same way, just smaller."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The florets sit in two sets of spirals running opposite ways, and you can count them. It usually comes out 34 one way and 55 the other. On a very large head it runs 89 and 144. Those are Fibonacci numbers, where each is the sum of the two before it."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Young sunflowers swing east to west across the day, then swing back overnight so they are facing east again before dawn. Hagop Atamian and Stacey Harmer's team filmed it and found the cause: the stem grows longer on its east side by day and its west side by night.","more":"They filmed it with time-lapse cameras, then went further. Plants moved into a room with one fixed light carried on swinging by themselves for several days, on a roughly 24-hour rhythm. There is a clock running in the stem, not just a reaction to whatever light happens to be falling on it."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"Grown-up heads stop swinging and settle facing east. That is not the plant getting tired. The same team measured east-facing heads as warmer in the morning, and warm heads pulled in more bee visits. The stopping point is the argument, not the movement.","more":"They tested it by turning pots around so mature heads faced west. Those heads stayed colder in the morning and got fewer visitors. Pointing a heater at the west-facing flowers brought the visitors back, which is how the warmth was pinned down as the thing that mattered."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Sunflower seed is one of the world's big oil crops. About 51.9 million tonnes of seed were harvested worldwide in 2024, with Russia and Ukraine together growing roughly 58 percent of it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One seed per floret means a big head carries a thousand seeds or more. Count the empty sockets on a head the birds have stripped and you are counting flowers."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"The wild plant is North American, native to the central and western United States, southern Canada and northern Mexico. Every sunflower field in Europe grows from plants carried out of North America."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Vincent van Gogh painted his sunflower canvases in Arles in the south of France in August 1888, then painted repetitions the following year. They are among the most copied paintings in the world."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Why spirals at all? Measure the angle between one floret and the next and it comes to about 137.5 degrees, the golden angle. The best read is that this angle is the one that packs the most seeds into a flat disc without leaving gaps."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names seed-bearing plants, each yielding seed after its kind. A sunflower head is that instruction taken seriously: hundreds of flowers on one stalk, every one of them making a seed, all in one summer."}]},{"id":"corpse-flower","name":"Corpse flower","group":"flowers","blurb":"A Sumatran plant that banks food underground for years, then spends the lot on one enormous bloom that smells of rotting meat.","size":"The bloom stands about 10 ft (3 m) — the height of a basketball hoop — over an underground store that can top 200 lb (90 kg).","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"height":10,"weight":200},"wonder":"Everything about it is oversized except the flowers themselves, which are small, ordinary and hidden right at the bottom.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The tallest one ever measured reached 10 ft 7 in (3.225 m) at Meise Botanic Garden in Belgium on 13 August 2024."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The underground store, called a corm, is the largest of any flowering plant. Over 200 lb (90 kg) is normal. One grown at the Royal Botanic Garden Edinburgh reached 339 lb (153.9 kg) after seven years.","more":"The corm is not a root. It is a swollen underground stem doing the job of a battery. Everything the plant collects through its one enormous leaf gets packed into it, and a single bloom empties it again."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It is not one flower. Hundreds of small ones are ringed around the base of the central spike, hidden behind the frilled collar. The famous part is packaging."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The smell has been taken apart chemical by chemical: dimethyl trisulfide (old cheese), trimethylamine (rotting fish), isovaleric acid (sweaty socks), phenol and indole. People have picked it up half a mile (800 m) away.","more":"Those are the same compounds a dead animal gives off as it breaks down. Nothing about the smell is a rough imitation. It is the real chemistry, made by a plant."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The spike heats itself. At its peak the tip reaches about 99°F (37°C), which is human body temperature. Put a hand near it and you can feel the warmth coming off."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Why heat up? The best read is that warm air rises and carries the smell with it, the way steam lifts off a hot mug. A cold spike would leave the scent pooled at ground level where far fewer insects would cross it."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"The visitors are carrion beetles and flies, the insects that lay eggs in dead animals. Working backwards from the chemicals, that is exactly the crowd those compounds would reach."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A plant grown from seed takes about ten years to bloom the first time. After that it flowers every three to seven years, and only once the corm has banked enough. The bloom itself lasts 24 to 36 hours.","more":"Botanic gardens weigh the corm between blooms for exactly this reason. If it has not put on enough since last time, the plant sends up another leaf instead of a flower and spends another year or two saving up."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Between blooms it grows a single leaf, up to 15 ft (4.7 m) tall on a stalk as thick as a person's thigh. One leaf, standing alone in the forest looking like a small tree."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Italian botanist Odoardo Beccari found it in Sumatra on 6 August 1878. It first flowered outside Sumatra at Kew, in London, in 1889."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows wild only in western Sumatra, Indonesia, and is listed as endangered. Fewer than 1,000 are thought to be left in the wild, mostly because forest has been cleared for oil palm."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three, before any animal. This one spends a decade filling a store underground before it flowers even once, which is a lot of patience to build into something that has no reason to hurry."}]},{"id":"rafflesia","name":"Rafflesia","group":"flowers","blurb":"The largest single flower there is, growing straight out of a jungle vine with no leaves, no stem and no roots of its own.","size":"About 3 ft 3 in (1 m) across — a car tyre laid flat — and up to 24 lb (11 kg).","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"weight":24},"wonder":"A plant stripped down to almost nothing but a flower, and the flower is the biggest there is.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A bloom measured in a forest in West Sumatra in January 2020 came to 3 ft 7.7 in (111 cm) across, the widest reliably recorded. A specimen logged at 3 ft 9 in (1.14 m) is the largest claim on the books."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"No leaves. No stem. No roots. No chlorophyll, so nothing green anywhere on it. Everything you can see of the plant is the flower; the rest lives as threads inside the tissue of a jungle vine."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"It cannot make its own food, and the reasoning is not subtle: green is the colour of the machinery that turns sunlight into sugar, and there is none. Everything it uses is taken from a Tetrastigma vine.","more":"Cut open an infected vine and you find pale strands of Rafflesia running through it, closer to a fungus threading through wood than to anything you would call a plant. It only becomes visible when a bud pushes out through the vine's bark."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The five fleshy lobes are up to 0.75 in (1.9 cm) thick — about the thickness of a slice of bread — and are brick red with raised white warts across them."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Buds swell for many months before opening, reaching cabbage size at about 12 in (30 cm) across. The largest bud on record was 17 in (43 cm) wide, found at Mount Sago in Sumatra in May 1956.","more":"Most buds never open at all. They rot, dry out, or get eaten by squirrels and wild pigs. Counting buds at a known site and seeing how few of them make it to flowering is the main way anyone tracks whether a population is holding on."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"After all those months of swelling, the open flower lasts only a few days. Then it collapses into a black slimy heap."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Each flower is either male or female, never both. So seed is only set when a male and a female happen to open close together at the same time. That is the strongest reason anyone has for why the plant is so rare.","more":"Stack up the odds: the flower lasts days, the bud takes months, and the plant lives only inside one kind of vine. Where forest has been broken into separate patches, a male and a female may never open close enough together at all."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"It smells of rotting meat, which is where the nickname corpse flower comes from. The visitors are flies, and they crawl over the central disc."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"Brick red, blotched with pale spots, and reeking. The best read is that the whole flower is doing a rotten-carcass impression, because that is the only thing the colour, the smell and the flies have in common."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The British surgeon Joseph Arnold collected one in 1818 at Bencoolen in Sumatra, on an expedition led by Stamford Raffles. The plant ended up carrying both their names."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows only in the forests of Sumatra and Borneo and is in trouble from logging. At sites visited often by tourists, the number of buds produced has been dropping year on year."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names seed-bearing plants. This is one, in the strangest possible packaging: no leaf, no root, no green, just a flower and a seed at the end of it. The job is unchanged even when everything else has been stripped away."}]},{"id":"orchid-bee","name":"Bee orchid","group":"flowers","blurb":"A small European orchid whose lower lip is furry, brown and shaped so much like a bee that people look twice.","size":"6 to 20 in (15–50 cm) tall, with each flower about the size of a thumbnail.","homes":["grassland"],"stats":{"height":1.5},"wonder":"Every bee orchid grows the same furry lip, the same two thin antenna petals and the same three green sepals behind them.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The big lower lip, the labellum, is velvety chestnut brown with a yellow-edged pattern across it, and two narrow inner petals stand up above like antennae. Hold a bumblebee alongside and the resemblance is not something you have to talk yourself into."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Behind the bee-shaped lip sit three broad pink sepals, spread out like a propeller. The whole arrangement is one flower, and a spike carries several of them up the stem."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"Around the Mediterranean, male Eucera longicornis bees have been watched landing on the lip and trying to mate with it, leaving with pollen sacs stuck to their heads. The best read is that the resemblance works on the bee, whatever else it does.","more":"That reading rests on watching the bees, not on the shape alone. The flower also gives off a scent close to the smell of a female bee, which is why the males keep coming back to a lip that is plainly not a bee once they are on it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"In Britain that bee is scarce, and the orchid mostly pollinates itself instead. Its pollen sacs hang on long flexible stalks that gradually bend down until they touch the flower's own sticky stigma. A puff of wind finishes the job.","more":"Pollinating itself is a safety net rather than a first choice. It guarantees seed, but every seedling is a copy of the one parent. In southern Europe, where the bee is around, the same species crosses with its neighbours and the seed comes out more mixed."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Darwin measured and described that swinging motion in his 1862 book on how orchids get fertilised by insects. He noted the stalks holding the pollen sacs were unusually long and bendy in this species."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows from Ireland and Portugal east to Iran and the Caucasus, and is common right round the Mediterranean. In Britain it flowers in June and July; further south it is out by mid-April."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Orchid seed is dust. A single seed pod can hold over a million of them, each one too small to see clearly without a lens."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Those seeds carry almost no packed lunch. They only get going once a fungus moves into them and feeds the seedling. The reasoning is a straight test: sow orchid seed in clean compost and nothing happens, sow it with the fungus and it grows."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Orchids make up one of the two largest plant families on earth: about 28,000 accepted species in around 700 genera, which is somewhere between 6 and 11 percent of every seed plant there is.","more":"Part of the reason for that number is where orchids will grow. They turn up on every continent except Antarctica, and they are thickest in the tropics, where a great many of them live perched on tree branches instead of in soil."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Vanilla is an orchid. The pods of Vanilla planifolia are the only orchid product grown as a major food flavouring anywhere in the world."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The names are blunt. Ophrys is Greek for eyebrow, which is what the furry lip looks like. Apifera is Latin for bee-bearing."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three has plants yielding seed after their kind. Bee orchid lips vary a little in pattern from plant to plant, but the parts list never changes: one furry lip, two antenna petals, three pink sepals, every time."}]},{"id":"lotus","name":"Sacred lotus","group":"flowers","blurb":"A water plant whose leaves shed dirt so completely that paint and fabric makers copied the surface.","size":"Leaves 2 ft 7 in to 3 ft 3 in (80–100 cm) across, held on stalks up to 6 ft 7 in (2 m) long.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"height":6},"wonder":"A flower that runs its own heater for two days is a strange thing to find growing out of a pond.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Leaf blades reach 2 ft 7 in to 3 ft 3 in (80–100 cm) across on stalks up to 6 ft 7 in (2 m). The flowers open up to 14 in (35 cm) wide, about the size of a dinner plate."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Water will not wet a lotus leaf. It balls up and rolls straight off, picking up dust on the way. Wilhelm Barthlott named this the lotus effect. Under a microscope the surface is covered in bumps 10 to 20 micrometres tall, coated in wax.","more":"A water drop sitting on that surface touches only about 0.6 percent of it. The rest of the drop is resting on air trapped between the bumps, which is why nothing sticks and why the leaf is always clean."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Manufacturers copied it directly. Self-cleaning wall paint and stain-shedding fabrics such as NanoSphere and HeiQ Eco Dry all come from studying that leaf surface. In tests in October 2005, treated cloth shrugged off tomato sauce, coffee and red wine."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The flower runs a heater. It holds itself between 86 and 95°F (30–35°C) inside for about two days, even when the surrounding air falls to 50°F (10°C)."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Why bother? The best read is that warm air carries scent further, and that beetles which crawl in overnight stay warm enough to keep moving about and shuffling pollen around instead of going sluggish in the cold."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Seeds recovered from a dry lakebed at Xipaozi village in Liaoning, north-east China, still sprouted after being buried in the mud. Of the seeds tested, 84 percent grew.","more":"The seed coat is the reason. It is hard, close to airtight, and packed with proteins that stay stable when they ought to have broken down. Researchers had to file the coats open before sowing, because water cannot get in on its own."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"How long had they been down there? Carbon measured in the seed coats works out to roughly 1,300 years for the oldest ones. Nobody watched them lie there. The carbon is the whole argument, and it is worth knowing that."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The seed pod looks like a showerhead: a flat green disc with 10 to 30 seeds sitting loose in holes across its face. Each seed is about 1 in (2.5 cm) across."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The underground stem creeps as much as 66 ft (20 m) in a single growing season, which is why one lotus plant can fill a pond.","more":"That speed is why lotus is treated as a pest in some warm countries. One plant dropped into a pond can cover the surface within a couple of seasons, and clearing it means getting every last piece of buried stem out of the mud."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Every part gets eaten somewhere. The underground stem is fried or pickled or dropped in soup, the seeds are ground into mooncake paste, the leaves wrap steamed rice and the flowers are brewed as tea."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"It grows from central and northern India up to 4,600 ft (1,400 m), through northern Indochina and East Asia. It is the national flower of India and turns up constantly in Hindu and Buddhist art."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three, before the sun of day four. This one does not simply take heat from the air; it makes and holds its own, in every flower, in every pond it grows in."}]},{"id":"rose","name":"Rose","group":"flowers","blurb":"A prickly shrub with 140-odd wild species behind it and around 30,000 garden versions bred out of them.","size":"A garden rose stands about 5 ft (1.5 m). The climbing dog rose at Hildesheim Cathedral covers a wall 69 ft (21 m) across.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":5,"life":35},"wonder":"Five petals and a ring of stamens is the whole wild plan, and every fancy garden rose is a rearrangement of it.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Those spikes are prickles, not thorns. A true thorn is a modified stem, wired into the plant's plumbing. A prickle is just an outgrowth of the skin, and you can snap one cleanly off a rose stem with a thumbnail without hurting the plant."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"There are 140 to 180 wild rose species, most of them native to Asia with smaller numbers in Europe, North America and north-west Africa. From those, growers have bred and named 30,000 to 35,000 garden hybrids."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The rose climbing the wall of Hildesheim Cathedral in Germany is the oldest living rose known, a wild dog rose documented for at least 700 years. Allied bombing in 1945 burnt it to a charred stump.","more":"The roots under the rubble came through it. That spring the stump pushed out 25 new shoots, and it was flowering again while the cathedral above it was still being rebuilt. It still flowers every year."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Rose hips, the fruit left after the petals drop, are among the richest sources of vitamin C in any plant. Depending on the species, 3.5 oz (100 g) of fresh hips holds several hundred to over 1,000 mg. The same weight of orange holds about 53 mg."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Rose oil is absurdly concentrated. The oil pressed out weighs somewhere between a three-thousandth and a six-thousandth of the flowers that went in. Roughly two thousand flowers make one gram of oil.","more":"Bulgaria and Turkey grow most of the world's rose oil. The flowers have to be picked before the sun is properly up, because the oil starts leaving the petals as the day warms, and the whole morning's crop goes straight to the still."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"On climbing roses the prickles hook backwards, pointing down the stem. That is the wrong way round for stabbing something climbing up and the right way round for gripping whatever the rose is scrambling over. The direction is the argument."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A wild rose has five petals. Cut open a fat garden rose and in among the extra petals you find odd half-and-half pieces, part petal and part pollen-bearing stamen. The extra petals were stamens.","more":"Growers have been picking out doubled roses for centuries without knowing what they were doing. The switch that decides whether a young flower part turns into a petal or a stamen was only worked out much later, using genes first identified in snapdragons."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The hip is a container, not a fruit stone. Split one and it is packed with hard seeds surrounded by fine irritating hairs. Birds eat the flesh and pass the seeds elsewhere."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Rose scent is not one smell. Different species smell of tea, of cloves, of apples or of nothing at all, and a lot of the showiest modern roses were bred for looks and lost the scent on the way."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Wild roses are hedgerow and woodland-edge plants across the northern half of the world. The dog rose scrambling through a British hedge is the same kind of plant as the one on the cathedral wall."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three sets out seed-bearing plants after their kind. Thirty-five thousand garden roses sounds like a lot of different things until you notice they are all shufflings of one plan: five petals, a ring of stamens, a hip full of seed."}]},{"id":"tulip","name":"Tulip","group":"flowers","blurb":"A mountain bulb from Central Asia that, for one winter in Holland, sold for more than a house.","size":"About 18 in (45 cm) tall, growing from a bulb 0.4 to 2 in (1–5 cm) across.","homes":["mountain","backyard"],"stats":{"height":1.5},"wonder":"A bulb splits off copies of itself, which is the only reason one particular tulip could be bought and sold at all.","facts":[{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"There are about 75 wild tulip species, and none of them started in Holland. They grow in a band along 40 degrees north, from south-eastern Europe through Turkey and across Central Asia to the Tian Shan and Pamir-Alai mountains, which is where most kinds are.","more":"Wild tulips are small tough mountain plants: short stems, narrow grey leaves, flowers that shut tight at night and open in the sun. The tall straight-stemmed tulip in a vase is the result of centuries of picking out the biggest, and it looks very little like its wild relatives."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Bulbs were sent to Vienna in 1554 by Ogier de Busbecq, an ambassador to the Ottoman court. They reached the Netherlands around 1593, when the botanist Carolus Clusius took up a post at Leiden University and planted them."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Tulip mania. Contract prices climbed hard from 1634 and collapsed in the first week of February 1637. On 3 February an auctioneer in Haarlem stood in front of a room and could not find a single buyer.","more":"Most of the trade was in paper contracts for bulbs still in the ground, not in bulbs anyone was holding. When spring came and the contracts had to turn into actual bulbs, the prices fell to a twentieth or even a hundredth of what people had agreed to pay."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"A Semper Augustus bulb was quoted at 10,000 guilders. A skilled craftsman earned about 300 guilders a year, so that one bulb was priced at over 33 years of a good wage."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The flamed and streaked petals everyone was paying for were caused by an illness. Tulip breaking virus splits one petal colour into two or more. It also weakened the bulb, so the most expensive tulips were the sickest ones.","more":"That is why the famous striped varieties are gone. Each generation of an infected bulb was feebler than the last, and the plants eventually gave out. The striped tulips sold today get their pattern from breeding, not from the virus."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Grown from seed, a tulip takes five to eight years to reach flowering size. Growers do not wait. They lift the bulbs and split off the small copies each one has made underground."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"In the Dutch Hunger Winter of 1944 and 1945, people in the occupied Netherlands ate tulip bulbs. Not as a curiosity. As food, in a famine, without much idea of how to prepare them safely.","more":"A bulb is stored food, which is exactly why people reached for them. They also carry substances that irritate the mouth and gut, and the core and outer layers have to be taken out first. Nobody had written that down for the public before the famine."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The Netherlands now produces about 3 billion tulip bulbs a year, most of them for export. It is the largest commercial grower in the world by a long way."},{"cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"The word tulip probably comes from a Turkish term for a turban, because of the shape of the closed flower."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Tulips keep growing after cutting. Put a bunch in a vase and measure them: over a few days the stems stretch another inch or more, and they bend towards the window while they do it."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"The flower opens in warmth and shuts in cold. Bring cut tulips into a heated room and they spread within the hour; move them somewhere chilly and they close up again."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three has seed-bearing plants after their kind. Through all of the mania, the streaking, the breeding and the fortunes, a tulip stayed a tulip. Colours and prices moved. The plant did not turn into something else."}]},{"id":"dandelion","name":"Dandelion","group":"flowers","blurb":"A lawn weed carrying the best-studied flying machine in the plant world.","size":"About 12 in (30 cm) tall, with a seed head roughly the size of a golf ball.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":1,"life":10},"wonder":"A hundred bristles holding up a ring of spinning air the seed never even touches.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The yellow head is not one flower. It is 40 to more than 100 separate little flowers packed side by side, each with its own tiny strap-shaped petal, and each one makes a single seed."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Counted properly, a head holds 54 to 172 seeds. One plant can put out more than 5,000 seeds in a year, which is why pulling a few is never enough."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Each seed hangs beneath a pappus of about 100 bristles. In 2018 Cathal Cummins and his team in Edinburgh put them in a wind tunnel with smoke and found a ring of spinning air sitting above the bristles, not touching them.","more":"It is called a separated vortex ring. Nothing else, natural or built, was known to fly that way. The ring pulls the seed upward and steadies it, which is how something with no engine stays up long enough to cross a field."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"The gaps between the bristles are the point. Air flows through them, and the spacing is what keeps the ring steady. The reasoning comes straight from the wind tunnel: change the spacing and the ring breaks apart."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Wind carries the seeds several hundred yards from the plant they came off. Some are picked up by rising air and go a great deal further than that."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Most North American dandelions make seed with no pollination at all. The reasoning: they carry three sets of chromosomes instead of two, which normally jams ordinary seed-making, yet they seed heavily and the seedlings come out as copies of the parent.","more":"It means the seed head on a lawn dandelion is a set of clones. That is why the plants in one field can look identical while dandelions a county away look subtly different. Botanists have named hundreds of these clone types in Britain alone, and telling them apart is specialist work."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Snap any part of it and white latex bleeds out, sticky and bitter. Stem, leaf, root, flower stalk. All of it."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The taproot is the reason it beats gardeners. Break the root and leave a piece in the ground and a new plant grows back from the fragment.","more":"The taproot can go down a couple of feet in decent soil and stores food through the winter. A gardener who snaps it off at the collar has removed the leaves and left the battery, which is why the plant is back within a few weeks."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"The whole plant gets eaten. Young leaves in salad, flowers brewed into wine, roots roasted and ground as a coffee substitute. It was grown deliberately as a vegetable long before it was treated as a weed."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The name comes from the French dent de lion, lion's tooth, for the jagged edge of the leaf. Not the yellow flower, and not the mane."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, seed-bearing plants after their kind. The flight trick people needed a wind tunnel and a smoke machine to work out is standing in every lawn, on every stem, working perfectly, in something most people bother to notice only when they blow one."}]},{"id":"poppy","name":"Common poppy","group":"flowers","blurb":"A red field flower that only shows up where the ground has been torn open.","size":"Up to 28 in (70 cm) tall, with flowers 2 to 4 in (5–10 cm) across.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":2.3,"life":1},"wonder":"Seed that will sit in the dark doing nothing for decades, waiting for the ground above it to be broken.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The petals sit crumpled inside the bud like a shirt stuffed into a bag. The bud hangs down, splits, and the creased petals push out and are pressed flat by the sun within a day."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seed lasts in soil for decades, doing nothing at all, until a plough or a shell or a digger turns the ground over. Then it comes up everywhere at once, which is why a bare building site turns red.","more":"A seed bank like that is why a poppy field is never a permanent thing. Let the ground settle and grass takes over within a season or two. The poppies have not died out; they have gone back to waiting under the turf for the next time it is broken."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seed capsule works like a pepper pot. A ring of small holes opens under the flat lid, and the wind rocking the dry stalk shakes seed out a little at a time."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In the spring of 1915, poppies came up in their thousands across the churned ground of Flanders. John McCrae, a Canadian army doctor, wrote In Flanders Fields after a friend's burial. Punch magazine printed it later that year.","more":"The poppies were not a sign or a symbol at the time. They were doing what they always do, which is grow on ground that has been ripped open. The shelling had ploughed the whole landscape."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Because of that poem the poppy became the remembrance flower across the Commonwealth, worn each November for soldiers who did not come home."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The leaves and milky sap taste sharp and acrid and are mildly poisonous to grazing animals. Cattle in a field will crop around them."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"This is not the opium poppy. That is a different species, Papaver somniferum, with grey-green hairless leaves and a fat round capsule. The common red field poppy contains no opium.","more":"The two are easy to tell apart once you look. The opium poppy is taller, smooth and blue-grey, with leaves that clasp the stem and a capsule the size of a walnut. The field poppy is bristly all over and its capsule is about the size of a pea."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One plant can throw up as many as 400 flowers across a warm season, one after another, each lasting a day or two."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It is a weed of ploughed land across Europe and follows farming about. Roadworks, new verges, a fallen tree, a field turned over: the poppies arrive the following summer."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody sows them. The best read is that being buried is itself the signal to wait, and that breaking the soil open is the signal to start, because that is the one thing every poppy field has in common."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Four petals, slightly overlapping, usually vivid red with a black blotch at the base, around a middle crowded with dark stamens. The stalk beneath is thin and covered in stiff bristly hairs."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names seed-bearing plants. A poppy seed can lie under a field for a lifetime and still be a working seed when the light finally reaches it. Whatever else it is, it is not fragile."}]},{"id":"lavender","name":"Lavender","group":"flowers","blurb":"A grey-green Mediterranean shrub in the mint family, grown for the oil held in glands on its surface.","size":"About 3 ft (1 m) tall and roughly as wide, with flower spikes on thin stalks above the leaves.","homes":["mountain","backyard"],"stats":{"height":3,"life":15},"wonder":"The smell is not in the petals at all — it is stored in tiny glands on the outside of the plant, waiting to be knocked open.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The name traces back to the Latin lavare, to wash. Romans put lavender in bath water and in the laundry, which is where the whole idea of a clean smell being a lavender smell comes from."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In Roman times lavender flowers sold for 100 denarii a pound. A farm labourer earned roughly that in a month."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Roll a lavender stem between finger and thumb and it is square, not round. Every plant in the mint family has that square stem, and lavender belongs to it along with mint, rosemary, sage and basil."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The oil sits in small glands on the outside of the leaves and stems. Brushing past a plant bursts them, which is why you can smell a lavender hedge from the path without touching a single flower.","more":"The glands are visible under a hand lens: small round heads on tiny stalks, scattered over the leaf and packed thickest on the flower spike. Each one is a sealed bag of oil, and each one bursts under a fingertip."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Chemists have picked about 93 different compounds out of lavender oil. Two of them do most of the smelling: linalyl acetate at 30 to 55 percent, and linalool at 20 to 35 percent."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Mihail Garbuzov and Francis Ratnieks at Sussex planted 13 kinds of lavender side by side and counted the insects on each, over and over. Dutch lavender pulled about four times as many pollinators as English lavender.","more":"That is the sort of thing you can only settle by counting. Both plants look inviting and smell strong. Only the tally showed that one of them was doing several times the work of the other for the bees in a garden."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Bumblebees get through a lavender spike about three times faster than honeybees do. The best read is tongue length: a longer tongue reaches the nectar without the bee having to shove itself into each flower."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Lavender honey is a named single-flower honey, made where the hives sit in lavender country. The dried buds also flavour biscuits, ice cream and shortbread, used sparingly because it goes soapy fast."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"record","text":"Lavandin is a cross between two lavender species, first made by bees carrying pollen between them. It grows bigger flowers and yields two to three times as much oil per field, which is why most cheap lavender oil is lavandin.","more":"Bees made the cross by carrying pollen from one lavender species to another. Growers noticed the offspring were bigger and oilier and started raising them on purpose. Lavandin plants are sterile, so every field of it is grown from cuttings."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"There are 47 lavender species, all from the Mediterranean and the drier parts of the Old World. French growers keep about 20,000 acres (8,000 hectares) of true lavender, and 97 percent of it is one species."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The flowers are tiny. What looks like one purple flower on a stalk is a spike of dozens of small tubes, opening a few at a time from the bottom up."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, before any animal was made. The oil glands are on the outside of every lavender plant, on every leaf and stem, and they were there before there was a single bee in the world to be drawn in by them."}]},{"id":"bird-of-paradise","name":"Bird of paradise flower","group":"flowers","blurb":"A South African plant whose flowers open sideways out of a hard beak and look like the head of a crested bird.","size":"The plant reaches about 6 ft 6 in (2 m), with leaves 10 to 28 in (25–70 cm) long on stalks up to 3 ft (1 m).","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":6},"wonder":"The perch was built into the flower, and it is stiff enough to take the weight of a bird standing on it.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The hard beak-shaped sheath, the spathe, sits at right angles to the stem, which is what makes the whole thing look like a bird's head turned sideways. Flowers come out of the top of it one at a time."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Each flower has three orange sepals standing up like a crest and three blue-purple petals below. Two of the blue petals are fused into an arrow shape, with the pollen hidden inside the arrow and the nectar at its base."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"A bird landing on that blue arrow makes it split open along its length, and the pollen presses onto the bird's feet. Almost nothing else in the plant world moves pollen by foot.","more":"The bird is not trying to help. It is after the nectar pooled at the base of the arrow. The splitting happens because of its weight on the petal, so a bird that is heavy enough gets sugar and leaves with orange feet."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Which bird actually does the work? Sunbirds come constantly, but watchers found they mostly perch on the beak, drink and leave without touching the pollen. Weavers land on the blue arrow itself. The reasoning from the two sets of observations is that the weavers are the real pollinators.","more":"Sunbirds are not doing anything wrong; a nectar thief is just a visitor that takes the reward without paying. Sorting one from the other took people sitting and watching where each bird put its feet, over and over, rather than assuming the obvious answer."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Francis Masson collected it in South Africa and it reached Kew in 1773. Joseph Banks named it Strelitzia after Queen Charlotte, wife of George III and Duchess of Mecklenburg-Strelitz. The second name, reginae, means of the queen."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Grown from seed it takes three to five years to flower, and rarely two. There is no shortcut, which is why the plants are expensive.","more":"Everything about the plant is slow. It grows from a thick clump of roots, adds only a few leaves a season, and flowers best when the pot is so crowded the roots have nowhere left to go."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"In the wild it grows only in the Cape provinces and KwaZulu-Natal in South Africa. Everywhere else it is a plant somebody put there."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"In South Africa it is called the crane flower, after the blue crane. The orange crest, the grey-green beak and the angle of the head are all genuinely there. What is not there is any bird."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"One spathe keeps producing. Flower after flower pushes out of the same beak over several weeks, so the bird appears to grow a fresh crest each time the last one withers."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The leaves are paddle-shaped and leathery, up to 28 in (70 cm) long, and the plant is a relative of the banana. Stood in a corner it looks like a small banana plant that has grown a bird."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three, birds on day five. The perch, the arrow, the nectar and the pollen were all in place before there was anything with feathers to stand on them."}]},{"id":"passionflower","name":"Passionflower","group":"flowers","blurb":"A climbing vine with a flower built in rings, named by missionaries who read the crucifixion in its parts.","size":"Vines climb 20 to 30 ft (6–9 m) on coiling tendrils, with flowers about 3 in (8 cm) across.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"height":30},"wonder":"A plant that grows fake butterfly eggs on its own leaves is doing something no gardener would think to design.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In Rome in 1609 a scholar named Jacomo Bosio was writing about the Cross when a friar back from Mexico showed him drawings of the flower. Bosio read the ring of threads as the crown of thorns, three stigmas as three nails and the tendrils as cords.","more":"Spanish missionaries in the New World had already been calling it the flower of the Passion. Whatever anyone makes of the reading, the parts Bosio was pointing at are genuinely there, in the same arrangement, in every flower in the genus."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The ring of coloured threads inside the flower is called the corona. Some species stack as many as eight rings of it, one inside another, in a single flower."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"There are about 550 species in the genus Passiflora, mostly in the warm parts of the Americas."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"The vine makes nectar outside its flowers as well as inside them, from small glands on the leaf stalks and leaf edges. Ants find the sugar and stay on the plant."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"What the ants are doing there is the interesting part. They patrol the leaves and go for caterpillars. The best read is a straight trade: the plant pays in sugar and gets a standing guard it does not have to grow itself.","more":"You can watch the trade fail. Wash the ants off a passionflower vine and leave the neighbouring vine alone, and the ant-free one gets stripped by caterpillars far faster. That comparison is where the confidence in the reading comes from."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"Some passionflowers grow small coloured bumps on their leaves that match the size, shape and yellow colour of Heliconius butterfly eggs."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"Female Heliconius butterflies inspect a leaf before laying and skip any that already carries eggs, since the caterpillars eat each other. So a leaf wearing fakes gets passed over. That reasoning comes from watching the butterflies, not from the bumps."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Many species carry cyanide-making compounds in their leaves, which makes them mildly poisonous. Heliconius caterpillars eat them anyway, and only them."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Passion fruit is Passiflora edulis. Sweet granadilla is P. ligularis, giant granadilla is P. quadrangularis and the maypop of the southern United States is P. incarnata. All of them come off vines with the same ringed flower."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It climbs on tendrils: thin threads that wave slowly in the air, grab whatever they touch, and then pull tight into a spring-shaped coil that hauls the vine in.","more":"A tendril hunts before it grips. It sweeps a slow circle in the air over hours, and if it touches nothing it eventually gives up and straightens. If it does touch something, it curls, then coils into a spring in the middle so the vine is pulled in with some give left in the line."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the plants. The rings, the threads, the three stigmas and the five-plus-five petals and sepals are in all 550 species. People argued about what the pattern means, but nobody had to invent the pattern."}]},{"id":"snapdragon","name":"Snapdragon","group":"flowers","blurb":"A Mediterranean garden flower with a mouth that snaps shut and a seed pod that dries into a tiny skull.","size":"Usually 1 ft 8 in to 3 ft 3 in (0.5–1 m) tall, with flowers about 1.5 in (4 cm) long.","homes":["mountain","backyard"],"stats":{"height":3},"wonder":"The same plant that gives a child a snapping dragon's mouth in July leaves a row of small skulls on the stalk by autumn.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Pinch the sides of the flower and it gapes open like a jaw. Let go and it snaps shut. That is the whole reason for the name, and it works on every snapdragon in the border."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The scientific name Antirrhinum means roughly like a snout, from the Greek for nose. The flower had been named after an animal's face long before anyone called it a dragon."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The seed capsule is 10 to 14 mm across, about the size of a pea, and dries into the shape of a skull with two hollow eye sockets and an open mouth. Leave the spike standing over winter and it carries a row of them.","more":"The holes are not decoration. The two sockets and the mouth are the pores the seed shakes out of when the wind rocks the dead stalk, the same pepper-pot trick a poppy uses."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"The flower is shut. A bumblebee has to land on the lower lip, force it down and climb bodily inside to reach the nectar, collecting pollen on its back as it goes. Honeybees mostly cannot manage it."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"The best read is that the closed mouth is a weight test. Insects too light to push the lip open never get in, so the nectar goes to the ones big enough to carry a useful load of pollen to the next plant.","more":"Small bees have another way in. Some chew a hole through the base of the flower and drink the nectar from outside without ever opening the mouth or touching the pollen. Look along a row of snapdragons in late summer and you can find the holes."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One capsule holds a great many dust-fine seeds. They are small enough that a shaken seed head looks like it is pouring out grit."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"In the wild it grows on rocky ground in south-central France, the eastern Pyrenees, north-eastern Spain and the Balearic Islands. Everywhere else it is a garden escape."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Snapdragons have been a laboratory plant for about a century. A gene first found in them, called DEFICIENS, supplies the D in MADS-box, the name for a family of genes that decides which flower part grows in which position.","more":"The point of a laboratory plant is that everyone works on the same one, so results can be compared. Snapdragons were picked early because they grow fast, are easy to cross by hand, and throw up odd flowers often enough to show what the genes are doing."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Snapdragons carry jumping genes, bits of DNA that move about inside the plant. One of them, Tam3, moves far more at low temperature, so a cold spring can change the colour pattern that shows up on the petals."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"A snapdragon goes from seed to seed in about four months, which is one reason researchers picked it. You can run through several generations while a tree is still deciding whether to leaf out."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Most reach about 3 ft (1 m), though plants have been recorded at 6 ft 6 in (2 m). The flowers open from the bottom of the spike upward, so one stalk has buds at the top and skulls forming at the base."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, seed after its kind. A snapdragon dies back to nothing and comes up the following year with the same snapping mouth, the same four-month timetable and the same skull-shaped pod on the end of it."}]},{"id":"water-lily","name":"Giant water lily","group":"flowers","blurb":"An Amazon water plant with leaves ten feet across, strong enough to hold a child standing up.","size":"Leaves up to 10 ft (3 m) wide, on stalks 23 to 26 ft (7–8 m) long running down to the mud.","homes":["freshwater","rainforest"],"stats":{"height":23},"wonder":"Turn a leaf over and you are looking at a bridge deck: main girders radiating out, cross-braces between them, the whole thing floating.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Leaves reach 10 ft (3 m) across. The largest measured was 10 ft 6 in (3.2 m), grown at La Rinconada Gardens in Bolivia."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"A leaf tested at Ghent measured 8 ft 6 in (2.6 m) across and weighed 13 lb (6.1 kg) by itself. Loaded up carefully with weights, it carried 498 lb (226 kg) before it gave way.","more":"That is a leaf holding roughly 38 times its own weight while floating. The load has to be spread out, on a board or on bare feet placed carefully, because all the strength is in the rib pattern and none of it is in the thin green skin between the ribs."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Turn one over. Thick ribs radiate out from the centre with cross-braces running between them, and every rib is covered in sharp spines. The rim of the leaf stands up around the edge like the side of a pie dish."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Joseph Paxton grew the first one to flower in Britain in November 1849, then dressed his seven-year-old daughter Annie in a fairy costume and stood her on a leaf. He copied the rib pattern into the Crystal Palace and said so outright.","more":"His own words were that nature had provided the leaf with lengthways and crossways girders and supports, and that he had borrowed them for the building. The Crystal Palace covered 19 acres and went up in a matter of months because of that structure."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flower opens white and fruit-scented on the first night, then closes with beetles shut inside it. The next night it reopens, and by then the petals have turned pink.","more":"The colour change is not a decoration. A white flower and a pink one on the same pond are at different stages, and a beetle leaving a pink one is carrying pollen it can only usefully deliver to a white one. The pond keeps them out of step on purpose."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"The best read on the trapped beetles: they spend the night feeding on starchy tissue inside, get dusted when the pollen sacs drop, and then fly off to a fresh white flower. The timing is the argument — white and scented on night one, pink and loaded on night two."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The flower itself is 16 in (40 cm) across and weighs 3.5 lb (1.6 kg). That is a flower the size of a football and the weight of a bag of sugar."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 2022 botanists at Kew named a third species, Victoria boliviana, the largest of them all. A pressed specimen of it had been sitting mislabelled in Kew's herbarium for 177 years before anyone noticed."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"The spines are all underneath, on the ribs and the stalk, and there are none on the smooth upper surface. The reasoning writes itself: the grazing animals in an Amazon river arrive from below."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It needs warm water, 85 to 90°F (29–32°C), which is why it grows in the shallow backwaters of the Amazon basin and in heated glasshouses everywhere else."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The stalk runs 23 to 26 ft (7–8 m) from the mud at the bottom up to the leaf on the surface. Most of the plant is out of sight underwater."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, before anyone built anything. The girders and cross-braces under that leaf were holding up 498 lb of load long before an engineer looked at one and copied it into a building."}]},{"id":"edelweiss","name":"Edelweiss","group":"flowers","blurb":"A woolly Alpine flower growing on high limestone rock, wrapped in hairs that soak up ultraviolet light.","size":"1 to 8 in (3–20 cm) tall in the wild, reaching 16 in (40 cm) when grown in a garden.","homes":["mountain"],"stats":{"height":1},"wonder":"A plant living where the ultraviolet is fiercest, wearing a filter for it.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The German name is edel, noble, plus weiss, white. The scientific name Leontopodium is Greek for lion's paw, which is what the woolly white star is meant to look like."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The white star is not petals. Those are bracts, which are modified leaves, arranged in a double star around five or six small yellow flower heads. Each of those heads is only about 5 mm across."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Every white surface is packed with dense white hairs, called tomentum. Under a lens the flower looks like it has been rolled in wool."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Researchers peeled a pad of those hairs off a bract and shone light through it. The hairs absorb near-ultraviolet before it reaches the living tissue underneath. They do not simply bounce it away — they take it in.","more":"Inside each filament is a structure at a scale below a thousandth of a millimetre, which is one of very few structures like it known in a plant. That internal pattern is what does the absorbing."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why that matters here: ultraviolet gets stronger the higher you go, and this plant lives at 5,900 to 11,200 ft (1,800–3,400 m). The reasoning is the match between where it grows and what its coat happens to block.","more":"Ultraviolet is what burns skin at altitude even on a cold day, because thinner air filters less of it. A plant that cannot move into shade has to carry its own cover, and this one carries it everywhere the sun can reach."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows on rocky limestone in the Alps, the Pyrenees and the Italian Apennines, between about 5,900 and 11,200 ft (1,800–3,400 m). It flowers July to September and that is the whole season."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"It was picked so heavily that it became rare, and it is now protected in most countries where it grows. Romania declared it a monument of nature in 1931.","more":"The problem was never grazing or weather. It was people climbing for a flower to take home, often from the most exposed and dangerous ledges, because a picked edelweiss was proof of where you had been."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Austria's mountain troops took it as their cap badge in 1907. It is used as a national symbol in Austria, Switzerland, Slovenia, Italy and Bulgaria."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The song Edelweiss is not an old Austrian folk song, whatever people assume. Rodgers and Hammerstein wrote it for the 1959 Broadway musical The Sound of Music."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A wild plant is small — often no taller than a thumb. Grown in a garden at low altitude it stretches to about 16 in (40 cm) and the wool thins out."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three and the sun on day four. This one is built to handle sunlight at its harshest, on bare rock two miles up, and it manages that in a package the size of a thumb."}]},{"id":"hibiscus","name":"Hibiscus","group":"flowers","blurb":"A tropical shrub whose big trumpet flowers open in the morning and are finished by evening.","size":"Flowers 1.5 to 7 in (4–18 cm) across on a shrub that reaches about 15 ft (4.5 m).","homes":["rainforest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":15},"wonder":"The bush spends a whole flower every single day and opens a fresh set the next morning without fail.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Look into any hibiscus and one long column sticks straight out of the throat. All the stamens are fused into that single tube, with the pollen bags stuck along its length and five knobbed stigmas on the tip."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A hibiscus flower opens in the morning and is limp by late afternoon. That is the whole life of it. One species is even called flower-of-an-hour.","more":"A shrub in flower is spending a great deal for a single day's display. The buds are built in advance in a queue along the stem, so as one set collapses the next is already swollen and ready to open at first light."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Depending on the species the flowers run from 1.5 to 7 in (4–18 cm) across. The big ones are wider than an adult's outstretched hand."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The genus is huge — 433 accepted species — and spread across the warm parts of the world, from tropical shrubs to hardy garden bushes to a marsh plant in the eastern United States."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Hibiscus tea is not made from petals. It is brewed from the calyces of roselle, a different species, which are the fleshy crimson cups left holding the seed pod after the flower drops. It tastes sharp, like cranberry.","more":"Roselle is grown as a crop for exactly that part. The petals are thrown away or eaten as a vegetable, and the red cups are dried. The colour in the drink comes from the same family of pigments that make blackcurrants dark."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Several countries and states claim one. Malaysia has Hibiscus rosa-sinensis, South Korea has H. syriacus, Haiti uses one as a national symbol, and Hawaii chose the yellow H. brackenridgei in 1988.","more":"Hawaii picked a native species rather than the familiar red one from the gardens. The yellow hibiscus was made the official state flower in 1988, after decades in which any hibiscus at all had stood in for the islands unofficially."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"That Hawaiian yellow hibiscus, called maʻo hau hele, is endangered in the wild. The flower on every tourist shirt is one of the rarest plants on the islands."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"The column sticks out well past the petals, right where a visitor has to pass. The best read is that nothing gets to the nectar without brushing it — bird, bee or butterfly, the flower does not much mind which."},{"cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"One relative, kenaf, is grown for its fibre and made into paper. The bark of sea hibiscus is twisted into rope. The same family gives us cotton and okra."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The five petals overlap each other in one direction, like the blades of a pinwheel, so the flower looks as though it is turning even when it is still."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, seed-bearing plants after their kind. A hibiscus throws away a fully built flower every evening and has another finished by dawn. Nothing about that is thrifty, and it happens on every bush, all season."}]},{"id":"foxglove","name":"Foxglove","group":"flowers","blurb":"A tall spike of speckled purple bells that is poisonous to eat and, measured out carefully, steadies a failing heart.","size":"3 ft 3 in to 6 ft 6 in (1–2 m) tall in its second year, with bells about 2 in (5 cm) long.","homes":["forest","grassland"],"stats":{"height":6,"life":2},"wonder":"The dose that stops a heart and the dose that steadies one sit close together, and it took a doctor ten years to find the line.","facts":[{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It is a biennial. The first year is a flat rosette of leaves on the ground and nothing else at all. The second year it throws up the spike, flowers, sets seed and dies."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The bells hang along one side of the spike, opening from the bottom upward. Inside each one is a pale floor covered in dark spots ringed with white, like a runway lit at night."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"A bumblebee crawls right inside the tube to reach the nectar at the back. The best read on the spots is that they mark the way in, since bees consistently enter on the spotted side. That is reasoning from watching bees, not from anything the plant said."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seeds are 0.1 to 0.2 mm across, smaller than a grain of salt, and one plant releases enormous numbers of them when the dried capsules split open. That is why a single foxglove becomes a bank of them."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Leaves, flowers and seeds all carry cardiac glycosides, mainly digitoxin. They jam a pump that every animal cell uses to move sodium and potassium in and out. Eating any part of the plant can kill a person.","more":"This is why it matters that a poisonous plant and a medicine can be the same plant. The chemical does one thing: it makes heart muscle squeeze harder. Too little does nothing, and too much makes the heart beat so hard and so wrongly that it stops."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 1775 a doctor named William Withering was asked about a family recipe for dropsy, a swelling caused by a weak heart. It had been kept secret by an old woman in Shropshire and contained twenty or more herbs. He worked out which one was doing the work.","more":"The way he did it matters as much as the answer. He did not guess. He went through the ingredients, tested them, and settled on foxglove because it was the only one whose effect he could reproduce on its own."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Withering then spent ten years testing doses on patients and published An Account of the Foxglove in 1785. Of 158 patients, 101 with heart failure got relief. He wrote it up mainly to warn other doctors off overdosing."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Foxglove is still the source. Digoxin, a heart drug on pharmacy shelves right now, is extracted from woolly foxglove, a different species from the purple garden one. The purple one gives digitoxin instead."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It takes roughly 2,200 lb (1,000 kg) of dried foxglove leaves to yield 2.2 lb (1 kg) of pure digoxin. The drug is still pulled out of plants rather than built from scratch in a factory."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows across temperate Europe and turns up in a woodland clearing the year after the trees come down. Fell a wood or burn a heath and the foxgloves arrive from seed that was already lying there.","more":"The seed was already in the soil, waiting, exactly as poppy seed does. Clear a wood and the foxgloves that appear the following summer were not blown in from anywhere. They were lying under the trees the whole time."},{"cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"The Latin name Digitalis comes from digitus, a finger — the bells are exactly the size to push a fingertip into, which is what most children do with them and precisely what they should not."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the plants. This one holds a chemical strong enough to stop a heart and precise enough to steady one, sitting in a hedge bank where anybody can walk past it."}]},{"id":"bluebell","name":"Bluebell","group":"flowers","blurb":"A woodland bulb that turns British woods solid blue for a few weeks in spring, then disappears underground for the rest of the year.","size":"Up to about 18 in (45 cm) tall, with bells around 0.8 in (2 cm) long hanging down one side of the stem.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"height":1.5},"wonder":"The whole show is squeezed into the few weeks between the end of winter and the moment the trees close over.","facts":[{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"The British Isles hold roughly half of all the native bluebells in the world. It is a western European plant, and Britain is its stronghold — the carpets are genuinely a local speciality."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Native bluebell: the stem droops over at the top, the flowers hang down one side, the tips of the bells curl right back, and the pollen is creamy white. Spanish bluebell: stem upright, flowers all round it, pollen blue-green.","more":"The two cross where gardens meet woods, and the hybrid looks close to the native. That hybrid is the real worry rather than the Spanish plant itself, because it is the one people cannot tell apart and so leave in place."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"They are slow. A bluebell seed takes at least five years to build a bulb big enough to flower. A wood does not gain a bluebell carpet in a hurry."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Because they take that long, a thick bluebell carpet is a clue that a wood is old. It is reasoning from growth rate, and it is used alongside other slow plants rather than as proof on its own."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"The sticky sap had jobs. It was used to glue pages into book bindings and to fix feathers onto arrows. In Elizabethan England the bulbs were crushed to make starch for the stiff ruffs worn round the neck.","more":"None of those uses are decorative. The sap is genuinely gluey, which anyone who has snapped a stem finds out, and before manufactured glues that made it worth collecting. The starch use meant digging up bulbs by the sackful."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Native bluebells are protected in Britain under the Wildlife and Countryside Act of 1981, and an addition in 1998 made trading in wild bluebell bulbs or seed an offence outright."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The bulbs are poisonous, which is one reason a bluebell wood is not grazed flat by deer and rabbits the way other spring growth is."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The timetable is tight. Leaves come up while the trees are still bare, the flowers open, seed sets, and the whole plant has yellowed and gone back underground by early summer."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Why that rush? The best read is light. On a woodland floor, almost all the light there will be arrives in the gap between the end of winter and the trees putting out leaves, and a bluebell spends the entire gap."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The second half of the name, non-scripta, means unlettered. It was chosen to separate this plant from the hyacinth of Greek legend, which was said to have marks written on its petals."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Walking through them does real damage. Crushed leaves cannot feed the bulb, and a trampled path through a bluebell wood stays bare for years afterwards, which is why the good woods have marked routes.","more":"The leaves are the plant's only chance to feed the bulb, and they are flat on the ground where feet land. A bulb that loses its leaves early has nothing stored for the following spring, so a shortcut worn across a wood shows up as a bare stripe long after the walkers have gone."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three, plants before anything else living. A bluebell spends eleven months of the year as a bulb in the dark and one month as a blue floor stretching out of sight under the trees."}]},{"id":"venus-flytrap","name":"Venus flytrap","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A bog plant with hinged leaves that snap shut on insects, and it will not close for a raindrop.","size":"A trap is about 1 in (2.5 cm) long — thumbnail size. The whole plant fits in a teacup, with the flowers held about 6 in (15 cm) above it.","homes":["grassland","freshwater"],"stats":{"height":1},"wonder":"The counting is the part that stops you — a leaf that waits for a second touch, and then waits for a fifth.","facts":[{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Each half of a trap carries three stiff hairs. Touch one and nothing happens. Touch a second — or the same one again — within 20 seconds, and the trap shuts. One touch is a raindrop. Two is something walking."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"The snap takes about one tenth of a second. The leaf is curved like the bottom of a popper toy, and closing flips that curve inside out. The trap does not bend shut so much as buckle.","more":"Measurements published in 2026 found the outer cell walls soften by 30 to 40 percent within about a second of the trigger. That is the fastest change in cell-wall stiffness recorded in any plant."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The counting does not stop at two. Rainer Hedrich's team at Würzburg tripped the hairs by hand and watched what switched on. At five, the digestive glands fire and flood the trap. Keep going and it pours out more."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The rim carries stiff bristles that mesh together like interlaced fingers. For the first stage the trap is only loosely closed — a cage with gaps, not a sealed box. It seals flat afterwards.","more":"Once sealed, the two lobes press against the prey along their whole surface, so the digestive glands are in contact with it everywhere at once rather than at the bottom of a cup."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why the loose cage stage? The best read is that it is a size filter. A tiny ant slips out through the gaps; anything big enough to keep tripping the hairs stays in. The argument is arithmetic — ten days of digestion is not worth spending on an ant."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Digestion takes about ten days. What is left is a husk of chitin, the insect's outer shell, which the plant cannot break down. The trap opens and the husk blows away."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Every wild Venus flytrap on earth grows in wet pine ground around Wilmington, North Carolina. The usual figure quoted is a 75-mile radius; surviving populations are all inside about 56 miles (90 km) of the city. Not Africa, not the Amazon."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"North Carolina made digging up a wild flytrap a felony in 2014. In 2019 one man was charged with 73 counts of it and held on $750,000 bond. Another case involved nearly a thousand poached plants."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Elsa Youngsteadt's team at NC State compared what was caught in the traps with what visited the flowers. The main pollinators — sweat bees and a checkered beetle — turned up in the traps hardly at all. The flowers stand on a long stalk, well above the traps."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Charles Darwin worked on carnivorous plants for years and published Insectivorous Plants in 1875. He called the Venus flytrap one of the most wonderful plants in the world."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week brought the plants — before the sun on day four, and before any animal on day five. A trap with nothing to catch is just a leaf. The equipment came first and the flies came after."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Count what a working trap needs at once: hairs that fire a signal, a leaf curved so it can buckle, a rim of bristles that mesh, and glands that hold off until the fifth touch. Take away the counting and it closes on rain until it starves."}]},{"id":"pitcher-plant","name":"Tropical pitcher plant","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A climbing vine that grows a lidded jug at the tip of every leaf and lets things fall in.","size":"The vines climb to 49 ft (15 m) or more. The record pitcher, on Nepenthes rajah, stood 16 in (41 cm) tall and holds close to a gallon (3.5 litres) of liquid.","homes":["rainforest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":49},"wonder":"The lid, the slick rim, the waxed wall and the fluid are four separate answers to one question, and they arrived together.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The pitcher is not a flower or a fruit. It is the tip of a leaf. The midrib runs out past the blade as a tendril, and the tendril's end swells into a bud that inflates into a lidded jug.","more":"One vine makes two shapes. Squat pitchers sit on the ground near the base; slimmer aerial ones hang in the air once the vine starts climbing. They catch different things."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The record trap was measured on Mount Kinabalu in Sabah, Borneo on 26 March 2011 by Alex Lamb: a Nepenthes rajah pitcher 16 in (41 cm) tall. Guinness lists the species as holding the largest carnivorous plant traps by volume."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The rim is the trap. Wet it and the surface turns into a water slide — insects aquaplane on the film and go over the edge. Inside, the upper wall is coated in loose wax flakes that come away on their feet."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Rajah pitchers have been found with drowned frogs, birds and small mammals in them, rats included. Nothing is hunted. They fall in.","more":"The fluid is far from sterile. Mosquito and midge larvae live in it, spiders hang above it, and mites, ants and even a species of crab have been found in it — feeding on what drowns without being digested themselves."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"High on the mountain, Nepenthes lowii mostly stops catching insects. Its upper pitchers are sized for a tree shrew to sit on, and the underside of the lid is smeared with something sweet. The shrew perches, licks, and does its business into the jug."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Charles Clarke's team traced where the nitrogen in those leaves came from. Between 57 and 100 percent of it came back to shrew droppings, not insects. On that plant the pitcher is not really a trap at all — the sums say it is a lavatory."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Nepenthes hemsleyana in Borneo houses a woolly bat instead. The back wall above the opening is shaped so bat sonar bounces straight back over a wide angle. Researchers enlarged the reflector and more bats came; they cut it down and fewer did."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Nepenthes ampullaria grows crowded clusters of pitchers on the forest floor with the lids swung back out of the way. What mostly lands in them is dead leaves falling from the canopy. It runs largely on compost."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Nepenthes bicalcarata has hollow tendrils, and a carpenter ant, Camponotus schmitzi, nests inside them. The ants swim in the pitcher fluid, drag out prey too big to break down before it rots the trap, and leave their own droppings behind."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"About 170 species, almost all in the Old World tropics — Borneo, Sumatra and the Philippines have the most. Some grow on the top of a single mountain and nowhere else, like Nepenthes attenboroughii on Mount Victoria in Palawan."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Look at what one pitcher needs at the same moment: a lid so rain does not dilute it, a rim slick only when wet, a waxed wall, and fluid that digests a rat but not the midge larvae swimming in it. Any one alone is a leaf with a problem."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants were made on day three, bearing seed after their kind. Nepenthes still does exactly that. About 170 species, and every one grows its jug the same way, from the tip of a leaf, out of a tendril."}]},{"id":"sundew","name":"Sundew","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A leaf covered in stalks, each holding a bead of glue that looks like dew and does not dry out in the sun.","size":"Most sundews are coasters on the ground, under 2 in (5 cm) across. Drosera erythrogyne, in Western Australia, climbs to about 10 ft (3 m).","homes":["freshwater","grassland"],"stats":{"height":10},"wonder":"Glue that stays wet in full sun is a harder thing to make than a snapping jaw, and it is the part nobody notices.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Every stalk on the leaf ends in a bead of clear glue. It catches the light like a dewdrop, which is where the name comes from — and unlike dew it is still sitting there at noon in full sun."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Once an insect sticks, the neighbouring stalks bend over and press onto it, adding their own glue. Drosera capensis goes further and wraps the whole leaf right around the prey in about 30 minutes.","more":"Nothing is carried anywhere. The insect is digested where it landed, on the open face of the leaf, and the leaf absorbs the meal straight through its surface before unrolling again."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Drosera glanduligera, from southern Australia, does not wait. Long outer stalks flick forward in 75 thousandths of a second and catapult the insect into the sticky middle of the leaf. That is faster than a Venus flytrap closes.","more":"Simon Poppinga and Thomas Speck's team at Freiburg filmed the snap-tentacles and published them in 2012. Guinness now lists the species as the fastest predatory land plant. The stalks appear to fire by dumping stored water pressure all at once, and each one works only once."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Darwin tested what the stalks actually respond to. Bits of glass, hair and sand got a weak reaction or none. Meat and insects got a strong one. The leaf is answering chemistry, not just weight."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Sundews grow in bogs, where the ground hands over almost no nitrogen. The best read on why a plant would build glue traps at all is that the insects are the fertiliser. The soil cannot supply it, so the leaf goes and gets it."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"worked","text":"A trapped fly is usually dead within about 15 minutes, which is quicker than digestion could manage. The usual read is suffocation — the glue plugs the row of tiny holes along an insect's sides that it breathes through."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Darwin spent years on sundews and gave them the bulk of Insectivorous Plants in 1875. In a letter in 1860 he wrote that he cared more about Drosera than about almost anything else he was working on."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"There are at least 194 species of sundew, on every continent except Antarctica. About half of them are Australian."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Not all of them sit flat. Drosera erythrogyne, from Western Australia, scrambles up through other plants to about 10 ft (3 m), trailing sticky leaves the whole way."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flowers open on a bare stalk held well clear of the sticky leaves — the plant keeps its pollinators and its dinner at different heights."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"The glue has to be sticky, clear enough to let light through, and able to stay wet in desert-grade sun. The stalks have to bend the right way. The enzymes have to arrive after the catch, not before. Glue with no enzymes just holds a corpse."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three, three days before any animal. A sundew is a plant first and a catcher second — roots, chlorophyll, its own sugar made from light. The glue is an extra bolted onto a plant that already works."}]},{"id":"bladderwort","name":"Bladderwort","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A rootless water plant strung with tiny sprung bladders that swallow a water flea faster than anything else in the plant world.","size":"The traps run from about 1/100 in to 1/2 in (0.2–12 mm). Most are pinhead-sized, and you need a lens to watch one work.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Half a thousandth of a second, in a plant with no muscles, no nerves and no roots.","facts":[{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"High-speed cameras at Freiburg clocked the trapdoor buckling open in about 0.5 milliseconds, with the whole catch finished inside that same half-millisecond. It is the fastest movement known in any plant — the Venus flytrap, at 100 milliseconds, is two hundred times slower."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is a vacuum, not a mouth. The plant pumps water out of the sealed bladder until the walls cave inward and the door is held shut against the pressure difference. Brush a trigger hair on the door and the seal lets go.","more":"The door does not swing on a hinge. It is curved, and the touch flips the curve inside out — the same buckling trick the Venus flytrap uses, at a thousandth of the size and two hundred times the speed."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Water rushing into a sprung bladder accelerates at up to 600 times gravity and reaches about 5 ft per second (1.5 m/s). A water flea sitting in front of the door gets no vote in the matter."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Then it resets itself. The bladder pumps the water back out over roughly 15 to 30 minutes and is armed again. No help, no repair, just pumping."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Bladderworts have no roots at all — and their parts are not cleanly sorted into stems and leaves either. The whole plant is a tangle of green threads with bladders strung along them."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Water species take water fleas, roundworms, mosquito larvae, tadpoles and newly hatched fish. The ones in wet soil take protozoa and rotifers — prey you need a microscope to see at all.","more":"The traps come in a range of sizes to match, from about 1/100 in to 1/2 in (0.2–12 mm). A single plant carries hundreds of them along its threads, all cocked at once."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Utricularia is the largest group of carnivorous plants there is: about 233 species, on every continent except Antarctica."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Utricularia gibba carries one of the smallest genomes of any flowering plant, around 80 megabases of DNA. Short instruction book, and it still builds the fastest trap in the plant world."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Most of the plant is invisible. From a pond bank you see yellow flowers shaped a little like snapdragons standing on stalks above the water, and nothing else. The traps are all below the surface."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"The trigger hairs are levers, not nerves. The best read is that a push on a hair twists the door's edge just far enough to break the buckled seal — no signal travels anywhere. That is why it works in half a millisecond. Nothing has to be decided."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Nothing about a bladder works in stages. Pump out the water without a door and you get a collapsed bag. Add a door that does not seal and it fills straight back up. Add a seal with no trigger and it never opens. It catches nothing until every piece is present."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week made the plants. The swarming things of the waters came on day five. The bladderwort is a plant that lives in the water and eats what swims there — made first, fed second."}]},{"id":"cobra-lily","name":"Cobra lily","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A pitcher plant from northern California and Oregon, shaped like a rearing snake, with a hood full of skylights.","size":"The hooded tubes usually reach knee height, and the tallest stand about 3 ft (1 m), with a two-pronged tongue hanging under the opening.","homes":["mountain","freshwater"],"stats":{"height":3},"wonder":"It does not fool an insect with a lie. It fools it with the truth about light.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The hood is peppered with translucent patches — real windows, letting daylight through. An insect inside flies at the bright spots, hits leaf, and drops back. The one genuine exit is a small hole tucked under the curled hood, in shadow."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Below the opening the tube is lined with hairs, every one of them pointing down. Walking in is easy. Walking back up is not."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It does not collect rainwater. The hood curls right over the mouth so rain runs off, and the plant fills the tube itself, pumping water up from the roots and letting it out inside. The water level is the plant's decision.","more":"Other pitcher plants leave the top open and take whatever falls in, which means a wet week dilutes their fluid to nothing. This one never has that problem, and never overflows either."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"For a long time the line was that Darlingtonia makes no digestive enzymes at all and leaves the work to bacteria and midge larvae living in the fluid. That is now known to be partly wrong — it does secrete at least one protein-cutting enzyme. The lodgers still do most of it."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"William Brackenridge found it at Mount Shasta in northern California in 1841, on the Wilkes Expedition. John Torrey described it in 1853 and named the genus after a Philadelphia botanist, William Darlington."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows only in northern California and southwest Oregon, and almost always over serpentine rock — ground so short of the usual nutrients and so loaded with metals that most plants will not grow on it at all.","more":"It also needs running water at its feet. In the wild the pitchers stand in full sun while cold spring water moves through the ground around the roots. Growers who let the roots warm up lose the plant."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"The forked appendage under the mouth gets called fangs, looks like a snake's tongue, and carries nectar. It was assumed to be essential bait — but when researchers cut the tongue off, the pitchers caught about as much as before."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It spreads sideways as much as by seed, pushing out long runners called stolons in late winter and spring. A patch of cobra lilies is often one plant that walked."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"Why fill the roof with windows? The best read is plain geometry. A flying insect steers for light. Put false skylights across the whole hood and keep the real exit in shade, and the insect spends everything it has on the wrong ones."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The trap is a one-way tube with no moving parts. Nothing snaps, nothing grabs, nothing closes. Every insect that dies in one walked in under its own power and could not find the door."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Windows, downward hairs, a hidden exit, a nectar tongue and a water supply pumped up from below. Alone, each is useless — a hood full of windows over an open cup is just a bright cup."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three. This one grows where almost nothing else will — bare serpentine, metal-heavy and starved of nitrogen — and solves the problem with a jug instead of with better roots."}]},{"id":"butterwort","name":"Butterwort","group":"carnivores","blurb":"A flat rosette of leaves that feel buttery to the touch, and that greasy film is the trap.","size":"Most rosettes are palm-sized or smaller and sit flat against wet ground, with a single flower on a bare stalk above.","homes":["freshwater","mountain"],"stats":{},"wonder":"The quietest carnivorous plant there is — no snap, no jug, no bristles. Just a leaf you would call slippery.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The leaf is covered in tiny stalked glands, each carrying a droplet of clear slime. That is what makes it feel greasy. A small fly lands and cannot get purchase, because the entire surface is glue."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A second kind of gland lies flat against the leaf and does nothing until something is caught. Then it releases enzymes into the puddle, and the leaf absorbs the meal straight through its own surface."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Some species roll the leaf edge inward around a catch. It is not a snap — it is a curl over hours, which brings more glands into contact with the prey."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The name is Latin for 'little greasy one', from pinguis, meaning fat. Conrad Gesner coined it in 1561 after noticing how the leaves glisten."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"In Norway and Sweden the leaves were used to set milk. Pour warm milk over butterwort leaves and it thickens into tjukkmjølk in Norway, filmjölk in Sweden — a farmhouse dairy product started with a carnivorous plant.","more":"Scandinavian country names for the plant keep pointing at dairy for the same reason. Much of the actual thickening is done by bacteria carried on the leaves rather than by the plant's own enzymes, but the leaves are what you use."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Butterworts want the opposite of what most carnivorous plants want: wet ground that is alkaline. Limestone country, dripping cliffs, seeping rock. There are 126 accepted species, and most of them are in Mexico and Central America."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Temperate butterworts spend winter as a tight bud of scaly leaves called a hibernaculum, with no traps on it at all. In spring the sticky leaves come back.","more":"Tropical species, which do not get a hard winter, keep their catching leaves going all year round. The bud is a response to the cold, not part of the plan for eating."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The catch is small — fungus gnats, springtails, the odd mosquito. Nothing strong is ever held. Indoor growers keep butterworts next to their houseplants precisely because they clear out gnats."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"There is no signal and no muscle here. The glue does the holding, the flat glands do the digesting, and the slow leaf curl is growth rather than movement. The best read is that this is the cheapest carnivorous plant to run, and it works."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flowers stand on bare stalks well above the leaves and look like small violets — two lobes up, three down, with a spur behind. Nothing about them is sticky."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Two gland types on one leaf: one that catches, one that waits and digests. Neither is any use alone. Glue by itself holds a corpse you cannot eat; enzymes by themselves spill onto a leaf with nothing on it."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three named the plants, yielding seed after their kind. A butterwort still does all the ordinary plant things — roots, leaves, flowers, seed. The greasy film is an addition to a working plant, not a replacement for one."}]},{"id":"saguaro","name":"Saguaro cactus","group":"desert","blurb":"The tall branching cactus of the Sonoran Desert — the one on every postcard, and it grows nowhere else.","size":"A big one stands 40 to 52 ft (12–16 m), higher than a two-storey house, and can weigh 4,800 lb (2,200 kg) full of water — about as much as a pickup truck.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"height":52,"life":175,"weight":4800},"wonder":"Seventy-five years of standing still before it lifts an arm. Nothing about it is in a hurry, and nothing about it is unfinished.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Saguaros start slowly and stay slow. Measured against height: about half a foot at nine years old, one foot at thirteen, five feet at twenty-seven, ten feet at forty-one, twenty feet at eighty-three."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The first arm usually does not appear until the plant is 75 to 100 years old, and some never grow one. An armless saguaro is not a young saguaro — the tallest ever measured, 78 ft (23.8 m) near Cave Creek, Arizona, had no arms at all.","more":"That one was blown down in a windstorm in 1986. The tallest known living saguaro, in Maricopa County, stands 45 ft 3 in (13.8 m) with a trunk 10 ft 2 in around, and is reckoned at about 200 years old."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The trunk is pleated like an accordion. After rain the pleats open and the cactus swells; through a dry spell they close again. Its weight swings by hundreds of pounds between a soaking and a drought, and the skin never splits."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Inside is a ring of woody ribs as long as the cactus itself. When a saguaro dies the flesh rots away and the ribs stand there like a bundle of poles. Dry saguaro rib weighs about 27 lb per cubic foot — lighter than most timber."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The roots are a shallow mat, not a deep drill. They spread out as far as the cactus is tall — up to 100 ft (30 m) across — while the main taproot goes down only about 3 ft (1 m). It is built to grab a downpour before it drains away."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flowers open well after sunset and shut by mid-afternoon. Under 24 hours, once, and that is the whole life of a saguaro flower. Bees work them by day; the lesser long-nosed bat works them at night.","more":"The white saguaro blossom has been Arizona's state flower since 16 March 1931 — a state flower that is only properly open in the dark and the early morning."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Each fruit holds around 2,000 seeds and ripens in June. Almost none of them become a cactus. A seedling needs shade — usually under a palo verde or a bursage — or the sun kills it in its first summer."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Gila woodpeckers drill nest holes straight into the trunk. The cactus seals the wound with a hard, cork-like shell of callus, and that shell — a saguaro boot — outlasts the plant itself. People used to pull them out of dead saguaros and use them as containers.","more":"The woodpeckers usually leave a fresh hole alone for months while the walls dry hard, then move in. After they move out, elf owls — the smallest owls in the world — take the empty boot."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"The Tohono O'odham harvest saguaro fruit with a kuibit: a pole about 20 ft (6 m) long, made of saguaro ribs lashed together. Nine pounds of fruit boils down to roughly a quart of syrup. One 1929 harvest brought in 99,000 lb across 600 families."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Arizona protects it by law. Move a saguaro over 4 ft tall and you need a permit, a tag and a seal from the state — even one growing on your own land. Damage a valuable one and it can be charged as a class four felony."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"The whole design answers one problem: rain that comes all at once and then not at all. Shallow roots to catch it, pleats to swell, ribs to hold the swelling up, waxed skin to keep it in. Remove any one and the other three are wasted."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week made the plants, the day before the sun. The saguaro is a fair emblem of a made thing taking its time: nine years to reach half a foot, seventy-five before it lifts an arm, and two centuries standing."}]},{"id":"welwitschia","name":"Welwitschia","group":"desert","blurb":"A Namib Desert plant that grows exactly two leaves when it sprouts and never grows another one.","size":"The largest measured plant is about 6 ft (1.8 m) tall and 28 ft (8.7 m) across — a sprawling mound of leaf, all of it two leaves.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"height":6,"life":1500},"wonder":"Two leaves, held for centuries, in a place with no rain. It is the fewest parts anything that size gets away with.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Two leaves. That is the total, for the whole life of the plant. They appear when it germinates, they never stop growing, and they are never replaced. What looks like a tangle of many leaves is those same two, split and frayed by wind.","more":"Afrikaans speakers call it tweeblaarkanniedood — 'two leaves, cannot die'. Welwitsch himself was told the local name was tumbo."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Because they never stop and never drop, the leaves get long. Mature plants add roughly 3 to 6 in (8–15 cm) a year, measured at about a third of a millimetre a day. The tips die and shred while the base keeps pushing new leaf out."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The largest known plant, in the Messum Mountains of Namibia, stands about 6 ft (1.8 m) high and sprawls 28 ft (8.7 m) wide."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Friedrich Welwitsch came across it in the Namib in 1859 and wrote that he could do nothing but kneel down and stare, half afraid that touching it would prove it a figment of his imagination.","more":"He wanted to call the genus Tumboa. Joseph Hooker in London, who wrote the formal description, asked leave to name it Welwitschia after him instead. The species name, mirabilis, simply means marvellous."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows in a strip about 620 miles (1,000 km) long, running up the coast from the Kuiseb River in Namibia into southern Angola, and it is seldom found more than 60 to 90 miles (100–150 km) inland."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Where does the water come from? Fog rolling off the Atlantic is worth perhaps 2 in (50 mm) a year and the leaves do take it up, but the sums say that is not enough on its own. A long taproot reaching groundwater is the rest of the answer."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"There are male plants and female plants, and no plant is both. Cones grow on stalks out of the crown between the two leaves; the female cones are larger, and the seeds carry papery wings."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Something has to carry pollen from one plant to the other. Insects are the obvious candidates and a wasp is the current best guess, but nobody has actually settled it. That is a strange gap in the record for a plant this famous."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Carbon dating puts the average plant at 500 to 600 years old, and the biggest at around 2,000. Nothing about a welwitschia is fast, including dying."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"record","text":"Namibia protects it by law. Digging one up is illegal. It is not classed as endangered or even rare — it is simply impossible to replace at any useful speed."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Two leaves is not a shortfall. A growth zone at the base that never switches off, leaf tough enough to be shredded for centuries and keep working, pores that open for fog — that is a complete answer to a coast without rain, built from the smallest possible parts list."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week made plants bearing seed after their kind. This one bears its seed in cones, off two leaves it has had since it sprouted, and has never needed a third."}]},{"id":"living-stones","name":"Living stones","group":"desert","blurb":"A pair of fat leaves that look exactly like the pebbles around them, with a window in the top to let the light in.","size":"A whole plant is about 1 in (2.5 cm) across and sits almost flush with the ground. You can walk across a colony and never see one.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{},"wonder":"A plant that hides underground and still gets the light it needs, through a window it grew for the purpose.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A Lithops is two leaves and almost nothing else. They are fused into a squat cone with a split across the top, and there is hardly any stem at all. Most of the plant is below the surface."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The top face is a window. It is translucent, partly or completely, and light passes through it to green tissue lining the inside of the buried leaves. The plant does its photosynthesis underground.","more":"The green is on the inner wall, not the outside. That is backwards for a plant, and it is the whole reason a Lithops can afford to look like a stone: the colour that matters is hidden where nothing has to see it."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"The windows are patterned — creams, greys, browns, dark blotches, fine red lines — and the pattern matches the gravel each population grows in. Different valleys, different pebbles, different markings."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"William Burchell picked one up in the Northern Cape on 14 September 1811, thinking it was a curiously shaped pebble off the stony ground. It turned out to be a plant. That was the first Lithops anyone described."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It replaces itself once a year. Through winter a new pair of leaves grows inside the old pair, feeding off them, and in spring the old pair splits and dries into papery skins around the new one.","more":"Growers learn this the hard way. Water a Lithops in spring while the old leaves are still being drained, and the new pair swells too fast and the plant splits open."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A flower comes out of the split, usually in autumn, and it is often wider than the whole plant. Yellow or white, one per leaf pair."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"They grow in Namibia and South Africa, with a little into Botswana, from sea level right up into the mountains — always in stony ground where there is more rock than soil."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why look like a stone? The best read is that it is about not being eaten. Where a fat, water-filled leaf is the wettest thing for miles, being indistinguishable from gravel is worth more than any spine could be."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"You cannot judge a Lithops from the top. Most of the leaf column is below ground. Lift one out and it is a fat carrot with a pebble on the end."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Buried leaves plus a window solves two problems with one design. Sunk in gravel the plant stays cool and out of sight, and the window still delivers light to the chlorophyll. A green plant sitting on top of that ground would cook."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"A window is no use without green tissue underneath it, and buried green tissue is no use without a window. Neither half is a plant on its own."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three: the earth brought forth grass, and herb yielding seed after his kind. Lithops does exactly that in about the least likely place there is — bare stone flats — and it does it by looking like the stone."}]},{"id":"resurrection-plant","name":"Resurrection plant","group":"desert","blurb":"A Chihuahuan Desert plant that curls into a dead-looking brown ball, waits years, and opens green within hours of getting wet.","size":"A dry ball is about fist-sized. Wet, it opens out flat into a green rosette a hand's width across.","homes":["desert"],"stats":{},"wonder":"It does not survive drying out by avoiding it. It dries out completely and comes back, which is a harder thing.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is a spikemoss — not a flowering plant and not a moss either. It has no flowers and no seeds. It spreads by spores, the way a fern does."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"It can lose about 95 percent of its water and live. Dried right down it holds around 3 percent of its wet mass, and simply stops, for years at a stretch, with no damage done."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Put a dry ball in a saucer of water and it starts opening within a few hours. The green comes back and it goes back to making sugar from light. It does not even need to be rooted in anything — a loose ball on a plate will do it."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The outer stems and inner stems curl differently. The outer ones snap into tight rings quickly; the inner ones wind slowly into spirals, because the strain runs unevenly along their length. That is why the ball closes in the order it does."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The trick is a sugar. As it dries it makes trehalose, which crystallises and takes the place of the water in its tissues, so the cell contents never dry to powder or turn too salty to work."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows in the Chihuahuan Desert, across northern Mexico and into the American Southwest, on rock and thin soil where it may go a whole season with no rain at all."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"A dried ball is light and round, and the wind moves it. If it fetches up somewhere damp it opens there and can take root. The plant travels as a ball and unpacks on arrival."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"It is sold as 'rose of Jericho', but two different plants carry that name. The true rose of Jericho is Anastatica hierochuntica, from the Middle East and North Africa — a small member of the mustard family, under 6 in (15 cm) tall, with tiny white flowers.","more":"Anastatica's seeds have a sticky coat that grips wet soil the moment the ball opens, so they stay where the water is instead of blowing on across the desert."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"And the true one does not actually come back to life. Anastatica's tissue is dead when it dries; the ball opens mechanically, by soaking up water, to release its seeds. Only the Mexican spikemoss turns green again.","more":"From the outside the two look like the same trick — curl up dry, open when wet. Only one of them is alive in between. If a shop sells you a resurrection plant that greens up, it is Selaginella."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Nothing eats a dry one, and the best read is that there is nothing in it worth eating. No water, no soft tissue, and the colour and texture of dead twigs. Being worthless for months at a time is its whole defence."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Drying out kills almost every plant, and the reason is mechanical — cells collapse and cannot be rebuilt. Coming back needs a substitute for water made in advance, a curling order that protects the middle, and a restart that still works after years. That is a system, not a knack."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three of the creation week, and in that account rain is not mentioned yet at all. This one can wait out the absence of rain for years and start again in an afternoon."}]},{"id":"agave","name":"Century plant","group":"desert","blurb":"A rosette of grey spiked leaves that spends its whole life saving up for one enormous flower stalk, then dies.","size":"The rosette spreads 6 to 10 ft (1.8–3 m), with leaves 3 to 5 ft (0.9–1.5 m) long. The flower stalk goes up 25 to 30 ft (8–9 m).","homes":["desert","backyard"],"stats":{"height":30,"life":30},"wonder":"Twenty years of saving, one month of spending, and the pups are already in the ground before the stalk goes up.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The name is wrong. A century plant does not take a century. It usually flowers somewhere between 10 and 30 years old, and in a warm garden with water it can be sooner than that."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"When it goes, it goes fast. The flower stalk has been measured growing 6 to 9 in a day, sometimes close to a foot, until it stands 25 to 30 ft (8–9 m) high — a stalk taller than a house, out of a plant that was knee-high."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It flowers once and dies. Everything the rosette stored for decades goes into that single stalk of yellow blossom, and when the seed is set the plant is finished.","more":"Before it dies it throws out shoots from the base — pups. The rosette that flowered is gone; the clone beside it carries on. A century plant in an old garden is often the third or fourth of its line in the same spot."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Cut the stalk before it flowers and the plant keeps pumping sugar into the stump. The sweet liquid that pools there is aguamiel — honey water — and fermented it becomes pulque, made in Mexico long before the Spanish arrived.","more":"The plant is not fooled for long. It goes on delivering sugar to a stalk that is no longer there, for weeks, and is drained dry by the end of it. The harvest kills the rosette just as surely as flowering would have."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Tequila is not made from this one. Tequila comes from a different agave, Agave tequilana, the blue agave. Century plant gives pulque and other mezcal; blue agave gives tequila."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"The leaves give a fibre called pita, stripped out and twisted into rope, netting, sacks, matting and coarse cloth. Sisal, from a related agave, is the same idea run at industrial scale."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Each leaf ends in a hard spine you would not want to walk into, with more teeth down the edges. The sap is worse — it can cause severe allergic dermatitis, and trimming a big agave bare-handed makes for a genuinely bad afternoon."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It is not native to most of the places it grows. Agave americana comes from Mexico and Texas, and has been planted so widely around the Mediterranean, Australia and southern Africa that people assume it is local."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Why save for twenty years and spend it in one go? The best read is arithmetic. In dry ground you cannot afford a flower stalk every year, but you can afford one enormous one if you save for it. The pups make sure the gamble is not total."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The rosette is a spiral, and the leaves press hard against each other as they grow. Unfold an old leaf and it carries a clean pattern of ridges — the imprint of the leaves that were folded against it years earlier."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"One flowering, one stalk, and a set of pups already rooted before it happens. Get the order wrong — flower before the pups, or pup and never flower — and the plant is a dead end. The timing is the design."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three: the herb yielding seed after his kind. Twenty years of storing and one month of stalk, and at the end of all of it, seed."}]},{"id":"barrel-cactus","name":"Barrel cactus","group":"desert","blurb":"A squat, ribbed cactus armed with hooked spines, and it leans towards the sun as it ages.","size":"A fishhook barrel usually stands 3 to 6 ft (0.9–1.8 m) tall and about 2 ft 3 in (0.7 m) across. Big ones reach 10 ft (3 m).","homes":["desert"],"stats":{"height":10,"life":100},"wonder":"A plant with no leaves at all, still doing the one thing plants are for — turning light into food.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Young barrels are smooth columns. The ribs form as they get older, and only then does the plant fill out into the barrel shape it is named for."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The spines are hooked, like fish hooks, and that is where the fishhook barrel gets its name. Thick, stiff, and curved back on themselves."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Why hooks? One reading is transport. In a flash flood a hooked barrel snags on floating debris, gets pulled loose and carried, then dropped where the water slows — which is exactly where water collects. It is a plausible read, not a filmed event."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Mature fishhook barrels lean, and they lean south or southwest. Desert hikers call it the compass barrel. The lean is consistent enough to take a rough bearing from."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"The reason worked out for the lean is uneven growth. The side facing the hard afternoon sun gets cooked and grows more slowly; the shaded side keeps going. Over years the faster side pushes the top over towards the slower one."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The cut-open-a-cactus-for-water scene is wrong, and dangerously so. Barrel pulp does hold liquid, but it is alkaline and loaded with oxalic acid. Seri people of the Sonoran Desert who tried it reported headaches, pain and diarrhoea.","more":"Drunk on an empty stomach it can bring on vomiting and severe muscle cramps, which in a desert costs you more water than the cactus gave you. It is an emergency measure that mostly makes the emergency worse."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Many barrel cacti are ant plants. They ooze nectar from glands set above each cluster of spines — not from the flower, from the body of the cactus — and ants set up on the plant and stay there.","more":"Nectar outside a flower is called extrafloral nectar, and it is not paying for pollination. The reading is that it is paying for a garrison: the ants get fed, and anything chewing on the cactus gets swarmed."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Flowers come out in a ring right at the top in summer: yellow, orange, red or purple depending on the species, often with a darker stripe down each petal. The fruit that follows is yellow and can sit on the plant for over a year."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Like the saguaro it is pleated, and the ribs let it swell after rain and shrink through the dry without splitting its skin. A barrel cactus a week after a storm is visibly fatter than it was."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It is in no rush. A fishhook barrel runs 50 to 100 years from seed to death, and spends most of that as a fairly boring green cylinder sitting in the sun."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Ribs to swell, wax to hold the water in, spines for shade and defence, nectar to keep ants on guard, roots spread wide for one downpour a year. Five answers to the same question, all present in one plant at once."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three brought forth plants of every kind. This one is the plainest possible proof that 'plant' does not have to mean green and leafy. No leaves at all, a barrel of water, and it still makes its own food out of light."}]},{"id":"prickly-pear","name":"Prickly pear","group":"desert","blurb":"A cactus built out of flat pads, with edible fruit, edible pads, and hairs that get into your skin and stay there.","size":"Pads run from hand-sized to dinner-plate size, and a clump can pile up well above head height.","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Armoured everywhere except the one place it wants an animal to help itself — the fruit.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The flat green pads are not leaves. They are stems — the plant's trunk and branches, flattened out. Botanists call them cladodes."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The big spines are the least of it. Around each spine cluster sit glochids: fine barbed hairs that come off the plant on contact and lodge in skin. They are hard to see and much harder to get out.","more":"Get them in your fingers and they work inward rather than out. Tape or glue lifts them better than tweezers. Swallowed, they irritate the throat — which is why prickly pear fruit has to be scrubbed or singed clean before anyone eats it."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Both halves are food. The fruit is the tuna — red, wine-red, green or orange — and the young pads are nopales, sold by the sackful in Mexico and eaten in tacos and with eggs."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Mexico's coat of arms is a golden eagle standing on a prickly pear with a rattlesnake in its beak, from the Aztec account of the founding of Tenochtitlan. The cactus is on the national flag."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"record","text":"A scale insect that lives on prickly pear pads, Dactylopius coccus, is packed with carminic acid — the deepest red dye anyone had. Spain held a near-monopoly on it for close to 300 years, and it became Mexico's second most valuable export after silver.","more":"It takes about 70,000 dried insects to make a pound of dye. Cochineal is still in use — check the label on pink yoghurt and red lipstick. Peru is now the biggest producer, ahead of Mexico."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Prickly pear taken to Australia got out of hand. By the 1920s it had covered about 60 million acres (24 million hectares) of New South Wales and Queensland and made the land useless. Farms were simply walked away from."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The fix was a moth. Eggs of Cactoblastis cactorum came from Argentina in 1925 and the first field release was in 1926; the caterpillars bore straight into the pads. By 1933 about 80 percent of the infested Queensland land was clear.","more":"Two earlier attempts to breed the moth had failed, so 3,000 eggs were imported and split between Brisbane and a station at Chinchilla, Queensland. At its peak Chinchilla was posting out as many as 14 million eggs a day. In 1936 the district built the Cactoblastis Memorial Hall — a public hall named after a moth."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"They are not all desert plants. Opuntia fragilis grows in northeastern British Columbia at 56 degrees north — a cactus that sits out Canadian winters."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A pad knocked off and left lying on the ground will root where it falls and become a new plant. That is much of why it went across Australia so fast: one pad carried on a cart wheel or a cow's hide moves a whole cactus."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Flat pads and round barrels are two answers to the same desert. Turned edge-on to the midday sun a pad takes far less heat than a cylinder does — but it also stores less water. The read is that a pad trades storage for a big cheap solar panel."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Spines, glochids, waxed skin, water held in the stem, roots that spread instead of dig — and on top of all that a sweet fruit any animal is welcome to carry off and plant. Defended everywhere except exactly where it wants to be eaten."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week named the fruit tree yielding fruit, whose seed is in itself. This is a fruiting plant with no trunk, no leaves and no branches in any ordinary sense — and it still yields fruit, with the seed inside it."}]},{"id":"aloe","name":"Aloe vera","group":"desert","blurb":"A succulent with thick spiky leaves full of clear gel, grown on windowsills worldwide and wild in almost nowhere.","size":"The plant stands about 2 to 3 ft (60–100 cm) tall, with a flower spike up to 3 ft (90 cm) above it in summer.","homes":["desert","backyard"],"stats":{"height":3},"wonder":"Everything valuable in the leaf sits behind three separate defences, and the outermost one is bitterness.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is not a cactus. Aloe vera is a succulent in the family Asphodelaceae — closer kin to a daylily than to anything with spines and areoles. Cacti are almost all American; aloes are African and Arabian."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"For a plant on this many windowsills, the wild home is oddly small: the southeastern Arabian Peninsula, in the Hajar Mountains of northeastern Oman and the eastern United Arab Emirates. That may be the only genuinely wild population left.","more":"Olwen Grace at Kew and Nina Rønsted in Copenhagen led the 2015 study that pinned it down, using the largest genetic sample of the aloes anyone had assembled. Until then nobody could say where the world's most-grown medicinal plant actually came from."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Cut a leaf across and you get two substances, not one. In the middle is the clear gel. Just under the green skin is a thin layer of bitter yellow sap, the latex. They are different things and they do different things."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The yellow latex holds aloin, and aloin is a powerful laxative. It was sold over the counter in the United States until 2002, when the FDA pulled it for want of safety data. It also stains hands and clothes.","more":"Gut bacteria turn aloin into aloe-emodin, which is what actually irritates the intestine. This is why 'aloe juice' from a shop is filtered and 'whole leaf' aloe eaten straight off a windowsill plant is a bad idea."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"The gel is the part that goes on burns. The honest position is that the evidence is mixed — some trials find it helps wounds and burns, some do not. It is a real substance with a real effect on skin, and also the most oversold plant in the shop."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Aloe turns up in the written record early. It is illustrated in the Juliana Anicia Codex, a Greek herbal copied out in the year 512, and it was already an old and standard medicine by the time that book was made."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It flowers in summer on a spike up to 3 ft (90 cm) tall, hung with tubular yellow flowers. Almost every aloe vera you can buy, though, was grown from a pup cut off a parent plant rather than from seed."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"It escapes. Aloe vera has naturalised across North Africa, the Canary Islands, parts of southern Europe and well beyond, and is listed as an invasive species in a good many countries."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The gel is mostly water held in a mesh of long sugar molecules. The best read on why a desert plant carries water as gel rather than liquid is straightforward: gel does not slosh, does not run out of a wound, and does not evaporate through a cut nearly as fast."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Snap a leaf and the bitter yellow latex runs out first, at the cut, before any gel appears. Growers stand a cut leaf upright and let it drain for that exact reason, so the latex is gone before they scoop."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"A leaf that is a water tank, skin thick enough to hold it, a bitter layer just inside that skin, and teeth along the edge. Everything worth taking sits behind three separate defences. That is not a coincidence of parts."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Plants came on day three, the herb yielding seed after his kind. People have since carried this one to nearly every warm country on earth, and it is still recognisably the same plant growing wild in the mountains of Oman."}]},{"id":"wheat","name":"Wheat","group":"crops","blurb":"A grass grown for its seeds, which are ground into flour. It is planted on more land than any other food crop.","size":"Waist to chest high on a nine-year-old, about 2 to 4 ft (0.6-1.2 m), with a seed head the size of your thumb.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":4,"life":1},"wonder":"A grain small enough to lose between your fingers holds a plant, a food store and a coat, and comes up wheat every time.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"In 2024 the world grew about 880 million tons (799 million tonnes) of wheat, on 542 million acres (219.5 million hectares). No other food crop covers that much ground. China, India and Russia grew the most.","more":"Russia sent out the most wheat of anyone in 2024, about 32.7 million tonnes, with Canada, the United States and Ukraine behind it. Egypt bought the most, 13.1 million tonnes."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A wheat grain has three parts: the bran on the outside, the germ, which is the young plant, and the endosperm, which is the packed lunch. White flour is endosperm only. Wholemeal keeps all three."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Bread wheat carries six sets of chromosomes, 42 in all. You carry 46, in two sets. It is a hexaploid, a mouthful meaning three whole grasses' worth of instructions stacked into one plant."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Gluten is about 75 to 80 percent of wheat's protein. Add water and the gluten proteins hook into a stretchy net. The best read of why bread rises is that the net traps the gas yeast makes. Rice flour has no such net, and rice bread does not rise."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Bread needs a fungus. Baker's yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is not a plant at all. It eats sugar in the dough and breathes out carbon dioxide, and the gluten holds the bubbles in place."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Norman Borlaug bred short-strawed wheat in Mexico from 1944. By 1963 Mexico's harvest was six times the 1944 one. India went from 12.3 million tons of wheat in 1965 to 20.1 million in 1970. He was given the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970.","more":"He used shuttle breeding, growing two crops a year at two different altitudes in Mexico. It halved the time to make a new variety and produced wheat that did not much mind where it was planted."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Tall wheat falls flat when it is fed well, and a flattened crop rots. Borlaug put dwarfing genes from a Japanese wheat, Norin 10, into his lines. Short stems hold up under heavy grain, so the fertiliser goes into seed instead of straw."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One sown grain does not make one stem. Wheat tillers: the seedling sends up several stems from its base, each with its own head of grain. Count the heads in a clump and you are counting one seed's work."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Wheat runs right through the Bible. Ruth gleans behind the reapers at the wheat harvest. Pharaoh dreams of seven full ears of grain swallowed by seven thin ones. Jesus says a grain of wheat has to fall into the ground and die before it bears anything."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Winter wheat is sown in autumn, sits out the cold as a small green plant, and will not make a head until it has had that cold. Spring wheat is sown after the frosts and does the whole job in one summer."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three of the creation week names the herb yielding seed after its kind. A wheat grain is that in one object: the young plant, its food store, and a coat around both. Sow it and wheat comes up, not barley."},{"cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Wheat comes in many shapes, from tall old field varieties to Borlaug's knee-high lines, and they all cross with each other. Sizes shift inside a kind. The equipment does not: hollow stem, flat leaves, a head of grain, a seed with germ and endosperm."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Deep in a mountain on Svalbard, in the Arctic, sits a vault for seeds. It runs more than 330 ft (100 m) into the rock, is held at 0°F (minus 18°C), and has room for 4.5 million seed samples — about 500 seeds to a packet, which comes to some 2.25 billion seeds.","more":"Wheat, rice and barley fill more of its shelves than anything else. Seeds have been taken out once. In September 2015 the gene bank driven out of Aleppo, Syria by the war asked for 128 boxes back, about 38,000 samples, and regrew them in Lebanon and Morocco. Fresh seed was later sent back up to Svalbard."}]},{"id":"rice","name":"Rice","group":"crops","blurb":"A grass that grows standing in water, and the main food for more than half the people alive.","size":"About 3 to 4 ft (1-1.2 m) tall in a flooded field, with a heavy drooping head of grain at the top.","homes":["freshwater","grassland"],"stats":{"height":4,"life":1},"wonder":"A plant that would drown most roots pipes its own air down through hollow stems, and feeds half the world standing in water.","facts":[{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Rice is the staple food of over half the world's population. The 2023 crop came to about 800 million tonnes. Nine tenths of it grew in Asia, with India and China between them making just over half."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Cut a rice stem across and it is honeycombed with air channels. Roots in flooded mud have no oxygen to draw on. The best read is that those channels pipe air down from the leaves, which is why rice stands in water that would kill wheat."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Flooding a paddy is a way of weeding. Most weeds drown; rice does not. The water is doing a job, not just watering."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The head of a rice plant, called a panicle, can carry up to 350 spikelets. Every one that gets pollinated turns into a grain."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Deepwater rice puts up with flooding more than 20 in (50 cm) deep for a month or more, growing upward fast enough to keep its leaves above the rising water."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"IR8 was made in 1966 at the International Rice Research Institute in the Philippines, by crossing an Indonesian rice called Peta with a Chinese one called Dee-geo-woo-gen. It was short, it did not fall over, and it yielded so heavily that people called it miracle rice."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Golden Rice was made by Ingo Potrykus and Peter Beyer and published in 2000, after eight years' work. It builds beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A, and that turns the grain orange. A later version, Golden Rice 2, makes 23 times more.","more":"Vitamin A shortage is a real cause of blindness where rice is nearly the whole diet. Canada and the United States cleared Golden Rice in 2018 and the Philippines in 2019; a Philippine court ordered it stopped again in 2024."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Brown rice and white rice are the same grain. White rice has had the bran and germ polished off, which makes it keep longer and cook faster, and takes most of the vitamins away with them."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Across much of Asia rice is not sown where it grows. Seed is sprouted thickly in a nursery bed, then the seedlings are pulled up and pushed into the mud of the paddy by hand, one small clump at a time."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Rice, wheat, maize, sugarcane and the lawn outside are all one family, the grasses. Every grain crop on earth is a grass seed."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the seed-bearing plants, each after its kind. A rice grain dropped into warm mud sends a root down and a shoot up within days, and what comes up is rice. Nobody has ever had to teach it which way is up."}]},{"id":"maize","name":"Maize","group":"crops","blurb":"A giant grass from the Americas that carries its seeds on a cob, and grows more weight of grain each year than anything else.","size":"Usually 7 to 10 ft (2-3 m) tall, well over an adult's head, with the cobs at about chest height.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":10,"life":1},"wonder":"One silk thread for every seed, each thread finding its own kernel, and a gap in the rows means one thread got missed.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The silk on a cob is not decoration. There is exactly one silk thread for each kernel, and a grain of pollen has to land on that thread and grow down it. A cob with gaps in the rows is a cob whose silks got missed."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Kernels sit in 8 to 32 rows around the cob, and a big cob can carry up to 1,200 of them. That is 1,200 seeds hanging off one stalk."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"World maize came to 1.16 billion tonnes in 2020, a greater weight than any other grain. The United States grew 31 percent of it and China 22.4 percent."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Barbara McClintock spent decades on maize and found that pieces of its genetic instructions move about, switching kernel colours on and off. Almost nobody believed her at first. She was given the Nobel Prize in 1983."},{"cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"Maize on its own is missing usable niacin, a B vitamin, and a maize-only diet causes pellagra. Peoples of the Americas soaked their maize in wood-ash or lime water, which frees the niacin. That is nixtamalisation, and it is what makes a tortilla a tortilla.","more":"Pellagra still turns up where donated maize is eaten without that soaking step. The fix was worked out long before anybody had heard of a vitamin, and it was hiding in the cooking method all along."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A popcorn kernel is about 14 percent water, sealed in a hard hull. Heat turns that water to steam at roughly 135 psi and 356 F (180 C) before the hull splits. The starch inside blows up into foam 20 to 50 times the size of the kernel."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Maize keeps its two flower types apart. The tassel on top makes pollen and drops it. The ears lower down catch it on their silks. Pollen from your own tassel usually drifts sideways before it lands, so most cobs are fathered by the neighbours."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Maize cannot sow itself. The cob grips its seeds inside a husk that does not open. Drop a whole cob and you get a knot of seedlings strangling each other. The best read is plain: this plant needs a person to strip the seeds off and space them out."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Corn smut is a fungus, not a plant. It swells kernels into grey lumps the size of golf balls. In Mexico that is not a disaster, it is huitlacoche, scraped off and cooked into quesadillas."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Look at the bottom of a maize stalk and you will find brace roots, thick props angling out of the stem above the soil. They go in later than the ordinary roots and hold a top-heavy plant upright in wind."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the herb yielding seed. A maize cob is that counted out: up to 1,200 seeds on one stalk, each one wrapped, each one fed by its own thread, each one a whole maize plant folded small."}]},{"id":"potato","name":"Potato","group":"crops","blurb":"A swollen underground stem from the Andes, and the biggest food crop in the world that is not a grain.","size":"The plant is knee to waist high, about 2 to 3 ft (60-90 cm). The part you eat is buried, on stems running sideways.","homes":["mountain","backyard"],"stats":{"height":3,"life":1},"wonder":"The eyes are not decoration. Every one is a bud, and a potato forgotten in a dark cupboard will prove it.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A potato is a stem, not a root. The eyes are buds, and if you leave one in the light, shoots come out of the eyes, exactly where a stem puts them. Roots do not carry buds arranged like that."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Tubers grow at the tips of stolons, underground stems running sideways from the base of the plant. One plant makes several, which is why you can dig a whole nest of potatoes out from under a single seedling."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"The International Potato Center in Lima, Peru keeps 4,870 kinds of potato, most of them traditional Andean varieties. Purple ones, spotted ones, ones shaped like a knuckle. The shop sells you about four."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The 2024 crop was 390 million tonnes. China grew 94.8 million tonnes of it, about a quarter of the world's potatoes, and India 57.1 million."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"A green potato is a warning you can see. Light makes the tuber build solanine, which tastes bitter and can make you ill. Green skin, green flesh, sprouting eyes: cut them out or throw it away."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Irish famine was caused by potato late blight. The killer is usually called a fungus, and it is not one. Phytophthora infestans is an oomycete, a water mould, a different sort of living thing altogether and closer to seaweed than to mushrooms.","more":"Blight rots a field in days in warm wet weather. It reached Ireland in the 1840s, where a huge share of people ate little else, and the crop failed year after year."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Potatoes are grown from pieces of potato, so a field is one plant copied thousands of times. Anything that can beat one plant can beat every plant in the field on the same afternoon. That reasoning is why thousands of varieties are kept safe in Lima."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Seed potatoes are not seeds. They are chunks of tuber with an eye in them, and what grows is a copy. Potato plants do make real seeds, in small green berries above ground after flowering, and those berries are poisonous."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"In the high Andes potatoes are turned into chuno. They are spread out to freeze on cold nights, thaw in the day, and get trodden to squeeze the water out, over and over, until they are hard and light and keep for years."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The potato is a nightshade. Tomato, chilli pepper and tobacco are all in the same family, which is exactly why Europeans were suspicious of it when it first turned up."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names plants yielding seed. The potato has two ways of making more of itself built in: flowers that set true seed inside a green berry, and a stem underground stuffed with food and studded with buds."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Pieces of potato plant — a leaf, a bud and a scrap of stem — flew on the shuttle Columbia in October 1995, in a growth chamber built at the University of Wisconsin. They grew real tubers in orbit, and those tubers were built the same inside as ones grown on the ground.","more":"Gravity was not needed to make a proper potato. Later the International Potato Center in Lima sealed a tuber inside a box that held Mars air pressure, Mars gases and Mars day-and-night temperatures, planted in near-lifeless soil trucked in from the Pampas de La Joya desert. In March 2017 they reported it had sprouted."}]},{"id":"tomato","name":"Tomato","group":"crops","blurb":"A berry from South America that nearly everyone files under vegetables, including the Supreme Court of the United States.","size":"A staked vine runs 6 ft (1.8 m) or more up its string; bush kinds sprawl to about knee height.","homes":["backyard","grassland"],"stats":{"height":6,"life":1},"wonder":"The fruit is a case built to be eaten: coloured to be found, sweetened to be worth it, packed with seeds meant to survive the trip.","facts":[{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A tomato is botanically a berry, a fruit with its seeds sitting in pulp. Grapes and bananas are berries too. Strawberries and raspberries are not."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 10 May 1893 the Supreme Court of the United States ruled, in Nix v. Hedden, that tomatoes are vegetables. Vegetables were taxed at the border and fruit was not. The judges agreed a tomato is botanically a fruit, and taxed it anyway, because people eat it with dinner and not for pudding."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"John Gerard's Herbal of 1597 told English readers the tomato was poisonous. He knew perfectly well that Spaniards and Italians were eating them. His book was trusted, and Britain kept its distance for a very long time afterwards."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"The popular explanation is that acid tomatoes pulled lead out of rich people's pewter plates. It gets repeated everywhere. Chemists who have checked it, among them Joe Schwarcz at McGill University, say a tomato is nowhere near acid enough. The nightshade family and Gerard's book explain the fear better."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Gerard was not entirely wrong. Tomato leaves, stems and green unripe fruit hold tomatine, an alkaloid the plant makes. The ripe fruit does not, and the ripe fruit is the only part anybody eats."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The heaviest tomato weighed 16.89 lb (7.661 kg) and measured 39 in around. It was a Domingo, grown by Dan Sutherland at Walla Walla, Washington, and weighed on 19 July 2025. He had broken his own record eight days earlier.","more":"Sutherland was the first person on record to grow a tomato past 10 lb. Giant tomatoes are usually lumpy and scarred, because they are several tomatoes' worth of flower fused into one fruit."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A tomato flower holds its pollen in tubes that only let go when they are shaken at the right speed. Bumblebees do it by gripping the flower and buzzing their flight muscles. Greenhouse growers either keep bumblebees or walk the rows with a vibrating wand."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Tomatoes ripen themselves with a gas. They give off ethylene, and ethylene tells the fruit to soften, redden and sweeten. Put a ripe tomato in a paper bag with green ones and the green ones catch up faster."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"The word comes from the Nahuatl tomatl, by way of Spanish. Spanish ships carried the plant back across the Atlantic in the 1500s, and it took a couple of centuries before Europeans north of Italy would eat it."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Nightshade family again: tomato, potato, chilli and tobacco are close relatives. You can graft a tomato shoot onto a potato plant and get tomatoes above and potatoes below, off one set of roots."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the seed-bearing plants and the fruit trees, each after its kind. Slice a tomato across and the arrangement is right there: seeds set in jelly, inside a case coloured to be noticed and sweetened to be eaten."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Frank Rubio picked a Red Robin tomato on the space station on 29 March 2023, bagged it, and lost it. Crewmates teased him about eating the evidence for months. On 6 December 2023 the crew announced they had found the bag, tomato inside, dried out and a bit squashed. He was cleared.","more":"Rubio was not meant to be up there that long. His Soyuz sprang a coolant leak, a replacement had to be flown up empty, and a six-month trip turned into 371 days — the longest single spaceflight by an American. He landed on 27 September 2023, ten weeks before the tomato turned up."}]},{"id":"banana","name":"Banana","group":"crops","blurb":"Not a tree and not a bush. It is the largest herb on earth, and its fruit is a berry with no seeds in it.","size":"Commonly 15 to 25 ft (4.5-7.5 m) tall, held up by rolled leaf bases with no wood anywhere in them.","homes":["rainforest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":20},"wonder":"A plant with no wood in it anywhere holds a whole bunch of fruit in the air on nothing but rolled-up leaves.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The banana is the largest herbaceous flowering plant there is. What looks like a trunk is a pseudostem, made of leaf stalks wrapped tightly round each other. There is no wood in it. You can cut one through with a knife."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A banana is botanically a berry. So is a tomato and so is a grape. The word berry means seeds set in pulp, which is awkward, because the banana you eat has no seeds at all."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Cavendish came out of a greenhouse in Derbyshire. Joseph Paxton, head gardener at Chatsworth House, raised plants shipped from Mauritius and named them Musa cavendishii after his employer, the sixth Duke of Devonshire. They flowered in 1835, fruited the next May, and won a medal at that year's Horticultural Society show."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Every Cavendish banana is a clone. They set no seed, so they are grown from suckers cut off the base of the parent plant. The banana in your lunchbox and the banana in a shop on the other side of the world are the same individual plant, copied and copied again."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"One clone means one weakness. If a disease can kill one Cavendish it can kill all of them, because there is no variation anywhere in the crop for it to trip over. That is not a prediction, it is arithmetic, and it has already happened once."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The banana in shops used to be the Gros Michel. A soil fungus, Fusarium oxysporum, caused Panama disease and wrecked the export crop by the 1950s. Growers switched to the Cavendish because it shrugged that strain off. Then a new strain, Tropical Race 4, turned up that does not.","more":"Tropical Race 4 was first identified in Taiwan in 1967 and has since spread through Asia, Australia, Africa, the Middle East and into Latin America. It attacks Cavendish and as much as 80 percent of the other kinds grown."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The killer is a fungus, which is not a plant. It lives in the soil rather than on the plant, so once a field has it, that field keeps it. You cannot spray it out and you cannot wait it out."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Wild bananas are full of hard black seeds the size of peppercorns, packed so tightly there is barely anything to eat. The eating kinds set fruit without being pollinated at all, so the seeds never form and the flesh fills the space."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A bunch is built in tiers called hands, about nine of them, with up to twenty fruits to a hand. One finger is one banana. The whole bunch grows on a single stalk that bends the plant over."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"India grows the most bananas by a long way: 34.5 million tonnes in 2022, roughly a fifth of the world's crop. Most of it never leaves the country."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Banana sweets taste of one chemical, isoamyl acetate, which is genuinely present in real bananas and in pears. A honeybee makes the same chemical at its sting, as an alarm signal telling the rest of the hive where to aim."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names fruit yielding fruit after its kind. The eating banana is an odd case: no seeds, so it cannot sow itself, and every plant alive is a cutting from a cutting kept going by human hands. The wild kinds still carry their seeds."}]},{"id":"coffee","name":"Coffee","group":"crops","blurb":"A tropical shrub grown for the two seeds inside its small red fruit, which are roasted, ground and soaked in hot water.","size":"Left alone it grows into a small tree. On farms it is cut back to 5 or 6 ft (1.5-1.8 m) so pickers can reach the fruit.","homes":["rainforest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":6},"wonder":"The bitterness that keeps insects off the leaves is the same bitterness people get up early for.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A coffee bean is not a bean, it is a seed. Each coffee fruit, called a cherry, usually holds two of them lying flat side to flat side. That flat face is why a roasted bean has a crease down the middle."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"About 5 to 10 percent of coffee cherries hold only one seed instead of two, and with nothing to press against it grows round. Those are peaberries, and some buyers sort them out and sell them separately."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The world picked 11 million tonnes of green coffee beans in 2023. Brazil grew 31 percent of it."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Coffee leaf rust, Hemileia vastatrix, is a fungus and not a plant. It was reported in Ceylon, now Sri Lanka, in 1867, and by 1890 the island's coffee industry was all but destroyed. The planters pulled out the coffee and put in tea.","more":"That is one of the reasons Britain became a tea-drinking country rather than a coffee-drinking one. The rust reached Brazil in 1970, was in every Brazilian coffee area by 1975, and had covered most of Central and South America by 1981."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"There are two coffees in the trade. Arabica is the one people fuss over. Robusta holds about 40 to 50 percent more caffeine, tastes harsher, and is easier to grow, so it gets blended in."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Caffeine is the plant's chemical, not ours. It puts off animals that would chew the leaves, and it stops the seeds of other plants sprouting near the roots. The best read is that it is a weapon on two fronts, and we drink it on purpose."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Caffeine also turns up in the nectar of coffee flowers, where it is not defending anything. Bees will work a caffeinated flower. Reasoned from that: the same chemical is being used to push some animals away and pull others in."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Gabriel de Clieu carried a coffee plant by ship to Martinique in the 1720s. Much of the world's arabica traces back to plants raised from that one."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Arabica is grown high on tropical mountains, where nights are cool and frost never comes. Robusta takes the hot lowlands. That split is why a bag of coffee tells you an altitude, and why a warming climate is a real worry for arabica farms."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A green coffee seed smells of hay and nothing else. Every smell you know as coffee is made in the roaster, in a few minutes, out of a seed that had none of it beforehand."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the herb yielding seed. Coffee's seed is the whole point of the plant: it is wrapped in sweet red pulp to get it carried off, and everything anyone has ever done to coffee has been done to a seed."}]},{"id":"vanilla","name":"Vanilla orchid","group":"crops","blurb":"A climbing orchid grown for its cured pods, and the only orchid in the world farmed for food.","size":"A vine that hauls itself up a tree or a post and can run 50 ft (15 m), gripping with roots along the stem.","homes":["rainforest"],"stats":{"height":50},"wonder":"A flower that opens for one day and shuts the door on its own pollen is not a flower that arrived by accident.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Vanilla is an orchid and it is a vine. It climbs a tree or a post, and along the stem it sends out thick roots that grip the bark as it goes."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A vanilla flower lasts about one day, sometimes less. Miss it and it drops off and no pod grows. Every morning of the flowering season somebody walks the whole plantation looking for flowers that opened that day."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 1841 Edmond Albius, an enslaved boy of twelve on the island of Reunion, worked out how to pollinate vanilla by hand. Inside the flower a flap separates the male part from the female. He lifted it with a sliver of bamboo and pressed the two together with his thumb.","more":"It is still done exactly that way. He showed his owner, Ferreol Bellier-Beaumont, two vanilla pods hanging on a vine and said he had made them himself. The technique went from Reunion to Madagascar and eventually back to Mexico. Albius died poor."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Morren, at Liege in Belgium, published a way to pollinate vanilla by hand in 1837, four years before Albius. His method was too slow and too costly to use on a farm, so nothing came of it. Albius's worked in the field, and that is the difference."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"In Mexico orchid bees of the genus Eulaema do the job. Even there, wild vanilla flowers only set fruit about one time in a hundred. Take the plant anywhere else and there is no insect shaped to fit it, so a person has to be the bee."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A pollinated flower takes five to six weeks to grow a full-sized pod and about six months more to mature. Then the curing begins: killing, sweating, drying and conditioning, which takes months again."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A green vanilla pod straight off the vine barely smells of anything. The vanilla smell is not harvested, it is made during curing, as the pod is heated, wrapped, sweated and slowly dried."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Madagascar grew 42 percent of the world's vanilla in 2023, with Indonesia next at 25 percent. Both are a long way from Mexico, where the plant is from, and neither has the bee."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Most vanilla is not vanilla. Synthetic vanillin is about 88 percent of the vanillin sold. Real extract from cured pods is under 1 percent of it, around 50 tons a year for the entire world."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Vanilla plantations are grown from cuttings, so the vines in a field are clones of each other. Same arithmetic as the banana: no variation in the crop means no variation for a disease to trip over."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names seed after its kind. The vanilla flower is built so carefully that a flap keeps its own pollen off its own stigma. Without the right bee, or a boy with a splinter of bamboo, no pod forms and no seed follows."}]},{"id":"pineapple","name":"Pineapple","group":"crops","blurb":"A South American bromeliad whose fruit is not one fruit at all. It is up to two hundred flowers grown into a single lump.","size":"A rosette of stiff spiky leaves about 3 to 4 ft (1-1.2 m) across, with one fruit on a stalk in the middle.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":4},"wonder":"A hundred separate flowers grow into one body, and the joins are still there on the skin as diamonds.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A pineapple is a multiple fruit. The plant sends up a spike of up to 200 small flowers, and they all swell and fuse into one. Every diamond-shaped eye on the skin is one flower that used to be separate."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Pineapple contains bromelain, an enzyme that takes proteins apart. That is why fresh pineapple makes your mouth tingle: it is digesting you back, a little. It is also why fresh pineapple will not set in jelly, because it chops up the gelatine.","more":"Tinned or cooked pineapple sets in jelly perfectly well. Heat wrecks the enzyme, and a wrecked enzyme does nothing. Try both in two bowls and you get the answer in an afternoon."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Cut the leafy crown off a pineapple, let the cut dry for a day or two, and plant it. It roots and grows. The plant also throws side shoots at its base, called slips and suckers, and those get planted too."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A pineapple takes about a year from planting to a ripe fruit, and the plant makes one fruit at a time. Everything it does for that year goes into a single pineapple."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The world grew 29.4 million tonnes of pineapples in 2024. Costa Rica led with 3.1 million tonnes, then the Philippines with 2.9 and Indonesia with 2.7."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Columbus's crew met pineapples in 1493. In Europe the fruit became a symbol of money, because growing one in a cold country meant building a heated glasshouse called a pinery and burning fuel in it for a year or more. People hired a pineapple for the evening to put on the table."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Pineapples are bromeliads. Spanish moss, the grey stuff hanging off trees in the American South, is a relative, and so are the spiky tank plants that hold rainwater in their centres."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The pineapples in shops are grown unpollinated on purpose. The flowers fuse into fruit anyway, and because nothing was fertilised there are no seeds. Wild ones, pollinated, come with small hard seeds in the flesh."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"A pineapple opens the pores in its leaves at night rather than in the day, takes in carbon dioxide then, and stores it until morning. That way it does its breathing when the air is cool. The best read is that this is how it survives long dry spells on thin soil."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Many pineapple varieties carry rows of small backward-pointing saw teeth along the leaf edges. Reaching into the middle of the plant to cut the fruit means reaching past every one of them."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names fruit after its kind. A pineapple is two hundred flowers built into one body on one stalk, and the crown you slice off the top will grow into the next plant. Nothing about it is spare."}]},{"id":"strawberry","name":"Strawberry","group":"crops","blurb":"The red part is not the fruit and the specks are not seeds. It is the swollen tip of a flower stalk, studded with the real fruits.","size":"A low clump about 8 to 12 in (20-30 cm) high, sending runners out sideways across the ground.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":1},"wonder":"The red part is not the fruit and the specks are not seeds, and knowing that makes the thing more interesting, not less.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Each speck on a strawberry is an achene, a dry fruit with one seed inside it. The red flesh is the receptacle, the tip of the stalk that the flower sat on, swollen up. Botanists call the whole arrangement an aggregate accessory fruit."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A medium strawberry carries around 200 achenes on its surface. So a strawberry is not one fruit with seeds on the outside. It is about two hundred fruits sitting on a red cushion."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A strawberry is not a berry. A tomato is, a grape is, a banana is. A strawberry is not, and neither is a raspberry."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The achenes drive the swelling. As they develop they release auxin, a growth chemical, and the flesh under each one puffs up in response. That explains lopsided strawberries: where the achenes failed, the flesh underneath stayed flat."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Strawberries send out runners, thin stems that creep across the ground, put roots down at a node and start a new plant. Growers peg the runners down, let them root and cut them free. Every one is a clone of the parent."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Amedee-Francois Frezier was a French military engineer sent to South America to sketch Spanish harbour defences. He came home in 1714 with five Chilean strawberry plants, which had enormous fruit. In France they flowered and set almost nothing, because all five were female.","more":"Only five plants survived the crossing, and he gave most of them away. The one he kept went to Brest in Brittany, where they ended up growing near North American strawberries, and the two crossed."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 1766, at Versailles, Antoine Nicolas Duchesne worked out that the garden strawberry was a cross between the Chilean plant and a North American one, and that strawberries have separate sexes. He was eighteen. He was born in 1747 and lived to 1827."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Strawberries are in the rose family. Apples, pears, plums, cherries, blackberries and roses are all relatives, which is easier to believe once you look at a strawberry flower: five white petals and a crowd of stamens, exactly like a wild rose."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The world grew 10.7 million tonnes of strawberries in 2024, with China producing 38 percent of them and the United States next."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The garden strawberry does not exist in the wild anywhere. It was made in Europe out of one plant from Chile and one from eastern North America, and every shop strawberry comes from that pairing."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names plants whose seed is in themselves. The strawberry wears its fruits on the outside, about two hundred of them, spread over a red cushion that was never a fruit at all and exists only to get them eaten and carried."}]},{"id":"pumpkin","name":"Pumpkin","group":"crops","blurb":"A sprawling vine that grows the heaviest fruit on earth. The record is over a ton and a third.","size":"The vine is only about knee high but runs many yards across the ground. The record fruit weighed 2,819 lb (1,278.8 kg).","homes":["backyard","grassland"],"stats":{"weight":2819,"life":1},"wonder":"The same vine that makes a pie pumpkin can make one heavier than a car, given water, room, and one fruit to feed.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The heaviest pumpkin ever weighed came to 2,819 lb 4 oz (1,278.8 kg). It was nicknamed Muggle and grown by twin brothers Ian and Stuart Paton, weighed at Wargrave in Berkshire on 6 October 2025. It also measured 21 ft 3.8 in (649.8 cm) around.","more":"It was the first time British growers had held the record. The Patons have been growing giant pumpkins since they were teenagers, which is around fifty years of practice for one afternoon on the scales."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"At full tilt a giant pumpkin can put on 50 lb (23 kg) in a single day. You cannot see it move, but you can measure it in the morning and again at night and the numbers will not match."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A giant pumpkin is up to 94 percent water, and its cells are bigger than an ordinary pumpkin's. It is not so much grown as filled."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Nearly every record pumpkin is one variety, Dill's Atlantic Giant, bred by Howard Dill in Nova Scotia. Growers trade seed from record fruit, so the seeds out of a champion are worth real money."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The Patons grew a heavier pumpkin than Muggle in 2022, at 2,907.85 lb, and it did not count. It had split. A damaged pumpkin is disqualified, so a season's work can end with one crack in the rind."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Pumpkins put male and female flowers on the same plant. The males open first, on long thin stalks. A female flower has a tiny green pumpkin already sitting behind it, and it only swells if pollen reaches it."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Giant-pumpkin growers do not leave that to the bees. They pick a male flower, brush its pollen onto the female by hand, and tape her shut so nothing else gets in. Then they know exactly which two plants made the fruit."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A pumpkin is a fruit, and specifically a berry, of the sort called a pepo: soft inside, seeds in the middle, hard rind outside. Cucumbers, melons and courgettes are all pepos too."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"To grow a monster you take every other fruit off the vine. One pumpkin, one plant, hundreds of square feet of leaf all feeding a single fruit. Leave two on and you get two medium ones."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Run a hand along a pumpkin stem and it is covered in short stiff bristles, and so are the undersides of the leaves. The flowers, by contrast, are soft and edible, and get stuffed and fried in Mexico and Italy."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the seed-bearing plants. A pumpkin is one taken to the limit: separate male and female flowers on the same vine, and a fruit that can end up over 2,800 lb with the seeds still sitting quietly in the middle of it."}]},{"id":"sugarcane","name":"Sugarcane","group":"crops","blurb":"A grass twenty feet tall with sugar stored in its stem. By weight it is the biggest crop grown anywhere on earth.","size":"6 to 20 ft (2-7 m) tall on stout jointed stalks, thicker than a broom handle.","homes":["grassland","rainforest"],"stats":{"height":20},"wonder":"A grass that catches sunlight better than almost anything else green, and then stacks the result in its own stem.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Sugarcane is the largest crop in the world by weight: 1.9 billion tonnes in 2020. Brazil grows about 40 percent of it, 724.4 million tonnes in 2022, with India next at 439.4 million."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Cane stands 6 to 20 ft (2-7 m) tall on jointed fibrous stalks. Walking into a mature cane field is like walking into a wall of bamboo you cannot see through."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"A ripe cane stalk is roughly 12 to 16 percent soluble sugar. The rest is fibre, water and other carbohydrate. Crushing the stalk squeezes that sugar out as juice."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Sugarcane is a C4 plant, which is a different and faster way of catching carbon dioxide. It can turn as much as 1 percent of the sunlight falling on it into plant material. That is close to the ceiling for anything green, and it is the reason the tonnage is so absurd."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Sugarcane is not planted as seed. Lengths of stalk are laid in a furrow and covered, and buds along the stalk sprout into new plants. A cane field is one plant repeated down every row."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Cut cane does not need replanting straight away. The stump left in the ground sends up new stalks, and the same planting can be harvested several times over. Growers call the regrowth a ratoon."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Sugarcane is a grass, family Poaceae, along with wheat, rice, maize and the lawn. It is a grass that grew to twenty feet and decided to store its food in the stem rather than in a seed."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"After the juice is squeezed out, the crushed fibre left behind is called bagasse. Mills burn it to run themselves, and it also gets made into paper and board. Almost none of the stalk is thrown away."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"In Brazil petrol is required by law to contain at least 22 percent bioethanol, and that ethanol is made from cane. A country's cars run partly on grass juice."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Sugar from cane and sugar from beet are the same molecule, sucrose. Once refined there is nothing to tell them apart, which is why the packet often does not say which one it came from."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the grass and the herb yielding seed. Cane is a grass, in the same family as wheat and rice. What it does differently is where it puts the food: not into a seed, but into the stalk, which is why it is us and not the next generation that gets it."}]},{"id":"peanut","name":"Peanut","group":"crops","blurb":"Not a nut but a legume, and one of very few plants that plants its own fruit underground.","size":"A low bushy plant about 1 to 2 ft (30-60 cm) high, with the pods forming under the soil beneath it.","homes":["grassland","backyard"],"stats":{"height":2,"life":1},"wonder":"The flower does its work above ground and then hands the job down a stalk into the dark, on purpose, every time.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A peanut is not a nut. It is a legume, so peas and beans are the relatives, not walnuts or almonds. The shell is a pod, and it splits down the seam like a pea pod does."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The flower opens above ground and is pollinated there. Then a stalk at the base of the fertilised ovary, called a peg, turns downward, grows to the soil and pushes into it. The pod develops underground, in the dark, 90 to 150 days after planting.","more":"The peg keeps growing until the young pod is buried, then stops and lets the seeds swell. Pull a peanut plant up and you can see the pegs still attached, running from the stem down to each pod like short ropes."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"The word for burying your own fruit is geocarpy, meaning earth fruit. Very few plants do it. Reasoned from where the pods end up, the peg is not a root doing a strange job; it is a fruit stalk with somewhere very specific to be."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Peanut roots carry nodules packed with bacteria that pull nitrogen straight out of the air and hand it to the plant. That is why peanuts are grown in rotation with other crops. In Texas rotations, peanuts yield about 50 percent more than peanuts grown in the same ground year after year."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"George Washington Carver pushed peanuts on southern farms whose soil cotton had stripped bare, and worked out hundreds of things to make from them, from paint to plastics. He did not invent peanut butter. That was patented by Marcellus Gilmore Edson in 1884."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The world grew 54.3 million tonnes of peanuts in 2023. China grew 36 percent of them, 19.2 million tonnes, with India next at 10.3 million."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Peanut butter takes nothing but peanuts. Grinding crushes the cells and the oil that was inside them comes out and coats everything else, so a dry powder turns into a paste with nothing added at all."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Damp stored peanuts grow Aspergillus, a mould, which is a fungus and not a plant. It makes aflatoxin, which is genuinely poisonous. This is why peanuts get dried hard and fast after lifting, and why a mouldy peanut is not worth the risk."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Peanut allergy is one of the most serious food allergies there is. It is not fussiness and it is not the same as disliking them. A trace can be enough, which is why so many schools keep them off the premises."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Peanut leaves fold shut at night, the four leaflets closing up like a book, and open again in the morning. You can watch a whole field do it."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Peanuts want light sandy soil, and the reason is mechanical rather than chemical: the peg has to be able to push into the ground. In heavy baked clay it cannot get in, and no pods form however well the plant flowers."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the seed-bearing plants. The peanut buries its own seed before it lets go of it. The flower is above ground, the pod is below, and a stalk built for the job carries one to the other."}]},{"id":"chilli","name":"Chilli pepper","group":"crops","blurb":"A fruit that defends itself with a chemical that switches on the nerve for heat, while nothing is actually hot.","size":"A bushy plant 2 to 3 ft (60-90 cm) high, carrying fruit from thumbnail size to the length of your hand.","homes":["rainforest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":3,"life":3},"wonder":"A chemical that fools one nerve into shouting fire, and leaves birds entirely unbothered.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The burn is a chemical called capsaicin, and most of it is not in the seeds. The highest concentration is in the white pith on the inner wall, where the seeds are attached. The seeds themselves make none. They just sit against the hottest part of the fruit."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Capsaicin locks onto TRPV1, the same receptor on a nerve that fires when something is genuinely hot. The nerve cannot tell the difference and reports burning. Nothing is burning. Your mouth is being lied to by a molecule."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The hottest chilli on record is Pepper X, at an average 2,693,000 Scoville Heat Units. It was bred by Ed Currie in Fort Mill, South Carolina, and tested at Winthrop University using samples from four years of crops. Guinness recognised it in October 2023.","more":"Currie also bred the previous record holder, the Carolina Reaper, which averages about 1.64 million units. Pepper X is roughly three times as hot as that. A jalapeno is around 3,000 to 8,000."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The scale belongs to Wilbur Scoville, a pharmacist, who set out his test in 1912 at Parke-Davis. He soaked pepper extract into sugar water and diluted it, again and again, until a panel of five tasters could no longer feel any heat. The number of dilutions was the score."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Pure capsaicin measures 16,000,000 Scoville units. That is the top of the scale, because it is the chemical itself with nothing else in the way."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Birds have TRPV1 too, and capsaicin does not touch it. Birds eat chillies and feel nothing. Mammal teeth grind the seeds to bits; birds swallow them whole and pass them still able to sprout. The best read is that the burn sorts the eaters by what they do to seeds."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Milk beats water. Capsaicin does not dissolve in water, so water just moves it about. Milk carries casein and fat, which pick the capsaicin up and take it away. Bread and rice help for the same sort of reason; a glass of water does almost nothing."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A bell pepper is a chilli with the heat switched off. It measures zero Scoville units. Same genus, same fruit, one broken step in the chemistry, and you can eat it in slices."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"They are not peppers. Columbus was looking for black pepper, found these instead, and called them by the same word. Black pepper is Piper, from India. Chillies are Capsicum, from the Americas, and are not related at all."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Chillies are nightshades. Tomato, potato and tobacco are the near relatives, which puts the hottest thing in the kitchen in the same family as the mildest."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"A chilli plant is not really an annual. Where frost never comes it keeps going for several years, getting woody at the base and fruiting each season. In a cold country it dies in the first hard night, so people treat it as a one-summer plant."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the fruit with its seed in itself. The chilli's seeds sit pressed against the hottest tissue in the fruit and come through it untouched. The burn is in the wall around them, not in the seed it is guarding."}]},{"id":"grape","name":"Grapevine","group":"crops","blurb":"A woody climber that hauls itself up trees on coiled tendrils and carries its fruit in bunches of berries.","size":"Left to climb, a vine can reach 30 ft (9 m) or more. Pruned in a vineyard it is kept at about chest height.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{"height":30,"life":100},"wonder":"A tendril reaches, finds nothing, waves, finds a branch, then coils itself into a spring, and it does all of that without eyes.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A vine climbs with tendrils. A tendril grows out, touches something, wraps round it, and then coils the middle section into a spring. The spring holds the vine tight and still lets it move in wind without snapping."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A grape is botanically a berry. Tomatoes and bananas are berries; strawberries and raspberries are not. A bunch of grapes is a bunch of separate berries on one stalk."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 9 says that after the flood Noah began to be a farmer, and planted a vineyard. It is the first vineyard the Bible names anyone planting, and the passage that follows is not flattering to him."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In Numbers 13 the men Moses sends to look at Canaan cut down a single cluster of grapes at the brook of Eshcol and carry it back slung on a staff between two of them. The cluster is the evidence: the land grows fruit like that."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Grape phylloxera, an insect from North America that eats vine roots, was first recorded in France in 1863 and destroyed more than 40 percent of the country's vines. Jules-Emile Planchon worked out what was doing it. The cure was to graft French vines onto American roots that the insect could not kill.","more":"It is called the Great French Wine Blight. Charles Valentine Riley, an American entomologist, confirmed Planchon's finding and helped find the American vines to graft onto. Nearly every wine vine in Europe today is still growing on American roots."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The Great Vine at Hampton Court Palace was planted in the 1760s and was recorded as the largest grape vine in the world. By 1968 its trunk measured 81 in (2.1 m) and the vine itself ran 100 ft (30 m). It still crops every year."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The world grew 72.5 million tonnes of grapes in 2023. China grew 19 percent of them, with Italy and France next."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Cut a grape almost in half, leave the halves joined by a strip of skin, and microwave it: it makes a flash of plasma. Hamza Khattak and Aaron Slepkov explained it in 2019. The halves concentrate the microwaves where they touch, hot enough to strip electrons off the air."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"The dusty bloom on a grape skin is wax, and wild yeasts live in it. Yeast is a fungus, not a plant. Crushed grapes left alone will start fermenting on their own, and the reasoning is straightforward: the thing that does it was already on the outside of the fruit."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Vines are grown from cuttings. A cut length of stem, stuck in soil, roots and becomes a vine. So a vineyard of one variety is one plant repeated down every row, which is why a named grape tastes the same in a thousand places."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Grapes only grow on shoots made that same year. That is why every vine is cut back hard each winter to a few buds. A vine left unpruned turns into a great tangle of old wood carrying almost no fruit."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the fruit tree yielding fruit, whose seed is in itself, after its kind. A grapevine is exactly that, and it is the first crop the Bible records anybody deliberately planting."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Old Vine in Maribor, Slovenia, is in the Guinness records as the oldest vine in the world still bearing fruit — over 400 years old, growing up the front of a house. It shows up in a picture of the city drawn in 1681, which is part of how the age is fixed.","more":"It still crops: 88 to 110 lb (40 to 50 kg) of Žametovka grapes a year, pressed into 4 to 9 gallons (15 to 35 litres) of pale pink wine and put into little bottles holding about a cup (250 ml). Cuttings are handed out as official gifts — more than 170 of them, in 24 countries. Pope John Paul II was given one."}]},{"id":"giant-kelp","name":"Giant kelp","group":"water","blurb":"A brown seaweed that grows in underwater forests off rocky coasts, running from the seabed to the surface — and it is not a plant.","size":"Measured at more than 150 ft (45 m) from the rock it grips to the daylight, and a few at close to 200 ft (60 m).","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"height":150},"wonder":"Something that stands upright all day in moving water needs a float on every single blade, and that is exactly what it has.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Divers have measured giant kelp at more than 150 ft (45 m) from the rock it grips to the surface, and a few at close to 200 ft (60 m). Most of that length is a single rubbery cable no thicker than a garden hose."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Two feet (60 cm) in a single day. That is the measured top growth rate of giant kelp, which puts it among the fastest-growing living things anywhere.","more":"It needs everything at once to manage that: cold water rising from below with nutrients in it, and bright light at the surface. A single frond only lasts about a hundred days, so the forest is always rebuilding itself at that speed."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is not a plant. Giant kelp is a brown alga: no roots, no true leaves, no flowers, no seeds, no wood. The knobbly tangle at the bottom is called a holdfast, and its only job is grip. It absorbs nothing.","more":"A holdfast is not a root and does not behave like one. It is a mass of branching, gripping fingers cemented onto rock, and the whole point of it is to survive a winter storm. Small animals live inside the tangle year-round."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"At the base of every blade sits a hollow bladder full of gas. The best read is the obvious one — they are floats. The blades they lift ride in the top few feet of water, which is the only place with enough light to work with."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"A tree pulls water and minerals up through roots and pipes them everywhere. Kelp has neither roots nor pipes. The read is that it simply takes what it needs in across its whole surface, because it is already sitting in the stuff."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Giant kelp anchors to rock and reaches all the way up, where the fronds bend over and lie flat in a floating canopy. Boat propellers get tangled in it."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Sea urchins graze kelp. Sea otters eat urchins. Where otters patrol a coast the urchins stay wedged in cracks and the forest stands; where the otters are gone, urchins spread across the rock and strip it bare. The link is a read, not a filmed event."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Near the bottom of the stalk are special blades that make spores and let them go. Each spore grows into a plant so small you need a microscope for it, and that microscopic generation is what produces the next 150-footer.","more":"So the species has two completely different bodies. One of them is among the largest things growing in the sea. The other would sit unnoticed on a pinhead. Same species, one after the other, every time."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Alginate is pulled out of kelp and put into ice cream, salad dressing, jam, cake mix, toothpaste and the goo a dentist uses to take a mould of your teeth. It is what stops things separating in the tub.","more":"Alginate is what makes the kelp bendy in the first place. In the seaweed it sits in the cell walls and lets a frond flex without tearing. Pulled out and dried, it does the same job in a tub of ice cream: it holds everything in one soft mass."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A single frond lasts about a hundred days. The forest you swim through in late summer is not the one that stood there in spring — it has replaced itself, piece by piece, without moving."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Storms rip whole kelp plants free and pile them on beaches, where they rot into black slippery heaps full of sandhoppers and flies. The smell carries a long way. Almost none of it is wasted; the beach eats it."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three did two things: it gathered the waters into seas, and it made the earth bring forth green growing things. Giant kelp sits on the seam — gripping bare rock like something off the land, standing in seawater, bending all day and never snapping."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Off Monterey, California, somebody did the counting. After 2014, when a disease killed the sunflower stars and purple urchins boomed, the sea otters there switched to eating about three times as many urchins as before, and their own numbers climbed from around 270 to around 432.","more":"Those are counted animals and watched meals, written up in March 2021. It did not save everything. North of San Francisco, where there are no otters, over 80 percent of the kelp forest went. Around Monterey, patches of forest held on where the otters worked, while the rock between them went bare."}]},{"id":"duckweed","name":"Duckweed","group":"water","blurb":"The smallest flowering plants there are — green specks that cover a still pond so completely it looks like spilled paint.","size":"One watermeal is about 0.6 mm long, small enough to pass through the eye of a sewing needle.","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Shrink a flowering plant as far as it will go and it still flowers, still sets seed, and still comes up duckweed.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Wolffia angusta, a watermeal, measures about 0.6 mm long and 0.33 mm wide — roughly one fortieth of an inch. Guinness lists it as the smallest flowering plant on record, and a relative, Wolffia globosa, may be smaller still.","more":"For scale, a grain of table salt is around 0.3 mm across. So a whole watermeal plant, flower included, is roughly two grains of salt end to end. It is the entire organism, not a seed or a piece of one."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Its fruit holds a record too: a quarter of a millimetre long, weighing seventy millionths of a gram. No flowering plant anywhere makes a smaller one."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Watermeal has no roots at all and no veins — a flowering plant reduced to a floating green crumb with nothing left to take away. Its cousin Lemna keeps exactly one thread of root under each disc."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It does flower. In a pocket on the upper surface sit one stamen and one pistil and nothing spare, in a flower about 0.3 mm long — the smallest known. Most of the time the plant skips all that and buds a new one off its own edge.","more":"That is why a green pond can be a single individual copied a million times over. Budding is quicker and cheaper. Flowering is the slow route, and it is the one the plant keeps in reserve."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Wolffia microscopica doubles itself in 29.3 hours — the fastest growth measured in any flowering plant. Two specks become four, four become eight, and inside a fortnight the whole pond is a green carpet."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Harvested duckweed can run 35 to 42 percent protein. Square yard for square yard it out-produces a soybean field for protein, and makes several times as much starch as the same area of corn."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"People in Asia have scooped duckweed off ponds and eaten it as a vegetable for a very long time. Ducks, geese and a lot of fish eat it too, which is where the English name came from."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"A pond with no duckweed in spring can be solid with it by August. Nobody has filmed the delivery. The read is feet and feathers: the specks stick to a wet duck, the duck flies to the next pond, and the specks come off."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"A complete duckweed cover is bad news underneath. Almost no light gets through, so the plants below stop making oxygen, and the fish in that pond can end up short of air. Small is not the same as harmless."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Duckweed is put to work cleaning water. Grown on the outflow of a farm or a sewage works, it pulls nitrogen and phosphate out and locks them into itself, and then the whole crop is skimmed off the top.","more":"The nitrogen and phosphate that would otherwise feed an algal bloom downstream end up in the crop instead, and the crop is worth feeding to ducks, fish or pigs. Whether it pays depends on how much it costs to skim, dry and shift it."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Duckweed is not a moss and not an alga. It sits inside the arum family, alongside cuckoo pint and the calla lily, which makes these green specks close relations of some very showy flowers."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the herb yielding seed, each after its kind. Duckweed is the smallest thing on earth that answers to that description: 0.6 mm across, still carrying a stamen, a pistil, a fruit and a seed, and still coming up duckweed."}]},{"id":"papyrus","name":"Papyrus","group":"water","blurb":"A giant African wetland sedge whose stem was sliced, layered and pressed into the world's first paper.","size":"Stems of 13 to 16 ft (4 to 5 m) are ordinary, and around some high African lakes they reach about 33 ft (10 m).","homes":["freshwater"],"stats":{"height":16},"wonder":"The stem was built to hold a plant up in a swamp. It turned out to hold something else as well — writing — and carry it far past everyone who cut it.","facts":[{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A papyrus stem runs 13 to 16 ft (4 to 5 m) of bare green, then bursts at the top into a spray of thread-like rays 4 to 10 in (10 to 30 cm) long. A young one looks exactly like a feather duster."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Roll a papyrus stem between your fingers and you feel three flat sides. It is a triangle, not a tube. Sedges have edges, and papyrus is the largest sedge most people will ever stand next to."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"To make a sheet: peel off the green rind, cut the sticky white pith into strips about 16 in (40 cm) long, lay them side by side slightly overlapping, lay a second layer across at right angles, then hammer the two together while damp and dry them under weight.","more":"The finished sheet was rubbed smooth with a stone or a shell. Hold one up to a window and you can still see the grid where the two layers cross."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"worked","text":"Why cross the layers? A sheet of parallel fibres splits along them the moment you pull. Turn the second layer ninety degrees and each layer holds the other shut. Plywood is the same idea, arrived at a very long time later."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The oldest inscribed papyrus anyone has found is the diary of a man named Merer, dug out at Wadi al-Jarf on Egypt's Red Sea coast. It covers the final stretch of building the Great Pyramid at Giza.","more":"Merer ran a boat crew. His diary records them hauling white limestone downriver from the Tura quarries to Giza for the pyramid's outer casing. The oldest surviving writing on papyrus is, essentially, a delivery schedule."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The English word paper comes from papyrus, through Latin and Greek. Modern paper has never been made from this plant. The name simply stayed on after the material underneath it changed."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Where the water is deep, papyrus does not stand on the bottom. It mats together into floating rafts called sudd, thick enough to block a river. Fishermen set up temporary camps on top of them.","more":"The Sudd, the enormous wetland in South Sudan, is named for these mats. They can seal a channel completely. Nineteenth-century expeditions trying to follow the Nile upstream were stopped for months at a time by vegetation they could neither sail through nor walk on."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Papyrus stems float well, so Egyptians bundled them into boats. A Fourth Dynasty carving shows men cutting papyrus for exactly that, and the same plant supplied the rope and the rigging that held the boat together."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Egyptians ate the thick underground stem and used the rest for rope, sandals, mats and baskets, and carved bowls and utensils from the woody root. One swamp plant covered writing, footwear, rigging, furniture, boats and dinner."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The feather-duster head is the flower. Small brown flowers sit right at the tips of those green threads, and the seeds follow. Almost every photograph of papyrus is a photograph of a flower head nobody recognises as one."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Papyrus went out of use slowly. By around the fourth century the important books were being made on parchment instead, and the last European document written on papyrus was a papal decree issued under Pope Victor II."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"The account puts seed-bearing plants on day three. The first material people wrote on at length was one of them — a swamp weed, sliced up and pressed flat into a page that still turns."}]},{"id":"seagrass","name":"Seagrass","group":"water","blurb":"A true flowering plant that lives underwater in the sea, where it flowers, sheds pollen and sets seed without ever coming up for air.","size":"Blades reach about 3 ft (1 m). One Australian meadow is a single plant with shoots 110 miles (180 km) apart.","homes":["ocean"],"stats":{"height":3},"wonder":"A flower is equipment for open air — dry pollen, a breeze, an insect. Every part of that job is done here in salt water instead, and it still works.","facts":[{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The largest known organism on earth by area is a seagrass. In Shark Bay, Western Australia, one Posidonia australis covers about 77 square miles (200 km2), and shoots sampled 110 miles (180 km) apart turned out to be the same plant.","more":"Researchers read the DNA of shoots from all over the bay and every sample came back identical, which is how a meadow turned out to be one individual. It carries 40 chromosomes where its relatives carry 20, and it creeps outward at about 14 in (35 cm) a year."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Seagrass is not seaweed. Kelp and wrack are algae. Seagrass has roots, a creeping underground stem, veined leaves, flowers, pollen and seeds — the complete kit of a flowering plant, working underwater. There are about 60 species."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Creep 14 in (35 cm) a year and 110 miles takes a while. Run that measured rate backwards and the Shark Bay plant comes out at least 4,500 years old. Nobody started the clock; the arithmetic is what gives the number."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Eelgrass pollen is wrong for pollen. Instead of round dry grains built to drift on a breeze, it comes out in long sticky threads that ride the current and wrap around whatever they touch."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Turtle grass was assumed to be pollinated purely by drifting current. Then researchers found small crustaceans and worm larvae crawling over the flowers at night with pollen grains stuck to them. Underwater flowers get visitors, the same as flowers in air.","more":"The check that settled it was done in tanks with the water held completely still. No current and no wind — and pollen still reached the female flowers, carried on the backs of animals."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Young fish shelter in seagrass, and the read is straightforward: a meadow is a thicket. A big predator cannot see through it or swim fast in it, and the small things hiding inside can. A lot of the fish people eat start life in one."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Dugongs, green turtles, manatees, geese, swans, sea urchins and crabs all eat seagrass. A feeding dugong ploughs visible furrows through a meadow, and you can follow its track from the surface."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Seagrass covers something like one or two thousandths of the sea floor. It accounts for more than a tenth of all the carbon the ocean stores. Acre for acre it holds twice as much carbon dioxide as rainforest."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"A meadow full of leaves slows the water moving through it, and slow water drops the sand it is carrying. The read is that seagrass builds and then holds the seabed it lives on. Tear it up and the bottom starts moving again."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Divers cannot cover thousands of square miles of seabed, so researchers strapped cameras to tiger sharks and let them swim. The footage, reported in 2022, mapped the largest seagrass bed known, near the Bahamas.","more":"The sharks were fitted with cameras and tracking tags and then let go, and they did the survey by going about their normal business. It is a neat solution to a dull problem: a tiger shark covers more seabed in a week than a dive team covers in a season."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"Eelgrass flowers in spring as the water warms. The plant has no eyes and no calendar, so the best read is that it is going by temperature — and every plant in the meadow reads it at about the same time."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three gathered the waters into seas and made the earth bring forth grass, the herb yielding seed. Seagrass is the one green thing doing both jobs in the same place: a seed-bearing plant, flowering, in the sea.","more":"It is not a halfway thing. Everything a flowering plant needs is present and working — root, creeping stem, leaf, flower, pollen, seed — with each part suited to salt water. Threads instead of grains. Current and crustaceans instead of wind and bees."}]},{"id":"sphagnum","name":"Sphagnum moss","group":"moss","blurb":"A bog moss built like a sponge, which soaks up water, turns the ground sour, and slowly turns into peat.","size":"One plant is a few inches of soft green stem. The bog it builds can be miles across and yards deep.","homes":["freshwater","forest","polar"],"stats":{},"wonder":"A plant that carries most of its water in cells that are already dead is a strange design, and it is the whole reason a bog exists.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Most of a Sphagnum leaf is dead on purpose. Big empty barrel-shaped cells sit between the living green ones, each with a pore in it, and water pours in and stays. The living cells do the food-making; the dead ones do the holding."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Sphagnum can hold 16 to 26 times its own dry weight in water, depending on the species. Lift a handful straight out of a bog and it runs like a squeezed sponge."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Sphagnum takes calcium and magnesium out of the water around it and puts hydrogen back. The water turns acid, and the read is plain enough: very little else will grow in it. The moss makes the bog it needs."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The spore capsule fires like a gun. It dries, the air trapped inside compresses, the lid blows off, and the spores leave at 12 ft per second (3.6 m/s) — about 36,000 g of acceleration — reaching 4 to 8 in (10 to 20 cm) up.","more":"The best read of that height is the still air near the ground. Within an inch of a bog surface almost nothing moves. Four inches up there is enough breeze to carry a spore somewhere new, so the capsule's job is to punch through the calm layer."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"No flowers, no seeds. Mosses make spores instead, in a capsule on a stalk, and a spore is a single cell — nothing like a seed with a packed lunch inside it."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Peat is mostly dead Sphagnum that never rotted. Peatlands cover about 3 percent of the world's land and hold 42 percent of all the carbon in soil — more than every forest and every other kind of plant put together.","more":"Peat forms because the bog is too sour and too airless for the bacteria and fungi that normally break dead plants down. So the moss just stacks up, layer on layer, each one squashing the one below it. Nothing is being burned off, which is why the carbon stays put."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Peat builds up at roughly a millimetre a year. A northern peat bog is typically 5 to 7 ft (1.5 to 2.3 m) deep, which is an extremely large number of millimetres."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Whole human bodies have come out of Sphagnum bogs with skin, hair and clothing intact — Tollund Man, Lindow Man, Clonycavan Man. Containers of butter up to two thousand years old have come out too, still recognisably butter.","more":"Three things do it. The water is acid enough to tan skin like leather, there is no oxygen down there for the bacteria that rot things, and Sphagnum releases a substance called sphagnan that kills bacteria outright."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Sphagnum was used as a wound dressing for centuries, including right through the First World War, when it went into field dressings in place of cotton. A botanist named John William Hotson published a paper called 'Sphagnum as a surgical dressing' in Science in 1918.","more":"It was the obvious material: it holds many times its weight in fluid, it costs nothing, and it grows in bogs by the acre. The same sourness that stops a bog rotting is no friendlier to bacteria in a bandage."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Peat is cut and dried for fuel in Ireland and Scotland, and the smoke gets used on purpose: Islay distilleries dry their barley over peat fires, which is where the smoky taste in that whisky comes from."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Sphagnum grows further north than almost anything green. There are populations in Svalbard at 81 degrees north, well inside the Arctic. About 380 species are accepted."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three has the earth bringing forth green growing things — before the sun on day four. Sphagnum grows in the dimmest, wettest, sourest ground there is and takes almost nothing out of the soil. Whatever it was built to run on, it is not rich living."}]},{"id":"tree-fern","name":"Tree fern","group":"ferns","blurb":"A fern that stands on a trunk like a tree, except the trunk is not wood — it is an upright stem wrapped in its own roots.","size":"Most stand 15 to 20 ft (5 to 6 m). The Norfolk Island tree fern reaches about 66 ft (20 m), with fronds 16 ft (5 m) long.","homes":["rainforest","forest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":66},"wonder":"Everything a tree normally uses to stand up — wood, bark, growth rings — is missing here, and it stands anyway.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The trunk is not a trunk. It is an upright underground stem that has stood up, and there is no wood in it at all. What holds it rigid is stiffening in the cell walls plus a thick interlocking felt of tiny roots grown down the outside.","more":"That is why a tree fern has no growth rings and cannot be aged by cutting it open. It also means the trunk cannot heal or thicken once it is formed. A tree fern gets taller, but the trunk it has at the bottom is the trunk it will always have."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Sphaeropteris excelsa, from Norfolk Island in the Pacific, reaches 66 ft (20 m) or more, with single fronds 16 ft (5 m) long. It is probably the largest fern species in the world."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"That species and its close relative are the fastest-growing tree ferns, at up to 20 in (50 cm) of new trunk in a year. That is quick for a fern and slow for a tree."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Dicksonia antarctica of south-east Australia and Tasmania usually reaches 16 ft (5 m) with a trunk a foot (30 cm) thick. Rare ones hit 49 ft (15 m) and 6 ft 7 in (2 m) thick, with fronds 13 ft (4 m) long."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"No flowers, no seeds, no fruit. Tree ferns make spores in patches on the underside of the fronds, and a spore is a single cell — smaller than a grain of dust, with nothing packed inside it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A fern spore does not grow into a fern. It grows into a flat green scrap about the size of a fingernail, and that scrap makes the fern. There are two separate plants in a fern's life, and the famous one is the second.","more":"The flat green scrap is called a prothallus. It is where the egg and the sperm meet, and it needs a film of water for the sperm to swim across — which is a large part of why ferns live in damp places even when the grown plant looks perfectly tough."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"found","text":"New fronds come up rolled into a tight spiral and open from the outside in. The shape has a name in New Zealand — koru — and it turns up all over New Zealand carving and design."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"New Zealand's silver fern, Cyathea dealbata, grows to 33 ft (10 m) with fronds up to 10 ft (3 m) long and 3 ft (1 m) wide, silver-white underneath. A New Zealand side wore it touring Britain in 1888, and it has been the country's emblem ever since.","more":"Silver ferns appear on the national coat of arms, on military badges and war memorials, and on the shirts of the All Blacks and the Silver Ferns netball side. It started with the 1888-89 New Zealand Native football team, who wore it touring Britain."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"record","text":"Maori used that silver underside as a road sign. Fronds laid silver-side-up along a track catch moonlight, so a party walking back after dark can see the way home."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Tree ferns grow in tropical rainforest and in the cool wet forests of Australia and New Zealand. Dicksonia antarctica needs at least 20 in (500 mm) of rain a year and grows from sea level up to about 3,300 ft (1,000 m)."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Some tree ferns come back from the trunk itself. Dicksonia squarrosa and D. youngiae push out new plants from offsets at the base and from pups along the standing trunk, so one plant slowly becomes a clump."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names grass, seed-bearing herbs and fruit trees. A tree fern is none of those and looks like one of them anyway: tree-shaped, without wood, without a flower, without a seed. There is more than one way to build something that stands 60 ft up."}]},{"id":"bracken","name":"Bracken","group":"ferns","blurb":"A coarse fern that spreads by underground stems and takes whole hillsides — one of the most widespread plants there is.","size":"Fronds stand 1 to 3.5 ft (0.3 to 1 m) tall over a rhizome that can run 50 ft (15 m) sideways and 11 ft (3.5 m) down.","homes":["forest","grassland","mountain"],"stats":{"height":3},"wonder":"Most of the plant is under your feet. The part you can see is the small part.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The fronds are the small end of it. Bracken's rhizome — its underground stem — is about 2 in (5 cm) thick, runs up to 50 ft (15 m) sideways, and can sit 11 ft (3.5 m) below the surface."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"One bracken clone at Raakkyla in Finland measures 1,604 ft (489 m) across. That is a single plant nearly a third of a mile wide, all of it joined up underground.","more":"Its age was worked out rather than watched: run the measured spreading rate back across 1,604 ft and the answer comes to more than 1,400 years. Individual rhizomes are reckoned to last up to 72 years, so the plant has replaced its own parts many times over while staying one plant."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Bracken grows on every continent except Antarctica and ranks about fifth among the most widely distributed weeds in the world. If you have walked in the countryside anywhere temperate, you have walked past it."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Bracken is poisonous. It contains ptaquiloside, which causes cancer, and cattle and sheep that eat enough of it get seriously ill. It also contains thiaminase, which destroys vitamin B1 in horses that graze on it."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Almost nothing grazes bracken, and that is the point. The read is that the poisons are a defence, and it works: a hillside of bracken is a hillside grazing animals walk around, which leaves the bracken more room."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"People eat it anyway. In Japan young bracken shoots are warabi, and warabimochi is a chilled dessert made from them. In Korea the same shoots are gosari — soaked, parboiled and stir-fried.","more":"The poison dissolves in water and boiling breaks down nearly all of it, which is why every traditional recipe starts with soaking and boiling. In parts of Japan and in north Wales, where young bracken has been eaten, stomach cancer rates run high."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"No flowers and no seeds — bracken makes spores under the rolled edges of its fronds. But most new bracken never involves a spore at all. It is the rhizome, creeping sideways underground and pushing up another frond.","more":"Bracken does still make spores, in enormous numbers, and they blow a long way. Almost none of them succeed: a spore has to land somewhere damp and bare and stay damp long enough to make a prothallus. The rhizome does not have to gamble like that."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A bracken hillside can be one plant. What looks like thousands of separate ferns is a single rhizome network with fronds sent up through the soil at intervals, like periscopes."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Burn bracken off and it comes straight back. The rhizome sits feet below the surface where a grass fire cannot reach it, and it pushes up fresh fronds from food stored down there."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The fronds are triangular and cut into leaflets, and in cold places they die back brown every autumn into a deep springy litter. New ones come up the next spring, rolled tight like a bishop's crook."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Bracken spreads two ways at very different speeds. Spores can travel miles on the wind and hardly ever take. The rhizome moves a few feet a year and almost always does. The read is that the slow way is the one that matters."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three names the plants that yield seed. Bracken yields none. It is out on the same earth doing the same work — covering ground, holding soil, dying back and coming again — with a completely different set of parts."}]},{"id":"horsetail","name":"Horsetail","group":"ferns","blurb":"A jointed, hollow-stemmed plant stiffened with real grit, which people used for centuries to scour pots and polish wood.","size":"Most are knee-high. Equisetum myriochaetum in Central America reaches about 26 ft (8 m).","homes":["freshwater","forest","grassland"],"stats":{"height":26},"wonder":"A plant that loads sand into its own walls, and then people borrowed it as sandpaper.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Rub a horsetail stem and it is rough, like fine sandpaper. The plant loads silica — the stuff of sand and glass — into its stem. That is not dust picked up off the ground. The plant put it there."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"That grit is why horsetail was used for scouring pots and mugs. In English the unbranched kinds are still called scouring rush, and in Japan the boiled and dried stems give the final polish on woodwork."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Horsetail stems are used to shape the reeds of clarinets and saxophones. A plant is doing a job that would otherwise need a file, on an instrument that costs rather more than the plant."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The stems are hollow and jointed, ridged along their length with anything from 3 to about 40 ridges, and they pull apart at the joints with a small pop. The leaves are fused into a collar at each joint.","more":"Pull a horsetail apart at a joint and you get a clean socket, like a tent pole. Every joint carries a ring of tiny fused leaves, and the branches, when there are any, come out in a whorl from the same ring. It is one design repeated all the way up."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"No flowers and no seeds. Spores form in a cone at the tip of the stem, and each spore has four ribbons attached to it, called elaters, which coil and uncoil as the air gets damper or drier."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Those four ribbons make the spore move. As they open and shut the spore crawls and hops across a surface. Put horsetail spores under a lens and change the humidity, and the whole pile visibly shifts."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Equisetum giganteum in South America reaches 16 ft (5 m), and Equisetum myriochaetum in Central America about 26 ft (8 m). Most horsetails are knee-high; those two are the exceptions."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Only 18 species of horsetail are generally accepted, and every one is in the genus Equisetum. It is the only living genus in its family — and the only genus in a whole subclass. There is nothing else quite like it alive.","more":"That is a strikingly small group for a plant this widespread. The 18 species between them cover most of the northern half of the world plus a good deal of South America, and they hybridise readily, which is part of why counting them is awkward."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Horsetails grow in wet ground: ditches, riverbanks, damp fields, railway cuttings and gravel. Common horsetail is a serious garden weed, because it comes back from rhizomes buried well below the depth of a spade."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Grazing animals mostly leave horsetail alone. The read is the grit: a mouthful of silica wears teeth down and is hard to get anything out of, so an animal with a choice picks something softer."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"In some horsetails the spore-bearing stem comes up first in spring, pale brown with no green on it anywhere. It does its job, dies back, and only then do the green stems appear. The same plant sends up two different shoots."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three sorted the waters from the dry land. Horsetail lives right on that line — roots in the mud, stem in the air — and it has never needed a flower, a seed, or a leaf worth the name to do it."}]},{"id":"lichen","name":"Lichen","group":"moss","blurb":"Not a plant at all: a fungus and an alga living as one organism, growing on bare rock where nothing else will start.","size":"From a paint-like crust you could cover with a thumbnail to shaggy grey tufts hanging a foot long off a branch.","homes":["mountain","polar","forest","desert"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Two living things from two different kingdoms, together making a third thing that neither could be alone, on bare stone.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A lichen is not one organism doing two jobs. It is a fungus and an alga — sometimes a cyanobacterium — living inside one body. The fungus makes the structure and catches the water; the alga inside it makes the food. Pull them apart and neither one is a lichen."},{"cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"Nobody worked this out until 1867, when a Swiss botanist called Simon Schwendener said a lichen was two organisms and not one. The lichen specialists of the day mostly refused to believe him. He turned out to be right."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Fungi are not plants. About one in five of all known fungus species lives as a lichen, which means a very large slice of the fungus kingdom has taken on a green partner and never let go."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A crust lichen spreads by 1 to 2 mm a year, and plenty manage less than one. A patch the size of a saucer has been sitting on that rock since long before anyone now alive."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Because lichens grow at a steady measurable rate, you can date a bare rock surface by measuring the biggest lichen on it. Map lichens on East Baffin Island come out at roughly 9,500 years old on that basis. It is arithmetic, not a birth certificate."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Lichens grow on bare rock, on gravestones, on roof tiles, on fence wire and on tree bark, in deserts and on Antarctic stone. They are not parasites on the trees they sit on — the bark is a perch, not a meal."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"In 2005 two lichen species rode a Soyuz rocket into orbit and were held in open space — vacuum, unfiltered sunlight, cosmic radiation — for fifteen days. Brought home, they were photosynthesising as though nothing had happened.","more":"The lichens went out with no protection at all: no air, no shielding from ultraviolet, and temperatures swinging wildly every time the craft passed into shadow. The result matched what they already do on a mountaintop. A lichen shuts down and waits, and space is just another dry spell."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"A lichen dries out completely. It has no way to hold water in and does not try; it goes brittle, shuts down and waits, sometimes for months. Wet it and it swells and starts working again."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Reindeer and caribou get through northern winters largely on lichen, digging it out from under the snow. Cladonia rangiferina is called reindeer moss, which is wrong twice over: it is not a moss, and it is not one organism.","more":"It grows 3 to 11 mm a year. A lichen range that has been overgrazed, burnt or trampled can take decades to come back, which is why reindeer herds keep moving instead of eating one patch out."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Litmus paper — the strip that goes red in acid and blue in alkali — is made from lichen. The dye comes from Roccella and its relatives, and a Catalan physician, Arnaldus de Villa Nova, was using it to test acids around the year 1300.","more":"Litmus is not one chemical but a mixture pulled out of the lichen with ammonia and lime. It changes colour between about pH 4.5 and 8.3, so it will tell you acid from alkali but not much more than that. For anything finer, chemists use other indicators."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Lichens make travel bundles: soredia, a few algal cells wrapped in fungal threads and blown off as dust, and isidia, small spiny outgrowths that snap off. Either way, both partners leave together in the same parcel."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three is when the earth brought forth green growing things. A lichen fits that line strangely. The green half is an alga, the other half is a fungus, and neither on its own is a lichen. It only ever turns up as a pair."}]},{"id":"moss","name":"Common moss","group":"moss","blurb":"A plant with no flowers, no seeds and no proper roots, which covers damp ground, walls and rock in a low green mat.","size":"Most mosses stand between a tenth of an inch and 4 in (0.2 to 10 cm) tall. The tallest of all reaches about 20 in (50 cm).","homes":["forest","backyard","mountain","polar"],"stats":{},"wonder":"No roots, no flowers, no seeds — and it still carpets more cold wet ground than anything else growing.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Moss is a plant, but it has no flowers, no seeds and no real roots. The hairs holding it down are called rhizoids and they are only for grip. Water and food go in through the leaves, across the whole surface at once."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"There are about 12,000 species of moss, and most of them stand between 0.2 and 10 cm high — a tenth of an inch to four inches. A moss forest is ankle-deep at best."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The tallest moss is Dawsonia superba of New Zealand, Australia and New Guinea, with a stem up to about 20 in (50 cm). It manages it by having internal plumbing — strands of cells carrying water up and food down — which most mosses do without."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Moss makes spores, not seeds. The green cushion sends up a bare stalk with a capsule on top, capped by a small hood, and the whole arrangement is a spore factory standing on a stem."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Around the mouth of the capsule sit teeth — usually sixteen of them, made from the walls of dead cells. They fold in to close it and fold back to open it, so the spores go out gradually instead of all at once.","more":"The teeth are made of dead cell wall, so the capsule is not spending energy on the mechanism. They are shaped to move as the air around them dries and dampens, which is a way of releasing spores on a dry day, when they will actually travel, rather than into wet still air."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"A moss can dry to a brown crisp and stay that way for months. Water it and it turns green and starts working again within hours. It does not fight drying out. It just stops."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Moss grows in the cracks between paving stones, on roofs, on walls, on gravestones and on tree bark. It does not need soil, because it has no roots to put in soil."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"People say moss grows on the north side of a tree. In the northern half of the world it does tend to be thicker there, but the reason is shade and damp, not north. On a slope, or beside a wall, or under other trees, the rule falls apart.","more":"It is a useful lesson about rules of thumb. The moss is not reading a compass. It is sitting wherever stays wet longest, and plenty of things besides the sun decide where that is."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"Dried moss was packed between the logs of cabins in Russia and the Nordic countries as insulation, and tribes in the north-eastern United States and south-eastern Canada used it to fill the gaps in wooden longhouses.","more":"Dry moss is mostly air trapped in small spaces, which is exactly what an insulator needs to be, and it can be stuffed into an awkward gap and stay there. It also swells when it gets damp, which helps close the joint rather than open it."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"One old group of mosses, Takakia, pairs up with fungi the way most flowering plants do at their roots — the fungus feeds it minerals, the moss feeds the fungus sugar. It is the only moss group known to do it."},{"cat":"home","kind":"worked","text":"Moss on bare rock is the start of soil. It holds water against the stone, grit and dust collect in it, and the surface crumbles faster than it would bare. The read is that moss goes first and everything else follows it."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Day three had the earth bring forth green growing things. Moss is the plainest version of that there is: no flower, no fruit, no seed, no roots. Just green, coming up true to kind from a speck you cannot see."}]},{"id":"fly-agaric","name":"Fly agaric","group":"fungi","blurb":"The storybook toadstool — scarlet cap, white flecks, growing under birches and pines — and not a plant at all.","size":"Cap 2 to 12 in (5–30 cm) across, on a white stalk up to 8 in (20 cm) tall.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Every scarlet cap is wired to a tree root underground, and the trade running through that joint feeds them both.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A fungus is not a plant. There are no leaves, no green anywhere, and it never makes a scrap of food out of sunlight. What you see is only the fruiting body, the spore-making part. The fungus itself is a web of fine threads spread through the soil.","more":"Fungi are their own kingdom, neither plant nor animal. They feed by pushing digestive chemicals out into whatever they are sitting in and absorbing the results — eating the way a stomach would if it were turned inside out and buried."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The white flecks are not painted on. A young fly agaric comes up wrapped in a white skin, and as the cap swells that skin tears into patches which sit on top like crumbs. A few days of heavy rain washes them off and leaves a plain red cap.","more":"This catches people out. A rain-washed fly agaric with no warts left has been mistaken for edible red-capped mushrooms more than once. The white cup at the base of the stalk is the rest of that same skin, and it stays."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Dig one up carefully and the threads are wrapped tight around the fine roots of birch, pine, spruce or oak. The best read of that is a trade: the fungus gathers water and minerals from a far wider reach of soil than roots manage, and takes sugar the tree made in its leaves.","more":"Most trees in a forest are joined to fungi like this. The joint is called a mycorrhiza, which just means fungus-root. A tree cut off from its fungi grows badly even in good soil."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The poisons are ibotenic acid and muscimol, both extracted from the mushroom in a laboratory. They work on the brain — sickness, twitching, sleepiness, and seeing things that are not there. About 6 mg of muscimol is enough to affect a grown adult.","more":"Ibotenic acid turns into muscimol inside the body, which is why the effects arrive late — usually half an hour to an hour and a half after eating, long after the meal seems to have gone fine."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"For a long time the blame fell on a chemical called muscarine, which is named after this very mushroom. It turned out there is barely any muscarine in it — far too little to explain the poisoning. The real culprits were only sorted out later."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The name is a job description. In parts of Germany and eastern Europe people broke the cap into a saucer of milk to kill flies, and the mushroom has been called the fly-killer ever since."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"record","text":"Deadly is the wrong word for it. The North American Mycological Association says there is no reliable record of anyone dying from these mushrooms' toxins in the last hundred years. Still poisonous, still a hospital trip — but the storybook one-bite-kills is not true.","more":"That is not permission to eat one. Poisoning is unpleasant and lasts hours, and children are smaller than the doses were measured on. The point is only that the fairy-tale version and the real one are different."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Lay a cap gills-down on dark paper overnight and lift it in the morning: a white shadow of the gills is left behind. That dust is spores, and white is the print colour for a fly agaric. It is one of the checks that tells one mushroom from another."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"It belongs to the cool north — Europe, Asia, North America. It is now growing wild in Australia, New Zealand, South Africa and South America too, none of which is where it started.","more":"It cannot live without a tree partner, so it almost certainly arrived with one: imported pine and birch seedlings shipped out with soil still round the roots, and the fungus already in it."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The cap starts as a rounded ball, opens out flat like a plate, and on a big one measures a foot (30 cm) across. The stalk is white, wears a ring of skin like a collar, and sits in a lumpy cup at the base.","more":"The cup at the base is the giveaway. Anyone learning mushrooms is taught to dig the whole stalk out rather than pick it, because the bit that tells you what you have is often the bit underground."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis has one Maker behind the whole living world, and calls the finished work very good. A fly agaric belongs to that world: bright red, no use as food, and joined by its threads to trees that would gather far less from the soil without it."}]},{"id":"honey-fungus","name":"Honey fungus","group":"fungi","blurb":"A tree-killing fungus that spreads underground on black bootlaces, and in Oregon has grown into one of the largest single living things known.","size":"The mushrooms are hand-sized. The organism they grow out of covers 3.5 square miles (9.1 km²) of forest.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{},"wonder":"The whole of it is one individual — a single living thing laid out across an entire mountainside, holding quietly together.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Honey fungus is a fungus, not a plant. Nothing about it is green and it makes no food from sunlight. The honey-coloured mushrooms that come up in clumps at the foot of a tree are only the fruit. The fungus itself is threads running through soil and wood."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"In the Malheur National Forest in eastern Oregon, one honey fungus covers 3.5 square miles (9.1 km²) — some 2,200 acres, or about 1,600 football fields. It is one of the largest single organisms anyone has found anywhere.","more":"Area is the fair way to measure it. It is not a solid block — it is a network threading through soil and roots, with living forest on top, so nothing about it looks like one organism from the surface."},{"cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody can see the whole thing. Samples were taken from trees right across the forest and their DNA compared, and they all matched. That match is the argument that this is one individual rather than hundreds of separate fungi growing side by side."},{"cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"It has never been weighed and never will be — it is spread through soil, roots and dead wood across a mountainside. The estimate, worked out from how much fungus a measured patch of ground holds, comes to roughly 35,000 tons."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"It travels on black cords called rhizomorphs, tough as bootlaces, running under bark and through soil from one tree to the next. Peel the bark off an infected trunk and there they are: flat, black and branching like a map of roads.","more":"Rhizomorphs are the reason it can cross open ground. A fungus that could only creep through wood would stop at the last root of an infected tree. These cords carry it on to the next tree with nothing in between."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Most fungi eat wood that is already dead. Honey fungus goes after living trees, killing the layer just under the bark, and Douglas firs, pines and hemlocks die standing up. Gardeners rank it among the worst things that can arrive in a garden."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Wood shot through with honey fungus glows. The light is faint, greenish and steady, and you have to sit in a properly dark wood with your eyes adjusted to see it. It comes from the fungal threads, not from the wood. People have called it foxfire for a long time.","more":"Foxfire has been reported for centuries by people working in woods at night. Rotting logs that glow were the first clue that something living, and not the wood, was doing it."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The mushrooms come up in tight crowded clumps in autumn, honey-brown, with a shaggy ring around the stalk and gills running a little way down it. Lay one on dark paper and the spore print is white."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Honey fungus is not rare or exotic. Its close relatives grow in woods, gardens and hedges across Europe, Asia and North America. The Oregon one is not a different creature — it is an ordinary fungus that got an unusually long run at an unusually large forest."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"A fallen tree does not disappear on its own. Almost nothing can digest wood, and fungi are the ones that can. The best read of a forest floor that stays clear is that fungi like this one take the fallen trunks apart and hand the pieces back to the soil.","more":"Wood is mostly cellulose and lignin, and lignin in particular is very hard to break down. Fungi are among the few things with the chemistry for it, which is why they get the job almost everywhere."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis has a Maker who made the living world and called it good — and the taking-apart is built in as carefully as the growing. A fungus that turns a dead tree back into soil is doing work the forest cannot manage without."}]},{"id":"stinkhorn","name":"Stinkhorn","group":"fungi","blurb":"A fungus that smells like rotting meat on purpose, because it sends its spores out by fly instead of by wind.","size":"Starts as a white egg buried in leaf litter and pushes up a stalk taller than your hand inside an hour.","homes":["forest","backyard"],"stats":{},"wonder":"The smell, the slime and the speed all solve one problem, and the problem is a fly.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Stinkhorns are fungi, not plants: no leaves, no green, no food made out of sunlight. The stalk you see is only the fruiting part, up for a day or two. The fungus itself lives all year as threads running through leaf litter and rotting wood."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"It is one of the fastest-growing things ever measured. The stalk pushes up about 5 mm a minute — a fifth of an inch — which is quick enough to see against a marked stick. People who have sat with one report a faint crackling as it goes.","more":"That works out at roughly a foot an hour if it kept going, which it does not — the whole performance is over in a morning, and by afternoon the flies have taken the slime and left a bare white stalk."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"It is not really growing, and that is how it manages the speed. The cells are already made and folded up inside the egg. The stalk shoots up by pumping them full of water and unfolding them — closer to inflating a tent than to building one."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The slime on the cap smells of dead animal and dung, and chemists have pulled the smell apart into its parts. Among them are oligosulfides, indole and p-cresol — the same compounds that come off carrion and manure."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Most fungi drop their spores and let the wind take them. A stinkhorn packs its spores into sticky slime and stinks instead. Flies land, feed, and leave carrying spores on their feet and in their guts — that much you can watch. That the smell is there to fetch them is the obvious read.","more":"You can test the idea cheaply. Cover a fresh stinkhorn with a jar and the slime sits there for days. Leave it open and the flies strip the cap clean within hours."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"It starts underground as a white egg with a rubbery skin. Cut one open and the whole mushroom is already in there, folded up in a layer of clear jelly, waiting for water.","more":"The jelly is a water store. Once the egg splits, the stalk has one shot at inflating itself, and if the soil is dry there is no second chance — which is why stinkhorns appear after rain."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"The egg stage is eaten in some countries — crisp and crunchy, with a taste people compare to radish. Once the stalk is up and the slime is out, nobody wants anything to do with it."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"One relative, the octopus stinkhorn, skips the tall stalk. It splits its egg and opens into a handful of scarlet arms lying flat on the ground, smeared with black spore slime. Its other name is devil's fingers, which is fair enough."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"It is a copy with no original. Nothing has died — there is no meat, no carcass, nothing for a fly to lay eggs on. The fungus manufactures the smell of a dead animal and collects the visitors a dead animal would have got, without ever having to be one."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A tropical relative, the veiled lady, hangs a white lace net from under its cap that drops most of the way to the ground. The stinkhorn family runs to around 77 species, most of them in the tropics."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Look in wood chips, garden mulch, leaf litter and old stumps, in late summer and autumn. You will normally smell one before you see it, which is the only mushroom in the wood you can say that about."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"The Maker's world runs on things being taken apart as well as put together, and a stinkhorn's work is rot. Even the rot has been given a delivery service, and the delivery service has wings."}]},{"id":"puffball","name":"Giant puffball","group":"fungi","blurb":"A smooth white ball the size of a football that sits in a meadow and, when it ripens, turns entirely into spore dust.","size":"4 to 20 in (10–50 cm) across. The heaviest on record, from Thunder Bay in Ontario, weighed 51 lb (23 kg).","homes":["grassland","forest","backyard"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Seven quintillion spores, and almost none of them will land anywhere useful. The design is not careful; it is extravagant.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A puffball is a fungus, not a plant. No green, no leaves, no food made from sunlight. The ball is the fruiting body; the fungus that made it is a mat of threads through the soil, and that mat has usually been there for years."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"They run from 4 to 20 in (10–50 cm) across, and the big ones look wrong in a field — too white, too round, too much like something dropped there. One found at Thunder Bay in Ontario weighed 51 lb (23 kg)."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A large giant puffball holds about seven quintillion spores — a 7 with eighteen zeroes after it. That is getting on for a billion spores in one puffball for every person alive on earth.","more":"Spores are single cells, a few thousandths of a millimetre across. That is small enough to drift on the faintest air current, which is exactly the point of making the ball and then letting it fall apart."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"While it is swelling, a giant puffball builds about sixty million new cells a minute. It is one of the fastest bits of building anything alive does, and it happens in a field with nobody watching."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A ripe one falls apart at a touch. The white paste inside has gone to brown powder, the skin has thinned to paper, and a kick sends up a cloud like a bag of soot. That cloud is the spores leaving."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Young ones are eaten — sliced thick and fried like bread. The rule is that the inside must be pure white all the way through. Yellow, or greenish, means the spores have started and it will make you ill.","more":"Cut it in half before cooking, every time. That single habit rules out both the poisonous look-alikes and an overripe puffball at once, and takes about four seconds."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The dangerous mix-up is a young Amanita, which is also a white ball at first because the whole mushroom comes up wrapped in a skin. So cut any white ball in half from top to bottom. A puffball is even white paste throughout. An Amanita has a tiny mushroom drawn inside it.","more":"The technical name for that wrapping is a universal veil. It is the same structure that leaves the white flecks on a fly agaric — which is another good reason to know it exists."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The other look-alike is the earthball: firmer, elastic, with a thick rind, and dark purple-black inside while it is still young. It is poisonous, and the cut test tells them apart in a second."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Meadows, pasture, field edges and open broadleaved woods, in late summer and autumn, in temperate places all over the world. A patch that produced one this year will often produce one again in the same spot."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"There are no gills, no stalk and no cap. The whole thing is a bag. Spores are made inside and stay shut in until the skin breaks — the opposite of an ordinary mushroom, which builds a roof and drops its spores out from underneath."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Nearly every one of those spores lands somewhere hopeless: a road, a roof, a pond, another spore. Making seven quintillion is the answer to those odds. At that number, a handful landing well is plenty, and a handful is all it needs."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Nothing in the design is stingy. One puffball carries more spores than a person could count in a lifetime, for the sake of the few that will take — which is the same generous arithmetic running right through the living world the Maker called good."}]},{"id":"bracket-fungus","name":"Bracket fungus","group":"fungi","blurb":"Shelves of fungus growing straight out of tree trunks, some as hard as the wood itself, adding a fresh layer every year.","size":"Most are hand- to dinner-plate-sized. An artist's conk can reach 30 in (75 cm) across, and the largest brackets weigh hundreds of pounds.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"life":80},"wonder":"A shelf of fungus on a dead trunk is the forest taking itself apart so that it can start again.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Bracket fungi are not plants, and they are not part of the tree either. They are fungi — no green, no leaves, no food made from sunlight. The living fungus is threads inside the wood; the shelf on the outside is only the spore factory."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Turn one over. Instead of gills there are pores — thousands of tiny tube mouths packed together like a honeycomb, and the spores fall out of the tubes. Some kinds have pores 5 mm wide. Others fit fifteen tubes into a millimetre and look perfectly smooth."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A patch of artist's conk just 4 in by 4 in (10 by 10 cm) can release 4.65 billion spores in a single day. A large bracket has many times that area, and it keeps going for months.","more":"Nearly all of those spores land on ground with no wood on it and come to nothing. The tiny fraction that land on fresh dead timber are the ones the whole arrangement is built for."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Some brackets are perennial: they live on year after year, adding a fresh layer of tubes underneath each season. The layers show as rings on top and can be counted the way tree rings are. Fruiting bodies of one kind have passed 80 years old.","more":"The rings are not a perfect calendar — a bad season may add very little, and two flushes in one year can confuse the count. Read like tree rings, with the same caution, they are still the best record the fungus keeps."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"The underside of an artist's conk is white and bruises dark brown wherever it is scratched. Draw on it with a nail and the lines stay; once the bracket dries out, the picture is permanent. That is exactly where the name came from."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Tinder fungus has been carried as fire-lighting kit for a very long time. The man found frozen in the Alps and nicknamed Ötzi had pieces of it in his gear. A spongy layer inside the bracket takes a spark and holds a slow ember you can carry.","more":"The spongy layer is called amadou. Prepared properly it takes a spark from flint and steel and smoulders rather than flaming, which is what you want when you are carrying fire rather than starting one."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"These are the best wood-eaters there are. Some digest lignin, the stiff brown glue that makes wood hard, and leave a pale stringy mess called white rot. Others leave the lignin alone, eat the cellulose, and the wood goes brown and crumbles into little cubes.","more":"Brown-rotted wood ends up as reddish cubes that crumble in the hand. White-rotted wood goes pale and stringy and feels wet. Once you know the two, you can read what killed a log from a few feet away."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Wood is very hard to eat and almost nothing else can manage it. Without these fungi, fallen trunks would simply pile up and stay piled. Nobody has run that experiment on a whole forest — it is a conclusion drawn from who else is capable, which is nearly nobody."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"A bracket ends up level however far the trunk leans. Spores have to drop straight down out of the tubes, so a tilted set of tubes would never empty. The shape is not proof of anything on its own, but it is a very good argument."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Chicken of the woods grows in bright orange and yellow shelves on oaks and is eaten; cooked, it really does have the texture of chicken. Turkey tail, thin and banded in rings of brown and grey, is one of the commonest fungi in any wood."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Many brackets are genuinely woody. You cannot pull one off a trunk — it takes a knife or a saw. Old ones sit through frost, drought and being climbed on, and are still there seasons later, still adding layers."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"The Maker built the taking-apart with as much care as the growing. A tree that comes down is not wasted: a shelf of fungus is already at work on it, handing the wood back to the soil for whatever grows next."}]},{"id":"truffle","name":"Truffle","group":"fungi","blurb":"A fungus that fruits underground, smells strongly enough to be dug up, and is the most expensive food on earth by weight.","size":"Most are between a walnut and a fist. A white truffle found in Umbria weighed 4 lb 3 oz (1.89 kg).","homes":["forest"],"stats":{},"wonder":"It never sees daylight and has no way to drop a spore, so it built a smell instead — a signal aimed at a nose it will never meet.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A truffle is a fungus, not a plant and not a root. It is the fruiting body, the spore-making part, and unlike nearly every other mushroom it never comes above ground at all. The rest of the fungus is threads wrapped around tree roots.","more":"That is the whole oddity. Every other mushroom builds a structure to lift its spores into moving air. A truffle builds nothing of the kind, because it never plans to be in the air at all."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"worked","text":"Those threads sheathe the fine roots of oak, hazel, beech, poplar and pine, and the sheath is plain to see under a lens. The read is a trade: the fungus reaches water and minerals from far more soil than a root can, and lives on sugar from the tree's leaves in return."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"They are found by nose. Pigs were the old method and are superb at it, but a pig that finds a truffle wants to eat it and wrecks the fungal threads digging. Italy banned truffle pigs in 1985. Dogs are trained instead — they find it and then do not care.","more":"A good truffle dog is worth a great deal of money and its training is guarded. Hunters work at dawn or after dark, partly for the dog's comfort and partly so nobody follows them to the trees."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"For years the story was that pigs were smelling androstenol, a pig scent. Work published in 1990 pinned it on something else: dimethyl sulfide, a sulphur gas. That is what the dog and the pig are actually following down through the soil."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"A mushroom above ground lets its spores fall away. A truffle cannot do that. Voles, squirrels, deer and wild boar dig them up and eat them, and the spores come out somewhere else entirely. The smell being so strong is the argument that it is the whole dispersal system."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"White truffles from around Alba in Piedmont are the dearest food on earth by weight. In December 2007 the Macau businessman Stanley Ho paid $330,000 for a single truffle weighing 3 lb 5 oz (1.5 kg). In 2021 an 830-gram Piedmont truffle sold for €103,000.","more":"Prices swing hard with the weather. A dry autumn means few truffles and the price climbs; a wet one brings the market down. Nothing about the supply can be planned, which is half the reason it costs what it does."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"In 2014 a white truffle of 1.89 kg from Umbria was auctioned at Sotheby's in New York for $61,000. In November 1999 one weighing 2 lb 14 oz (1.31 kg), found near Buje in Croatia, went into the Guinness Book of Records."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"They are not cooked. A white truffle is shaved raw over hot pasta or eggs in slices thin enough to see light through, and the heat of the plate lifts the smell. A few shavings scent the whole dish."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Truffle orchards do exist. Hazel and oak seedlings are grown with the fungus already on their roots and then planted out — and after that you wait. It takes 7 to 10 years for the underground network to build before there is any chance of fruit, and some orchards never produce.","more":"That is a long time to wait on something that may not happen. Growers plant hazel because it fruits sooner than oak, and even then a plantation is a bet made a decade before it can be settled."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Black Périgord truffles can be farmed reliably. The Italian white one has beaten almost every attempt: it wants a particular soil, a particular dampness and a particular set of trees, and outside that it simply does not appear. Most white truffles sold are still hunted wild."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The famous ones come from Piedmont in north-west Italy, around Alba and Asti, and from Istria on the Adriatic coast. Truffles of one sort or another grow in woods across Europe, North America, Australia and China — some worth a fortune, most worth nothing."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"It is a strange piece of design: a fruit that never sees the light, and a smell built to travel up through a foot of packed soil to a nose. None of it was aimed at us. We simply learned to follow the signal the voles were already following."}]},{"id":"mimosa","name":"Sensitive plant","group":"odd","blurb":"A small tropical weed whose leaves fold shut the instant you touch them, and open again a few minutes later.","size":"Low and sprawling, with fern-like leaves and pink flower heads under half an inch (8–10 mm) across.","homes":["grassland","rainforest","backyard"],"stats":{},"wonder":"No nerves, no muscles, nothing like a brain — and it still answers a touch faster than you can pull your hand away.","facts":[{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Brush a leaf and the leaflets snap shut in pairs, running outward from the exact spot you touched, and then the whole leaf stalk drops. It takes a second or two. Warmth, a puff of breath or a knock on the pot will all set it off."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"There is no muscle anywhere in it. At the base of each leaflet sits a swollen joint called a pulvinus, full of cells held stiff by water. On the signal, the cells on one side let their water go and collapse, and the leaflet falls over the joint.","more":"You can prove there is no muscle by drying a leaf. A dried pulvinus cannot fold anything. The whole mechanism is water under pressure, and the moment the water is gone so is the movement."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"The signal travels. It runs along the leaf from the point you touched, which is why the leaflets shut in order rather than all at once. It moves as a pulse of electricity — the same kind of pulse an animal's nerve uses, in a plant with no nerves at all.","more":"The pulse is called an action potential. Animals use them to run nerves and muscles. Finding the same electrical trick in a plant with nothing resembling a nervous system is one of the odder results in botany."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Nothing pushes the leaf back up. The joints slowly refill with water and the leaf lifts itself again over a few minutes. Watching it reopen takes far more patience than knocking it down did."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Monica Gagliano dropped potted sensitive plants a short distance, over and over. After enough drops they stopped folding. Then she shook them instead — and they folded fully. So it was not exhaustion. They had sorted a harmless drop from something that might matter.","more":"Habituation is a proper term in animal learning: ignoring a signal that keeps turning out to be harmless. Showing it in a plant caused an argument that has not entirely finished."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Once a plant had learned to ignore the drop, it still ignored it 28 days later. Plants kept in dim light learned quicker than plants in bright light, which is not what anyone expected."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It also folds up every night with nobody touching it, and opens again in the morning. That is a different movement from the touch one, following light and dark rather than contact, and it runs on its own schedule."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Why do it at all? A study in 2022 found the folding does protect the plant from insects chewing on it — a leaf that collapses is harder to sit on and eat. Another reading is that folding cuts the sun the leaf catches and saves water. Both could be right.","more":"Folding costs the plant sunlight, so it is not free. Whatever the folding buys has to be worth more than the food the leaf could have made while it was open — which is a useful way to think about any plant behaviour."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seed pods come in clusters of two to eight, each about half to three-quarters of an inch (1–2 cm) long, with bristly prickles along both edges. Those prickles catch on fur, socks and trouser legs, and that is how the plant gets about."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It belongs to the tropical Americas and has since spread right round the tropics. In Tanzania, in southern and south-east Asia and on many Pacific islands it is now a weed, taking over pasture and cropland where nobody planted it."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the plants on day three, each yielding seed after its kind. A plant that answers a fingertip in one second, with nothing in it that resembles a nerve or a muscle, is a fair place to start wondering how carefully that day was thought through."}]},{"id":"bamboo","name":"Bamboo","group":"odd","blurb":"A grass that grows as tall as a building, and then a whole forest of it flowers on the same day and dies.","size":"The largest kind reaches 151 ft (46 m), with stems 14 in (36 cm) thick weighing 1,000 lb (450 kg) each.","homes":["forest","rainforest","mountain"],"stats":{"height":151,"weight":1000},"wonder":"A hundred and fifty feet of hollow tube, built by a grass, and most of it goes up in a single season.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Bamboo is a grass. Not a tree — the same family as wheat, barley and the stuff in a lawn, only enormous. There is no bark and no rings inside. The stem is a hollow tube, divided into segments by solid walls at every joint."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"It is the fastest-growing plant ever measured. A Japanese timber bamboo has been recorded putting on 36 in (91 cm) in 24 hours — nearly 1.5 in (40 mm) every hour. Mark a stem in the morning and there is measurably more of it by lunchtime.","more":"Growth like that is possible because the segments are already formed inside the shoot and simply extend, one after another, filling with water as they go. The building was done underground the season before."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The largest bamboo, Dendrocalamus sinicus, reaches 151 ft (46 m) — about the height of a fifteen-storey building. One stem is 14 in (36 cm) across and can weigh 1,000 lb (450 kg), which takes several people to shift."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"A bamboo stem comes out of the ground at its final thickness and never gets any fatter. It only extends, segment by segment, like a telescope pulling open. That is a large part of why it can go up so fast — none of the effort is spent on getting wider.","more":"It also explains the sound. A bamboo grove on a hot day creaks and cracks as segments stretch against each other, which is a genuinely unsettling thing to stand in the middle of."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"record","text":"Most bamboos hardly ever flower. Phyllostachys bambusoides goes roughly 120 years between flowerings — and then every plant grown from the same stock flowers at once, wherever in the world it has been shipped to. Afterwards they all die.","more":"A flowering wipes out a bamboo forest and the industries built on it. Growers who bought stock from the same parent plant lose everything at once, wherever they are, because the clock was set before the cuttings were taken."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"worked","text":"Flowering kills it. The plant pours everything it has into seed and then dies back, roots and all. The usual read on why they synchronise: a sudden glut of seed is far more than the birds and rodents can eat, so a share of it survives to grow."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"In the hills of Mizoram in north-east India, bamboo flowering brings famine. The seed feeds an explosion of rats, and when the seed runs out the rats move into the rice fields. It comes round on a long and predictable cycle, and it has its own name: mautam.","more":"The pattern is well recorded because people plan for it. Local governments store grain and set rat bounties ahead of a flowering, on the strength of the last one."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Bamboo pulls harder than it looks. Bambusa tulda has measured 60,000 psi (400 MPa) in tension, which is in the range of structural steel — from a hollow plant stem light enough to carry on one shoulder.","more":"Steel is far denser, so a steel bar of equal strength is much heavier. That ratio — strength for weight — is why bamboo keeps turning up in bridges, houses and scaffolding wherever it grows."},{"cat":"copied","kind":"record","text":"Hong Kong still puts up scaffolding made of bamboo, lashed together with nylon ties, running the full height of skyscrapers. Nothing else is as light for its strength or as quick to assemble, and crews build it faster than steel goes up."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Giant pandas eat almost nothing else — about 99 percent of the diet is bamboo. It is poor food for an animal built like a bear, which is why a panda spends most of its waking day chewing."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Running bamboos spread underground on thick stems called rhizomes and come up wherever those reach: through a lawn, under a fence, yards from the original clump. Gardeners sink metal barriers to contain them, and the bamboo usually finds the edge."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the plants on day three, each after its kind. Bamboo is a grass behaving like a tree — a lawn's relative standing a hundred and fifty feet up — and all of that was folded into a seed no bigger than a grain of rice."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Mautam runs on a clock of roughly 48 years. Muli bamboo across Mizoram, in north-east India, flowered and seeded in 1861, in 1911, in 1959 and again in 2007. Each time the rats followed, then the hunger. The 2007 flowering was expected well in advance, and grain was stored ahead of it.","more":"Mizoram lost more than half its crops in 2006 to 2008. The 1959 mautam did worse. The relief that arrived was thin, and the anger at it hardened into the Mizo National Famine Front and then into an armed movement. A grass coming into flower changed the politics of a whole state."}]},{"id":"strangler-fig","name":"Strangler fig","group":"odd","blurb":"A fig that starts life as a seed in another tree's branches, sends roots down to the ground, and ends up standing where its host used to be.","size":"The Curtain Fig in Queensland stands 160 ft (50 m) tall with a trunk 128 ft (39 m) around.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{"height":160},"wonder":"It begins as a speck in a bird dropping, forty feet up, and finishes as the largest thing in the forest.","facts":[{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A strangler fig does not start in the ground. A bird eats a fig, the seed passes through it, and it lands in a fork or a crack high up another tree. It sprouts there, in a handful of trapped leaf mould, with no soil underneath it at all."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"From that perch it drops roots straight down the host's trunk, thin as string at first. They take years to reach the ground. The moment they do, the fig has soil and water of its own and stops being a passenger.","more":"Until those roots land, the fig is living on rainwater and whatever leaf mould has collected in the fork. Most seedlings that start up there dry out and die. Almost none of them make it."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The roots thicken, fuse where they cross, and knit into a cage around the host's trunk. The host loses light above and gets squeezed below, and eventually it dies and rots. Nobody has watched one whole case through — the hollow trunks left standing are the argument."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"What is left is a fig-shaped tree with nothing in the middle. Walk up to an old one and you can often step inside the trunk and look straight up through a chimney of woven roots. The gap is the shape of the tree that used to be there."},{"cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"The Curtain Fig near Yungaburra in Queensland formed when its host tree toppled at an angle into a neighbour. Roots dropped from the leaning trunk 49 ft (15 m) to the forest floor in a sheet. The tree is 160 ft (50 m) tall and measures 128 ft (39 m) around."},{"cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Every fig species has its own pollinating wasp, and neither can manage without the other. There are more than 600 kinds of fig, each with a wasp that works that one species and no other.","more":"The wasps are around 2 mm long. The whole arrangement runs on an insect the size of a comma finding, entering and pollinating a fruit that has sealed itself against everything else."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A fig is not a fruit in the ordinary sense. It is a bag of flowers turned inward, with hundreds of tiny blooms facing into a hollow middle. No wind or bee can reach them. The only way in is a hole at the tip about the width of a pinhead."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"The female wasp forces her way through that hole. It is so tight that her wings and most of her antennae are torn off going in, and she cannot get out again. She lays her eggs, spreads the pollen she carried from the fig she was born in, and dies inside."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Her sons hatch first, wingless and nearly blind. They mate with their sisters and then chew a tunnel out through the wall of the fig. The females leave through that tunnel carrying pollen. The males never leave at all.","more":"The males are built for one job and no more: no wings, barely any eyes, and jaws for digging. They hatch, mate, dig the exit, and die inside a fruit they never see the outside of."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Fig trees feed a great many animals — monkeys, hornbills, fruit bats, pigeons. Those animals pay for it by dropping seeds in high branches, which is the only place a strangler can start. The reasoning goes in a circle, and the circle is what keeps both ends going."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Stranglers are all figs, genus Ficus. The Florida strangler grows in the Everglades and across the Caribbean, the Moreton Bay fig in eastern Australia, and the banyan of India runs the same trick on a much larger scale."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the trees on day three and the birds on day five, and this tree needs both — a fig only a bird can plant, and a bird that has to be fed. Neither half is worth much on its own."}]},{"id":"corpse-lily","name":"Ghost plant","group":"odd","blurb":"A flowering plant with no green in it anywhere, which has given up sunlight and takes its food from a fungus instead.","size":"2 to 12 in (5–30 cm) tall, carrying one nodding flower under an inch (10–20 mm) long.","homes":["forest"],"stats":{"height":1},"wonder":"It gave up the one thing that makes a plant a plant, and it is still here, standing white in the dark under the trees.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is white. Not pale green — white, waxy and slightly see-through, stem, scales, flower and all. There is no chlorophyll in it anywhere, which means it cannot make a single crumb of food out of sunlight.","more":"Chlorophyll is the green chemical that catches sunlight. A plant without it cannot photosynthesise, which is normally the definition of a plant's whole way of life. This one simply does without."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It looks like a fungus and it is not one. It has proper flowers, it sets seed, and it belongs to the heath family alongside blueberries and rhododendrons. It is a flowering plant that has stopped doing the part of the job plants are famous for."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"So where does the food come from? Traced through the roots, the chain runs: tree makes sugar in its leaves, passes some to a fungus wrapped round its roots, and the ghost plant — tangled into that same fungus — takes its cut on the way past.","more":"The word for it is mycoheterotrophy: feeding through a fungus. There are several hundred plants that do some version of it, and every one of them has given up some or all of its green."},{"cat":"food","kind":"worked","text":"Its fungal hosts are in the Russula family. In most root-and-fungus partnerships both sides gain something. Here, as far as anyone can measure, only one side does. The plain word for it is theft, and the ghost plant is very good at it."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Because it needs no light, it grows where almost nothing else will bother: deep gloom under a closed canopy, in thick leaf litter, often with no other green plant anywhere in sight. Dark is not a problem it has to solve."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Pick one and it is ruined. Within hours it goes glassy, then brown, then black, and dries to something like a spent match. Old plants left alone do the same thing standing up."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The single flower hangs straight down when it opens, like the bowl of a pipe — which is where the old name Indian pipe comes from. As the seed ripens the whole flower swings upright and points at the sky.","more":"The turn is not decoration. A hanging flower keeps rain off the pollen; an upright one lets the ripe capsule shake its dust seeds out into moving air. The same stem does both jobs in order."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"The seeds are 0.6 to 0.8 mm long. That is dust: a scattering of them on a fingertip looks like nothing at all.","more":"A seed that small carries almost no food inside it for the seedling. The reasonable conclusion is that it has to meet the right fungus almost at once or it is finished, which would explain why the plant makes so many."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The whole plant stands 2 to 12 in (5–30 cm) high and usually comes up in small clusters, several stems from one matted clump of root below. That root clump is stiff and brittle and packed through with fungal threads."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows in North America, in Latin America and in East Asia, but not in the wide stretches between. Nowhere is it common, and a patch that appears one year may not appear the next."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Carl Linnaeus gave it the name Monotropa uniflora, which means roughly once-turned, one-flowered. Both halves are just descriptions of what is in front of you: a single flower, on a single stem, bent over in a single curve."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the plants on day three, before the sun arrives on day four. Here is a plant that never needed the sun in the first place — white as bone, living in the dark, still opening a flower and setting seed after its kind."}]},{"id":"touch-me-not","name":"Himalayan balsam","group":"odd","blurb":"A tall pink-flowered plant from the Himalayas whose ripe seed pods explode at a touch and throw the seed clear across a riverbank.","size":"3 to 7 ft (1–2 m) tall, and every inch of that is built from seed in a single season.","homes":["freshwater","grassland"],"stats":{"height":6},"wonder":"One season from seed to seed, and it spends the end of that season firing the next one twenty feet down the bank.","facts":[{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"A ripe pod is loaded. Touch it — a finger, a raindrop, a passing sheep — and it splits along its seams, curls up instantly and flings the seeds as far as 23 ft (7 m). Held in a closed hand, you can feel it go off.","more":"The mechanism is stored tension. As the pod ripens, the five outer strips build up strain against each other until the seams can barely hold. Anything at all then acts as the trigger."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The pods are 3/4 to 1.25 in (2–3 cm) long and about a quarter inch (8 mm) across: a small green club that fattens as the seed fills, then turns tight and springy in the days before it fires."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Seeds that land in the river do not drown. They float downstream, wash up on a bank somewhere else and grow there. That is the best explanation for why the plant is nearly always found in a long line beside water.","more":"It also explains why clearing one stretch achieves nothing if there is more balsam upstream. River groups work from the top of a catchment downwards, or they do the same stretch again every summer for ever."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"It was brought to Britain in 1839 by John Forbes Royle, a professor of medicine at King's College London, as a garden ornament. Within ten years it was out of the gardens and spreading along English river systems by itself."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It belongs to the Himalayas, the country between Kashmir and Uttarakhand, at 6,500 to 8,200 ft (2,000–2,500 m). It is built for cool, wet mountain ground, which is a fair description of a British riverbank."},{"cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"In a British survey of nectar, its flowers came top for the amount each single flower produces in a day. Bumblebees work it hard and come out of the hood dusted pale with pollen.","more":"Beekeepers notice it. Late in the summer, hives near a big stand of balsam bring in a pale honey, and the bees themselves come home looking dusted with flour."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"worked","text":"Nectar like that pulls pollinators away from the native flowers growing nearby, and the balsam grows fast enough to shade those flowers out as well. Put together, that is the argument for treating it as a weed rather than a wildflower."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"The flower is shaped enough like a policeman's helmet that it is one of the plant's names. Others are kiss-me-on-the-mountain, ornamental jewelweed, and plain touch-me-not, which is the pods talking rather than the flowers."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"It is an annual, so every plant dies back in autumn and the bank it was holding is left bare all winter with nothing rooted in it. The soil washes into the river over the wet months. A native bank keeps its roots and keeps its dirt."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The stem is hollow, ridged and reddish, as thick as a thumb at the base, and it snaps clean with a wet crunch. Pull one up and the root ball is small and shallow, which is why volunteers with no tools can clear a whole stretch by hand.","more":"That shallow root is the one weakness the plant has. Because it is an annual with no runners underground, a stretch pulled up before the pods ripen does not come back from the roots — only from seed."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"All of it — up to 7 ft (2 m) of stem, leaf and flower — is built in one season from a single small seed, and then thrown away. Nothing overwinters. Next summer's stand starts again from scratch."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the seed-bearing plants on day three, each yielding seed after its kind. Most plants let theirs drop. This one throws them 23 ft (7 m), which is still yielding seed after its kind — just with more enthusiasm."}]},{"id":"telegraph-plant","name":"Telegraph plant","group":"odd","blurb":"A tropical shrub with two small leaflets on every leaf that swing round in circles all day, fast enough to watch.","size":"A leggy shrub. Each leaf carries one large blade and two small leaflets at its base — and only the small ones move.","homes":["rainforest","forest"],"stats":{},"wonder":"Nothing else in a garden moves while you stand and look at it. This one does, all day, for a reason nobody has settled.","facts":[{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Each large leaf has two small leaflets at its base, and those leaflets rotate — round and round, in jerky steps, along an elliptical path. One full circuit takes about three to five minutes, and it is fast enough to follow with the naked eye.","more":"Plant movements are usually too slow to see, or too fast to see — a Venus flytrap shuts in a fraction of a second. This one sits in the narrow band where a person can simply stand and watch it happen."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The movement happens at a hinge — a swollen joint called a pulvinus at the base of each leaflet. It works by shifting water from one side of the joint to the other, stiffening one side while the other slackens. There is no muscle anywhere in it."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"It only moves in daylight, and only when the air is above 72°F (22°C). Cold stops it. Night stops it. Nothing you do to the plant restarts it until both of those conditions come back."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Darwin wrote it up in detail in The Power of Movement in Plants, published in 1880. He spent years on the question of how plants move, and this shrub had the rare courtesy of moving while he was still looking at it.","more":"Darwin's book was one of the last he wrote, and much of the work was done with his son Francis. They tracked plant movements by marking positions on glass over hours, which is how the elliptical path was mapped in the first place."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"One idea is that the small leaflets are light meters. Each sweeps a circle, sampling brightness at every point along the way, and the big leaf is then aimed at the brightest part. Nobody has proved it — it is a reading of what the sweep would be useful for."},{"cat":"disguise","kind":"worked","text":"Another idea is that flicking leaflets look like a butterfly already sitting on the plant, so a butterfly hunting for somewhere to lay eggs goes elsewhere. That one is harder to test and easier to doubt, but it is still on the table."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Telegraph plant is the older name: the jerky steps looked like the swinging arm of a semaphore telegraph sending a message. Dancing plant and semaphore plant are the same shrub under different names."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"It is often said the two leaflets swing together like the blades of a kayak paddle. They do not. There is nothing rigid joining them, and watched closely each one goes round on its own."},{"cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"It gets sold in some places as a plant that dances to music. What has actually been measured is light and warmth — above 72°F, in daylight, it moves, with or without a sound in the room.","more":"It is a fair reminder to check the claim rather than the story. Sound is easy to test for: run the plant in silence at the right temperature and it dances anyway, which settles it."},{"cat":"speed","kind":"worked","text":"Movement costs a plant energy, and this one spends it all day, every warm day, on two leaflets that catch hardly any light themselves. Whatever they are for, it must be worth the bill — which is the main reason people keep hunting for the answer."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It grows wild across tropical Asia — Bangladesh, India, Thailand, Vietnam, Indonesia. It is a pea-family plant, a relative of beans and clover, and its small purple flowers turn orange as they age."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the plants on day three, before there was a sun on day four for them to answer. This one answers light with a movement you can stand and watch — and what it is answering for, nobody has yet worked out."}]},{"id":"squirting-cucumber","name":"Squirting cucumber","group":"odd","blurb":"A Mediterranean weed with a fruit that builds up pressure for days, blows itself off its own stalk, and fires its seeds out in a jet.","size":"The fruit is about 2 in (5 cm) long, blue-green and bristly — a small hairy cucumber.","homes":["grassland"],"stats":{},"wonder":"It has no muscles and nothing to push with, so it spends days quietly building pressure and then lets go of all of it at once.","facts":[{"cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"When the fruit is ripe it snaps off its own stalk, and the hole that leaves becomes a nozzle. The fruit contracts and fires a jet of slime and seed straight out of the hole, as far as 23 to 26 ft (7–8 m).","more":"The fruit hangs down at an angle, so the jet goes out and up rather than straight at the ground. The angle is not adjustable — whatever the fruit was pointing at is where the seed goes."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The pressure inside a ripe fruit has been measured at up to 27 atmospheres — around 400 pounds per square inch, more than ten times the pressure in a car tyre. It builds up over days and is spent in a fraction of a second.","more":"Twenty-seven atmospheres is around the pressure a hundred metres down in the sea. A plant holds it inside a green skin about as thick as a fingernail, without any moving parts at all."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The pressure comes from the fruit's own sap. As it ripens, the large thin-walled cells load up with a dissolved compound, water follows the compound in, and the cells swell against a skin that will not stretch any further. Something has to give, and the stalk is the weakest point."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"A seed that drops straight down has to compete with its own parent for light and water. A seed thrown 25 ft lands in ground the parent is not using. That is the read on why a plant would spend so much of a season's energy building a launcher."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"The juice is poisonous. It carries cucurbitacins, the compounds that make a bad cucumber taste bitter, and here they are concentrated enough to blister skin and burn the lining of the nose. The jet in an eye is not something to shrug off.","more":"It is a member of the cucumber and melon family, and the bitterness is the family trait. Ordinary cucumbers were bred to have very little of it; this one kept all of it and then some."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The dried juice, called elaterium, was once a standard medicine for clearing out the gut — one of the strongest anybody had. It is not used now: the dose that works and the dose that harms sit far too close together."},{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The fruit is about 2 in (5 cm) long, oval, blue-green and covered in stiff hairs — plainly a cucumber, in miniature and in a bad mood. The plant itself is a sprawling vine running flat over dry stony ground."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It is native to southern Europe, north-west Africa and south-west Asia — roadsides, waste ground and dry banks around the Mediterranean. It has since turned up in Australia, New Zealand, central Asia and eastern North America."},{"cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"It does not need to be touched. A ripe fruit goes off on its own the moment the pressure wins, and brushing past one only brings the moment forward. Walk through a patch in late summer and they let go around you."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"What comes out is not dry seed. It is a stream of mucus with the seeds carried along inside it, so the seeds travel in the jet and land wet and sticky. The slime is the vehicle, not a side effect."},{"cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The fruit is 2 in (5 cm) long and the jet carries about 25 ft (8 m). That is roughly 150 times the length of the thing doing the throwing, from a plant that sprawls at ankle height."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the seed-bearing plants on day three, each yielding seed after its kind. This one yields it under pressure — the same instruction carried out with a pump, a nozzle and a good deal of noise."}]},{"id":"tumbleweed","name":"Tumbleweed","group":"odd","blurb":"A plant that dies on purpose, snaps itself off at the ground, and rolls across the desert scattering seed the whole way.","size":"A big one is a tangled ball more than 3 ft (1 m) across, and light enough for a breeze to move.","homes":["desert","grassland"],"stats":{},"wonder":"It finishes growing, kills its own stem at one exact point, and hands the rest of the job to the wind.","facts":[{"cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The tumbleweed rolling past is a dead plant, and that is the design working. Once the seed is ripe the stem gives way at a single spot just above the root. The root stays in the ground and the whole round bush leaves without it."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The break is not damage. There is a weak line built into the stem in advance, the same way a leaf carries a built-in line where it lets go in autumn. The neatness of the break, in the same place every time, is the argument that it was prepared.","more":"The layer is called an abscission zone. Leaves use one every autumn. Using the same trick on a whole plant, to cut the entire body loose from its own root, is a considerably bolder application."},{"cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"One large Russian thistle carries about 200,000 seeds. It does not drop them all at once — the ball shakes a few loose with every bounce, so a single plant seeds a long line of ground instead of one spot.","more":"Holding the seed back matters. If it all fell at once the plant would seed one patch and stop. Shaking a few out per bounce spreads the seed over every mile the wind carries the ball."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Nothing steers it. It goes where the wind goes until a fence stops it, and then the next one stops behind that, and the next. In Richland, Washington, in 2019, tumbleweeds piled 15 to 20 ft (4.5–6 m) deep and shut a road with cars still on it."},{"cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"At Mobridge in South Dakota in 1989, tens of tons of large tumbleweed piled against houses and buried them so deeply that machinery had to be brought in to dig them out."},{"cat":"home","kind":"record","text":"Russian thistle is not American. It arrived in South Dakota in the 1870s in a shipment of flax seed from Russia that had tumbleweed seed mixed into it, and spread west from there. The plant in every cowboy film is an immigrant.","more":"It spread along the railways as much as by wind — seed in grain trucks and stuck to freight moved it faster than any breeze could. Within a few decades it was across most of the dry west."},{"cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Young plants are soft and succulent, with leaves over 2 in (5 cm) long, and livestock will eat them. As the plant matures those leaves shrink to under half an inch and go rigid and spine-tipped. By the time it rolls, it is a ball of thorns."},{"cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"A dry tumbleweed is kindling with air in it. A pile of them against a building is a serious fire risk, and a burning one does not stay put — it keeps rolling, which is considerably worse than a fire that stays where it started."},{"cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"The shape is the machine. It starts upright, then branches over and over into a tangled ball wider than it is tall. Built like that, there is no way for it to land that stops it rolling — which is the point of building like that."},{"cat":"travel","kind":"worked","text":"Dying first is part of it. Living tissue is heavy with water; dead tissue is light and brittle. The plant dries out before it lets go, and that drying is what leaves a bush three feet across light enough for an ordinary breeze to shift.","more":"That is an unusual order of operations. Nearly every other plant does its dispersing while alive and green. This one finishes its life first and uses its own corpse as the vehicle."},{"cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"It does best where nothing else wants to be: dry ground, salt flats, roadsides, ploughed fields, railway ballast. Bare disturbed soil is its favourite thing in the world, which is why it tends to follow people around."},{"cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the plants on day three, each yielding seed after its kind. Most of them let go of the seed. This one lets go of itself, and posts the entire plant across the country with the seed still on board."}]}];
globalThis.PLANTS = PLANTS;
